{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <climits>\n\nint max_sum_subarray(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint max_val = INT_MIN;\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i <= nums.size() - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint current_sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i; j < i + k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[j];\n\t\t}\n\t\tif (current_sum > max_val) max_val = current_sum;\n\t}\n\treturn max_val;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nint max_sum_subarray(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tif (nums.size() < (size_t)k) return 0;\n\tint current_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) current_sum += nums[i];\n\tint max_val = current_sum;\n\tfor (size_t i = k; i < nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[i] - nums[i - k];\n\t\tmax_val = std::max(max_val, current_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn max_val;\n}\n---\nThe original nested loop approach re-sums  elements for every index, resulting in  complexity. Using a fixed-size sliding window updates the sum in  by adding the next element and subtracting the one leaving the window, reducing total time to ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longest_unique_substring(std::string s) {\n\tint max_len = 0;\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < s.length(); ++i) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i; j < s.length(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tstd::string sub = s.substr(i, j - i + 1);\n\t\t\tstd::vector<bool> seen(256, false);\n\t\t\tbool dup = false;\n\t\t\tfor (char c : sub) {\n\t\t\t\tif (seen[(unsigned char)c]) { dup = true; break; }\n\t\t\t\tseen[(unsigned char)c] = true;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif (!dup) max_len = std::max(max_len, (int)sub.length());\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_len;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longest_unique_substring(std::string s) {\n\tstd::vector<int> last_seen(256, -1);\n\tint max_len = 0, start = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < (int)s.length(); ++end) {\n\t\tif (last_seen[(unsigned char)s[end]] >= start) {\n\t\t\tstart = last_seen[(unsigned char)s[end]] + 1;\n\t\t}\n\t\tlast_seen[(unsigned char)s[end]] = end;\n\t\tmax_len = std::max(max_len, end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn max_len;\n}\n---\nThe brute-force approach checks all  substrings, each taking , leading to  complexity. A variable sliding window with an index-tracking array allows finding the longest unique substring in  time by moving the start pointer only when a duplicate is encountered."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nint count_subarrays_sum_target(const std::vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n\tint count = 0;\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i; j < nums.size(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tint current_sum = 0;\n\t\t\tfor (size_t k = i; k <= j; ++k) {\n\t\t\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[k];\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif (current_sum <= target) count++;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nint count_subarrays_sum_target(const std::vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n\tint count = 0, current_sum = 0, start = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < (int)nums.size(); ++end) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[end];\n\t\twhile (current_sum > target && start <= end) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum -= nums[start++];\n\t\t}\n\t\tcount += (end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nThe original triple-nested loop results in  time complexity. Assuming non-negative integers, a sliding window maintains a running sum and adjusts the start pointer in  total time, using the window size to count all valid subarrays ending at the current index."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nstd::vector<int> max_sliding_window(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tstd::vector<int> result;\n\tif (nums.empty()) return result;\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i <= nums.size() - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint max_val = INT_MIN;\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i; j < i + k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tmax_val = std::max(max_val, nums[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t\tresult.push_back(max_val);\n\t}\n\treturn result;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <deque>\n\nstd::vector<int> max_sliding_window(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tstd::vector<int> result;\n\tstd::deque<int> dq;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < (int)nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tif (!dq.empty() && dq.front() == i - k) dq.pop_front();\n\t\twhile (!dq.empty() && nums[dq.back()] < nums[i]) dq.pop_back();\n\t\tdq.push_back(i);\n\t\tif (i >= k - 1) result.push_back(nums[dq.front()]);\n\t}\n\treturn result;\n}\n---\nScanning each window of size  manually leads to  performance. A monotonic deque preserves indices of elements in decreasing order of value, allowing the maximum for each window to be retrieved from the front in , resulting in  total time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint smallest_subarray_with_sum(const std::vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint min_len = n + 1;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tint current_sum = 0;\n\t\t\tfor (int k = i; k <= j; ++k) current_sum += nums[k];\n\t\t\tif (current_sum >= target) min_len = std::min(min_len, j - i + 1);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (min_len > n) ? 0 : min_len;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint smallest_subarray_with_sum(const std::vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint min_len = n + 1;\n\tint current_sum = 0, start = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[end];\n\t\twhile (current_sum >= target) {\n\t\t\tmin_len = std::min(min_len, end - start + 1);\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum -= nums[start++];\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (min_len > n) ? 0 : min_len;\n}\n---\nThe brute-force  approach recomputes sums for every possible subarray. A variable-size sliding window shrinks the range from the left as soon as the target sum is met, ensuring each element is visited at most twice for  complexity."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint min_sum_subarray(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint min_val = 2147483647;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; ++j) sum += nums[j];\n\t\tif (sum < min_val) min_val = sum;\n\t}\n\treturn min_val;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <numeric>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint min_sum_subarray(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tif (nums.size() < (size_t)k) return 0;\n\tint current_sum = std::accumulate(nums.begin(), nums.begin() + k, 0);\n\tint min_val = current_sum;\n\tfor (size_t i = k; i < nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[i] - nums[i - k];\n\t\tmin_val = std::min(min_val, current_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn min_val;\n}\n---\nThe original code re-sums the entire window for every shift, leading to  complexity. A fixed sliding window updates the sum by adding the entering element and subtracting the exiting one, achieving  linear time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <unordered_map>\n\nint max_fruits_two_types(const std::string& fruits) {\n\tint max_f = 0;\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < fruits.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i; j < fruits.size(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tstd::unordered_map<char, int> counts;\n\t\t\tfor (size_t k = i; k <= j; ++k) counts[fruits[k]]++;\n\t\t\tif (counts.size() <= 2) {\n\t\t\t\tint len = j - i + 1;\n\t\t\t\tif (len > max_f) max_f = len;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_f;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <unordered_map>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_fruits_two_types(const std::string& fruits) {\n\tint max_f = 0, start = 0;\n\tstd::unordered_map<char, int> counts;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < (int)fruits.size(); ++end) {\n\t\tcounts[fruits[end]]++;\n\t\twhile (counts.size() > 2) {\n\t\t\tcounts[fruits[start]]--;\n\t\t\tif (counts[fruits[start]] == 0) counts.erase(fruits[start]);\n\t\t\tstart++;\n\t\t}\n\t\tmax_f = std::max(max_f, end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn max_f;\n}\n---\nChecking all  substrings and building maps leads to  complexity. Using a sliding window with a frequency map allows for tracking the number of distinct elements in a single pass, yielding  time complexity."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nint count_subarrays_with_product_less_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tif (k <= 1) return 0;\n\tint count = 0;\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tlong long prod = 1;\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i; j < nums.size(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tprod *= nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (prod < k) count++;\n\t\t\telse break;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nint count_subarrays_with_product_less_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tif (k <= 1) return 0;\n\tint count = 0, start = 0;\n\tlong long prod = 1;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < (int)nums.size(); ++end) {\n\t\tprod *= nums[end];\n\t\twhile (prod >= k && start <= end) {\n\t\t\tprod /= nums[start++];\n\t\t}\n\t\tcount += (end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nThe original approach has a worst-case  complexity by expanding from every index. A sliding window maintains the product and shrinks the range from the left in  time, counting all valid subarrays ending at 'end' in each step."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <numeric>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::vector<double> average_of_windows(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tstd::vector<double> result;\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i <= nums.size() - k; ++i) {\n\t\tdouble sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i; j < i + k; ++j) sum += nums[j];\n\t\tresult.push_back(sum / k);\n\t}\n\treturn result;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nstd::vector<double> average_of_windows(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tif (nums.size() < (size_t)k) return {};\n\tstd::vector<double> result;\n\tdouble window_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) window_sum += nums[i];\n\tresult.push_back(window_sum / k);\n\tfor (size_t i = k; i < nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\twindow_sum += nums[i] - nums[i - k];\n\t\tresult.push_back(window_sum / k);\n\t}\n\treturn result;\n}\n---\nRecalculating the sum for every window position results in  operations. By using a sliding window to add one element and subtract one element per step, the complexity is reduced to  time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint k_distinct_substring(std::string s, int k) {\n\tint max_len = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < (int)s.length(); ++i) {\n\t\tstd::vector<int> freq(256, 0);\n\t\tint distinct = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < (int)s.length(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (freq[(unsigned char)s[j]] == 0) distinct++;\n\t\t\tfreq[(unsigned char)s[j]]++;\n\t\t\tif (distinct <= k) max_len = std::max(max_len, j - i + 1);\n\t\t\telse break;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_len;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n\nint k_distinct_substring(std::string s, int k) {\n\tif (k == 0) return 0;\n\tstd::vector<int> freq(256, 0);\n\tint max_len = 0, start = 0, distinct = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < (int)s.length(); ++end) {\n\t\tif (freq[(unsigned char)s[end]] == 0) distinct++;\n\t\tfreq[(unsigned char)s[end]]++;\n\t\twhile (distinct > k) {\n\t\t\tfreq[(unsigned char)s[start]]--;\n\t\t\tif (freq[(unsigned char)s[start]] == 0) distinct--;\n\t\t\tstart++;\n\t\t}\n\t\tif (end - start + 1 > max_len) max_len = end - start + 1;\n\t}\n\treturn max_len;\n}\n---\nThe brute force approach checks all substrings in  time. A sliding window with a frequency array tracks the number of distinct characters, shifting the window boundaries in a single  pass."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_ones_after_k_flips(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint max_len = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < (int)nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tint zeros = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < (int)nums.size(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (nums[j] == 0) zeros++;\n\t\t\tif (zeros <= k) max_len = std::max(max_len, j - i + 1);\n\t\t\telse break;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_len;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_ones_after_k_flips(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint max_len = 0, start = 0, zeros = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < (int)nums.size(); ++end) {\n\t\tif (nums[end] == 0) zeros++;\n\t\twhile (zeros > k) {\n\t\t\tif (nums[start] == 0) zeros--;\n\t\t\tstart++;\n\t\t}\n\t\tmax_len = std::max(max_len, end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn max_len;\n}\n---\nThe original  solution redundantly checks every possible range. A sliding window approach tracks the zero count and shrinks from the left only when the budget  is exceeded, ensuring  time complexity."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::vector<int> min_sliding_window(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tstd::vector<int> result;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= (int)nums.size() - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint min_val = nums[i];\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < i + k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (nums[j] < min_val) min_val = nums[j];\n\t\t}\n\t\tresult.push_back(min_val);\n\t}\n\treturn result;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <deque>\n\nstd::vector<int> min_sliding_window(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tstd::vector<int> result;\n\tstd::deque<int> dq;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < (int)nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tif (!dq.empty() && dq.front() == i - k) dq.pop_front();\n\t\twhile (!dq.empty() && nums[dq.back()] > nums[i]) dq.pop_back();\n\t\tdq.push_back(i);\n\t\tif (i >= k - 1) result.push_back(nums[dq.front()]);\n\t}\n\treturn result;\n}\n---\nIterating through each window to find the minimum results in  complexity. Using a monotonic deque to maintain indices of elements in increasing order allows the window minimum to be found at the front in , totaling  time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <numeric>\n\nint subarray_sum_equals_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint count = 0;\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tint sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i; j < nums.size(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tsum += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (sum == k) count++;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <unordered_map>\n\nint subarray_sum_equals_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tstd::unordered_map<int, int> prefix_sums;\n\tprefix_sums[0] = 1;\n\tint current_sum = 0, count = 0;\n\tfor (int x : nums) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += x;\n\t\tif (prefix_sums.count(current_sum - k)) {\n\t\t\tcount += prefix_sums[current_sum - k];\n\t\t}\n\t\tprefix_sums[current_sum]++;\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nThe nested loop approach takes  time to check all subarrays. Using a hash map to store frequencies of prefix sums allows us to find subarrays with sum  in  average time by checking for (current_sum - k) in the map."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n\nbool contains_permutation(std::string s1, std::string s2) {\n\tint n = s1.length(), m = s2.length();\n\tif (n > m) return false;\n\tstd::vector<int> target(26, 0);\n\tfor (char c : s1) target[c - 'a']++;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= m - n; ++i) {\n\t\tstd::vector<int> window(26, 0);\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) window[s2[i + j] - 'a']++;\n\t\tif (window == target) return true;\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n\nbool contains_permutation(std::string s1, std::string s2) {\n\tint n = s1.length(), m = s2.length();\n\tif (n > m) return false;\n\tstd::vector<int> target(26, 0), window(26, 0);\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\ttarget[s1[i] - 'a']++;\n\t\twindow[s2[i] - 'a']++;\n\t}\n\tif (target == window) return true;\n\tfor (int i = n; i < m; ++i) {\n\t\twindow[s2[i] - 'a']++;\n\t\twindow[s2[i - n] - 'a']--;\n\t\tif (target == window) return true;\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}\n---\nRecalculating the frequency map for every window in  takes  time. A fixed-size sliding window updates the character counts in  by adding the incoming char and removing the outgoing one, reducing the time to ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_vowels_in_k_substring(std::string s, int k) {\n\tauto is_vowel = [](char c) {\n\t\treturn c == 'a' || c == 'e' || c == 'i' || c == 'o' || c == 'u';\n\t};\n\tint max_v = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= (int)s.length() - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint current_v = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (is_vowel(s[j])) current_v++;\n\t\t}\n\t\tmax_v = std::max(max_v, current_v);\n\t}\n\treturn max_v;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_vowels_in_k_substring(std::string s, int k) {\n\tauto is_v = [](char c) {\n\t\treturn c == 'a' || c == 'e' || c == 'i' || c == 'o' || c == 'u';\n\t};\n\tint current_v = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) if (is_v(s[i])) current_v++;\n\tint max_v = current_v;\n\tfor (size_t i = k; i < s.length(); ++i) {\n\t\tif (is_v(s[i])) current_v++;\n\t\tif (is_v(s[i - k])) current_v--;\n\t\tmax_v = std::max(max_v, current_v);\n\t}\n\treturn max_v;\n}\n---\nThe brute force method re-scans each substring of size , resulting in  complexity. A fixed sliding window updates the vowel count by checking only the entering and exiting characters, achieving  linear time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longest_subarray_abs_diff(const std::vector<int>& nums, int limit) {\n\tint max_len = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < (int)nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < (int)nums.size(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tint min_v = nums[i], max_v = nums[i];\n\t\t\tfor (int k = i; k <= j; ++k) {\n\t\t\t\tmin_v = std::min(min_v, nums[k]);\n\t\t\t\tmax_v = std::max(max_v, nums[k]);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif (max_v - min_v <= limit) max_len = std::max(max_len, j - i + 1);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_len;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <deque>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longest_subarray_abs_diff(const std::vector<int>& nums, int limit) {\n\tstd::deque<int> max_dq, min_dq;\n\tint start = 0, max_len = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < (int)nums.size(); ++end) {\n\t\twhile (!max_dq.empty() && nums[max_dq.back()] <= nums[end]) max_dq.pop_back();\n\t\twhile (!min_dq.empty() && nums[min_dq.back()] >= nums[end]) min_dq.pop_back();\n\t\tmax_dq.push_back(end);\n\t\tmin_dq.push_back(end);\n\t\twhile (nums[max_dq.front()] - nums[min_dq.front()] > limit) {\n\t\t\tstart++;\n\t\t\tif (max_dq.front() < start) max_dq.pop_front();\n\t\t\tif (min_dq.front() < start) min_dq.pop_front();\n\t\t}\n\t\tmax_len = std::max(max_len, end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn max_len;\n}\n---\nThe original solution is  as it iterates through all possible subarrays and their contents. Using two monotonic deques to track the minimum and maximum in the current sliding window allows for adjusting window size in  time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <numeric>\n\nint find_max_consecutive_sum(const std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tint max_s = -2147483648;\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i; j < nums.size(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tint current = 0;\n\t\t\tfor (size_t k = i; k <= j; ++k) current += nums[k];\n\t\t\tif (current > max_s) max_s = current;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_s;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint find_max_consecutive_sum(const std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tif (nums.empty()) return 0;\n\tint max_so_far = nums[0], current_max = nums[0];\n\tfor (size_t i = 1; i < nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tcurrent_max = std::max(nums[i], current_max + nums[i]);\n\t\tmax_so_far = std::max(max_so_far, current_max);\n\t}\n\treturn max_so_far;\n}\n---\nThe brute force  approach re-sums all subarrays redundantly. Kadane's algorithm uses dynamic programming to track the maximum sum ending at the current position, solving the problem in a single  pass."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <set>\n\nint count_distinct_in_windows(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint total_distinct = 0;\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i <= nums.size() - k; ++i) {\n\t\tstd::set<int> unique_elements;\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i; j < i + k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tunique_elements.insert(nums[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t\ttotal_distinct += unique_elements.size();\n\t}\n\treturn total_distinct;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <unordered_map>\n\nint count_distinct_in_windows(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint total_distinct = 0;\n\tstd::unordered_map<int, int> freq;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) freq[nums[i]]++;\n\ttotal_distinct += freq.size();\n\tfor (size_t i = k; i < nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tif (--freq[nums[i - k]] == 0) freq.erase(nums[i - k]);\n\t\tfreq[nums[i]]++;\n\t\ttotal_distinct += freq.size();\n\t}\n\treturn total_distinct;\n}\n---\nCreating a set for every window takes  time. Using a sliding window with a frequency hash map allows us to update the count of distinct elements in  average time, resulting in  total complexity."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_sum_subarray_at_most_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint max_sum = -2147483648;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < (int)nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tint current = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < (int)nums.size(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tcurrent += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (j - i + 1 <= k) max_sum = std::max(max_sum, current);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_sum;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <deque>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_sum_subarray_at_most_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tstd::vector<long long> prefix(n + 1, 0);\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) prefix[i + 1] = prefix[i] + nums[i];\n\tstd::deque<int> dq;\n\tdq.push_back(0);\n\tlong long max_sum = -2147483648;\n\tfor (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) {\n\t\tif (!dq.empty() && dq.front() < i - k) dq.pop_front();\n\t\tmax_sum = std::max(max_sum, prefix[i] - prefix[dq.front()]);\n\t\twhile (!dq.empty() && prefix[dq.back()] >= prefix[i]) dq.pop_back();\n\t\tdq.push_back(i);\n\t}\n\treturn (int)max_sum;\n}\n---\nThe original nested loops result in  time. By using prefix sums and a monotonic deque to find the minimum prefix sum within the previous  elements, the maximum subarray sum can be found in  time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint min_window_substring(std::string s, std::string t) {\n\tint min_len = 1e9;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < (int)s.length(); ++i) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < (int)s.length(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tstd::string sub = s.substr(i, j - i + 1);\n\t\t\tstd::vector<int> freq_t(256, 0), freq_sub(256, 0);\n\t\t\tfor (char c : t) freq_t[(unsigned char)c]++;\n\t\t\tfor (char c : sub) freq_sub[(unsigned char)c]++;\n\t\t\tbool ok = true;\n\t\t\tfor (int k = 0; k < 256; ++k) {\n\t\t\t\tif (freq_sub[k] < freq_t[k]) { ok = false; break; }\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif (ok) min_len = std::min(min_len, (int)sub.length());\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (min_len == 1e9) ? 0 : min_len;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint min_window_substring(std::string s, std::string t) {\n\tstd::vector<int> target(256, 0), window(256, 0);\n\tfor (char c : t) target[(unsigned char)c]++;\n\tint required = 0;\n\tfor (int count : target) if (count > 0) required++;\n\tint formed = 0, start = 0, min_len = 1e9;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < (int)s.length(); ++end) {\n\t\tunsigned char c = s[end];\n\t\twindow[c]++;\n\t\tif (target[c] > 0 && window[c] == target[c]) formed++;\n\t\twhile (start <= end && formed == required) {\n\t\t\tmin_len = std::min(min_len, end - start + 1);\n\t\t\tunsigned char sc = s[start];\n\t\t\twindow[sc]--;\n\t\t\tif (target[sc] > 0 && window[sc] < target[sc]) formed--;\n\t\t\tstart++;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (min_len == 1e9) ? 0 : min_len;\n}\n---\nThe brute force complexity is  due to checking all substrings. A variable-size sliding window (two pointers) with character frequency counters identifies the minimal valid window in  time by shrinking as much as possible once the target is met."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longest_subarray_sum_at_most_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint max_len = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < (int)nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tint current_sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < (int)nums.size(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (current_sum <= k) max_len = std::max(max_len, j - i + 1);\n\t\t\telse break;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_len;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longest_subarray_sum_at_most_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint start = 0, current_sum = 0, max_len = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < (int)nums.size(); ++end) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[end];\n\t\twhile (current_sum > k && start <= end) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum -= nums[start++];\n\t\t}\n\t\tmax_len = std::max(max_len, end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn max_len;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach checks every starting point. For non-negative values, a sliding window can expand to the right and contract from the left in a single pass, resulting in  time complexity."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint find_max_avg_subarray(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint max_s = -2147483648;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= (int)nums.size() - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint current_sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; ++j) current_sum += nums[j];\n\t\tif (current_sum > max_s) max_s = current_sum;\n\t}\n\treturn max_s;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <numeric>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint find_max_avg_subarray(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tif (nums.size() < (size_t)k) return 0;\n\tint window_sum = std::accumulate(nums.begin(), nums.begin() + k, 0);\n\tint max_s = window_sum;\n\tfor (size_t i = k; i < nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\twindow_sum += nums[i] - nums[i - k];\n\t\tmax_s = std::max(max_s, window_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn max_s;\n}\n---\nThe  approach repeatedly sums the interior of overlapping windows. Using a fixed-size sliding window allows for updates in  constant time, reducing the total runtime to ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nint count_binary_subarrays_sum(const std::vector<int>& nums, int goal) {\n\tint count = 0;\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tint sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i; j < nums.size(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tsum += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (sum == goal) count++;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nint count_at_most(const std::vector<int>& nums, int goal) {\n\tif (goal < 0) return 0;\n\tint start = 0, sum = 0, count = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < (int)nums.size(); ++end) {\n\t\tsum += nums[end];\n\t\twhile (sum > goal) sum -= nums[start++];\n\t\tcount += (end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n\nint count_binary_subarrays_sum(const std::vector<int>& nums, int goal) {\n\treturn count_at_most(nums, goal) - count_at_most(nums, goal - 1);\n}\n---\nThe original  approach is slow for large binary arrays. By calculating the number of subarrays with sum at most  using a sliding window in , we can find the exact count for  in  time using the difference method."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_sum_distinct_subarray(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint max_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= (int)nums.size() - k; ++i) {\n\t\tstd::vector<int> window;\n\t\tint current_sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; ++j) {\n\t\t\twindow.push_back(nums[j]);\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[j];\n\t\t}\n\t\tstd::sort(window.begin(), window.end());\n\t\tif (std::unique(window.begin(), window.end()) == window.end()) {\n\t\t\tmax_sum = std::max(max_sum, current_sum);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_sum;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <unordered_map>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_sum_distinct_subarray(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tif (nums.size() < (size_t)k) return 0;\n\tstd::unordered_map<int, int> freq;\n\tlong long current_sum = 0, max_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[i];\n\t\tfreq[nums[i]]++;\n\t}\n\tif (freq.size() == (size_t)k) max_sum = current_sum;\n\tfor (size_t i = k; i < nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[i] - nums[i - k];\n\t\tfreq[nums[i]]++;\n\t\tif (--freq[nums[i - k]] == 0) freq.erase(nums[i - k]);\n\t\tif (freq.size() == (size_t)k) max_sum = std::max(max_sum, (long long)current_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn (int)max_sum;\n}\n---\nSorting each window to check for uniqueness results in  complexity. A sliding window with a frequency hash map tracks uniqueness and sum in  average time per step, yielding  total time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint shortest_subarray_with_at_least_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint min_len = n + 1;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tint sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tsum += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (sum >= k) min_len = std::min(min_len, j - i + 1);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (min_len > n) ? -1 : min_len;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <deque>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint shortest_subarray_with_at_least_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tstd::vector<long long> prefix(n + 1, 0);\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) prefix[i + 1] = prefix[i] + nums[i];\n\tstd::deque<int> dq;\n\tint min_len = n + 1;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n; ++i) {\n\t\twhile (!dq.empty() && prefix[i] - prefix[dq.front()] >= k) {\n\t\t\tmin_len = std::min(min_len, i - dq.front());\n\t\t\tdq.pop_front();\n\t\t}\n\t\twhile (!dq.empty() && prefix[i] <= prefix[dq.back()]) {\n\t\t\tdq.pop_back();\n\t\t}\n\t\tdq.push_back(i);\n\t}\n\treturn (min_len > n) ? -1 : min_len;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach is too slow for large inputs, especially with negative values. Using prefix sums and a monotonic deque allows us to maintain a candidate list of starting indices, enabling an  solution for the shortest subarray problem."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint count_subarrays_with_fixed_bounds(const std::vector<int>& nums, int min_k, int max_k) {\n\tint count = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < (int)nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < (int)nums.size(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tint cur_min = nums[i], cur_max = nums[i];\n\t\t\tfor (int k = i; k <= j; ++k) {\n\t\t\t\tcur_min = std::min(cur_min, nums[k]);\n\t\t\t\tcur_max = std::max(cur_max, nums[k]);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif (cur_min == min_k && cur_max == max_k) count++;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nlong long count_subarrays_with_fixed_bounds(const std::vector<int>& nums, int min_k, int max_k) {\n\tlong long count = 0;\n\tint min_idx = -1, max_idx = -1, bad_idx = -1;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < (int)nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tif (nums[i] < min_k || nums[i] > max_k) bad_idx = i;\n\t\tif (nums[i] == min_k) min_idx = i;\n\t\tif (nums[i] == max_k) max_idx = i;\n\t\tcount += std::max(0, std::min(min_idx, max_idx) - bad_idx);\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nThe  brute force approach is highly redundant. By tracking the most recent indices of the min value, max value, and any value outside the range , we can count all valid subarrays ending at the current index in , resulting in  total time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nint total_subarray_sum(const std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tint total = 0;\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i; j < nums.size(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tfor (size_t k = i; k <= j; ++k) {\n\t\t\t\ttotal += nums[k];\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn total;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nlong long total_subarray_sum(const std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tlong long total = 0;\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\ttotal += (long long)nums[i] * (i + 1) * (n - i);\n\t}\n\treturn total;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach is extremely inefficient. By calculating the contribution of each element to the total sum (an element at index  appears in  subarrays), the result can be computed in a single  pass."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n\nint max_freq_char_in_windows(std::string s, int k) {\n\tint max_count = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= (int)s.length() - k; ++i) {\n\t\tstd::vector<int> freq(26, 0);\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tfreq[s[j] - 'a']++;\n\t\t}\n\t\tfor (int f : freq) if (f > max_count) max_count = f;\n\t}\n\treturn max_count;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_freq_char_in_windows(std::string s, int k) {\n\tif (s.empty() || k == 0) return 0;\n\tstd::vector<int> freq(26, 0);\n\tint max_count = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) freq[s[i] - 'a']++;\n\tfor (int f : freq) max_count = std::max(max_count, f);\n\tfor (size_t i = k; i < s.length(); ++i) {\n\t\tfreq[s[i] - 'a']++;\n\t\tfreq[s[i - k] - 'a']--;\n\t\t// In a fixed window, we only need local max if requested per window,\n\t\t// but to find global max freq ever reached in any window:\n\t\tmax_count = std::max(max_count, freq[s[i] - 'a']);\n\t}\n\treturn max_count;\n}\n---\nRe-calculating the frequency array for every window shift takes  time. A sliding window updates character frequencies in  by incrementing/decrementing, allowing the maximum frequency to be tracked across all windows in  time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longest_subarray_with_sum_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint max_len = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < (int)nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tint sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < (int)nums.size(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tsum += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (sum == k) max_len = std::max(max_len, j - i + 1);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_len;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <unordered_map>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longest_subarray_with_sum_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tstd::unordered_map<long long, int> prefix_map;\n\tlong long current_sum = 0;\n\tint max_len = 0;\n\tprefix_map[0] = -1;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < (int)nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[i];\n\t\tif (prefix_map.count(current_sum - k)) {\n\t\t\tmax_len = std::max(max_len, i - prefix_map[current_sum - k]);\n\t\t}\n\t\tif (prefix_map.find(current_sum) == prefix_map.end()) {\n\t\t\tprefix_map[current_sum] = i;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_len;\n}\n---\nThe brute-force approach iterates all subarrays in  time. Using a hash map to store the earliest occurrence of each prefix sum allows us to find the longest subarray summing to  in  average time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint find_max_consecutive_ones(const std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tint max_c = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < (int)nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tif (nums[i] == 1) {\n\t\t\tint count = 0;\n\t\t\tfor (int j = i; j < (int)nums.size() && nums[j] == 1; ++j) {\n\t\t\t\tcount++;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tmax_c = std::max(max_c, count);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_c;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint find_max_consecutive_ones(const std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tint max_c = 0, current_c = 0;\n\tfor (int x : nums) {\n\t\tif (x == 1) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_c++;\n\t\t\tmax_c = std::max(max_c, current_c);\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tcurrent_c = 0;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_c;\n}\n---\nThe original  code restarts the count unnecessarily for nested loops. A single pass  solution tracks the current streak and resets it when a zero is encountered, providing optimal performance."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::vector<int> find_anagrams(std::string s, std::string p) {\n\tstd::vector<int> result;\n\tint n = s.length(), m = p.length();\n\tif (n < m) return result;\n\tstd::sort(p.begin(), p.end());\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - m; ++i) {\n\t\tstd::string sub = s.substr(i, m);\n\t\tstd::sort(sub.begin(), sub.end());\n\t\tif (sub == p) result.push_back(i);\n\t}\n\treturn result;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n\nstd::vector<int> find_anagrams(std::string s, std::string p) {\n\tstd::vector<int> result;\n\tint n = s.length(), m = p.length();\n\tif (n < m) return result;\n\tstd::vector<int> p_freq(26, 0), s_freq(26, 0);\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < m; ++i) {\n\t\tp_freq[p[i] - 'a']++;\n\t\ts_freq[s[i] - 'a']++;\n\t}\n\tif (p_freq == s_freq) result.push_back(0);\n\tfor (int i = m; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\ts_freq[s[i] - 'a']++;\n\t\ts_freq[s[i - m] - 'a']--;\n\t\tif (p_freq == s_freq) result.push_back(i - m + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn result;\n}\n---\nSorting every substring takes  time. A fixed-size sliding window with frequency arrays allows comparing character distributions in  (for a fixed alphabet size), reducing complexity to ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_points_from_cards(const std::vector<int>& cards, int k) {\n\tint n = cards.size();\n\tint max_p = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= k; ++i) {\n\t\tint current = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < i; ++j) current += cards[j];\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < k - i; ++j) current += cards[n - 1 - j];\n\t\tmax_p = std::max(max_p, current);\n\t}\n\treturn max_p;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <numeric>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_points_from_cards(const std::vector<int>& cards, int k) {\n\tint n = cards.size();\n\tint current_sum = std::accumulate(cards.begin(), cards.begin() + k, 0);\n\tint max_p = current_sum;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum = current_sum - cards[k - 1 - i] + cards[n - 1 - i];\n\t\tmax_p = std::max(max_p, current_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn max_p;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach re-sums card values for every combination of left and right picks. Using a sliding window starting with  elements from the left and progressively swapping them for elements from the right reduces the complexity to ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint smallest_subarray_sum_greater_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint min_len = n + 1;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tint sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tsum += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (sum > k) {\n\t\t\t\tmin_len = std::min(min_len, j - i + 1);\n\t\t\t\tbreak;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (min_len > n) ? 0 : min_len;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint smallest_subarray_sum_greater_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint min_len = n + 1, current_sum = 0, start = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[end];\n\t\twhile (current_sum > k) {\n\t\t\tmin_len = std::min(min_len, end - start + 1);\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum -= nums[start++];\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (min_len > n) ? 0 : min_len;\n}\n---\nThe  brute force checks every possible starting index. A sliding window effectively finds the smallest range by expanding until the sum threshold is reached and then shrinking from the left in  total time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nint count_subarrays_with_sum_zero(const std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tint count = 0;\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tint current_sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i; j < nums.size(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (current_sum == 0) count++;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <unordered_map>\n\nint count_subarrays_with_sum_zero(const std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tstd::unordered_map<long long, int> prefix_counts;\n\tprefix_counts[0] = 1;\n\tlong long current_sum = 0;\n\tint count = 0;\n\tfor (int x : nums) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += x;\n\t\tif (prefix_counts.count(current_sum)) {\n\t\t\tcount += prefix_counts[current_sum];\n\t\t}\n\t\tprefix_counts[current_sum]++;\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nThe  brute force checks every subarray sum. By using prefix sums and a hash map to track how many times each sum has been seen, we can find subarrays that sum to zero (where ) in  time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_consecutive_zeros_after_flip(const std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tint max_z = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < (int)nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tint ones = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < (int)nums.size(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (nums[j] == 1) ones++;\n\t\t\tif (ones <= 1) max_z = std::max(max_z, j - i + 1);\n\t\t\telse break;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_z;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_consecutive_zeros_after_flip(const std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tint start = 0, ones = 0, max_z = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < (int)nums.size(); ++end) {\n\t\tif (nums[end] == 1) ones++;\n\t\twhile (ones > 1) {\n\t\t\tif (nums[start] == 1) ones--;\n\t\t\tstart++;\n\t\t}\n\t\tmax_z = std::max(max_z, end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn max_z;\n}\n---\nNested loops checking all possible ranges take  time. A sliding window tracks the count of 1s in the current range and shrinks the window from the left only when more than one flip is required, resulting in  time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_sum_subarray_size_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint max_s = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint current = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcurrent += nums[j];\n\t\t}\n\t\tif (i == 0 || current > max_s) max_s = current;\n\t}\n\treturn max_s;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <numeric>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_sum_subarray_size_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tif (nums.size() < (size_t)k) return 0;\n\tint window_sum = std::accumulate(nums.begin(), nums.begin() + k, 0);\n\tint max_s = window_sum;\n\tfor (size_t i = k; i < nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\twindow_sum += nums[i] - nums[i - k];\n\t\tmax_s = std::max(max_s, window_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn max_s;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach recalculates the sum from scratch for every window. By using a sliding window to update the sum in  at each step (adding the new element and removing the old one), we achieve  total time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <set>\n\nint count_distinct_elements_all_subarrays(const std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tint total = 0;\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tstd::set<int> distinct;\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i; j < nums.size(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tdistinct.insert(nums[j]);\n\t\t\ttotal += distinct.size();\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn total;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <unordered_map>\n\nlong long count_distinct_elements_all_subarrays(const std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tlong long total = 0, current_sum = 0;\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tstd::unordered_map<int, int> last_seen;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tint prev = last_seen.count(nums[i]) ? last_seen[nums[i]] : -1;\n\t\tcurrent_sum += (i - prev);\n\t\ttotal += current_sum;\n\t\tlast_seen[nums[i]] = i;\n\t}\n\treturn total;\n}\n---\nThe brute force  solution is extremely slow for large arrays. By observing that an element at index  adds to the distinct count of all subarrays ending at  except those starting after its previous occurrence, we can solve this in  using a hash map."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longest_substring_with_2_unique(std::string s) {\n\tint max_l = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < (int)s.length(); ++i) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < (int)s.length(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tstd::vector<int> freq(256, 0);\n\t\t\tint unique = 0;\n\t\t\tfor (int k = i; k <= j; ++k) {\n\t\t\t\tif (freq[(unsigned char)s[k]] == 0) unique++;\n\t\t\t\tfreq[(unsigned char)s[k]]++;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif (unique <= 2) max_l = std::max(max_l, j - i + 1);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_l;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <unordered_map>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longest_substring_with_2_unique(std::string s) {\n\tstd::unordered_map<char, int> freq;\n\tint start = 0, max_l = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < (int)s.length(); ++end) {\n\t\tfreq[s[end]]++;\n\t\twhile (freq.size() > 2) {\n\t\t\tif (--freq[s[start]] == 0) freq.erase(s[start]);\n\t\t\tstart++;\n\t\t}\n\t\tmax_l = std::max(max_l, end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn max_l;\n}\n---\nThe  brute force checks every substring and its character counts. A variable-size sliding window with a frequency map maintains the number of unique characters in the window, finding the longest valid range in  total time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint find_min_subarray_sum(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint min_s = 2147483647;\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i <= nums.size() - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint current = 0;\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i; j < i + k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcurrent += nums[j];\n\t\t}\n\t\tmin_s = std::min(min_s, current);\n\t}\n\treturn min_s;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <numeric>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint find_min_subarray_sum(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tif (nums.size() < (size_t)k) return 0;\n\tint window_sum = std::accumulate(nums.begin(), nums.begin() + k, 0);\n\tint min_s = window_sum;\n\tfor (size_t i = k; i < nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\twindow_sum += nums[i] - nums[i - k];\n\t\tmin_s = std::min(min_s, window_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn min_s;\n}\n---\nRe-summing the window at every index results in  complexity. A fixed-size sliding window updates the running total in  time per step, reducing the overall complexity to ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint count_subarrays_with_max_element_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint count = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < (int)nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tint cur_max = -1;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < (int)nums.size(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tcur_max = std::max(cur_max, nums[j]);\n\t\t\tif (cur_max == k) count++;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nlong long count_subarrays_with_max_element_at_most(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tlong long count = 0, current_streak = 0;\n\tfor (int x : nums) {\n\t\tif (x <= k) current_streak++;\n\t\telse current_streak = 0;\n\t\tcount += current_streak;\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n\nlong long count_subarrays_with_max_element_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\treturn count_subarrays_with_max_element_at_most(nums, k) - count_subarrays_with_max_element_at_most(nums, k - 1);\n}\n---\nThe  approach is inefficient for large datasets. By calculating the number of subarrays where all elements are  in  time using a streak counter, the exact count for subarrays with maximum element  is found in linear time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_consecutive_chars_after_replacement(std::string s, int k) {\n\tint max_l = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < (int)s.length(); ++i) {\n\t\tstd::vector<int> freq(26, 0);\n\t\tint max_f = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < (int)s.length(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tfreq[s[j] - 'A']++;\n\t\t\tmax_f = std::max(max_f, freq[s[j] - 'A']);\n\t\t\tif ((j - i + 1) - max_f <= k) max_l = std::max(max_l, j - i + 1);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_l;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_consecutive_chars_after_replacement(std::string s, int k) {\n\tstd::vector<int> freq(26, 0);\n\tint max_f = 0, start = 0, max_l = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < (int)s.length(); ++end) {\n\t\tmax_f = std::max(max_f, ++freq[s[end] - 'A']);\n\t\tif ((end - start + 1) - max_f > k) {\n\t\t\tfreq[s[start] - 'A']--;\n\t\t\tstart++;\n\t\t}\n\t\tmax_l = std::max(max_l, end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn max_l;\n}\n---\nThe original solution takes  by checking all possible substrings. A sliding window tracks the character with the maximum frequency in the current window and shrinks only when the number of characters to replace exceeds , yielding  complexity."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::vector<int> sliding_window_median(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tstd::vector<int> result;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= (int)nums.size() - k; ++i) {\n\t\tstd::vector<int> window(nums.begin() + i, nums.begin() + i + k);\n\t\tstd::sort(window.begin(), window.end());\n\t\tresult.push_back(window[k / 2]);\n\t}\n\treturn result;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <set>\n\nstd::vector<int> sliding_window_median(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tstd::vector<int> result;\n\tstd::multiset<int> ms(nums.begin(), nums.begin() + k);\n\tauto it = std::next(ms.begin(), k / 2);\n\tresult.push_back(*it);\n\tfor (size_t i = k; i < nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tms.insert(nums[i]);\n\t\tif (nums[i] < *it) it--;\n\t\tif (nums[i - k] <= *it) it++;\n\t\tms.erase(ms.find(nums[i - k]));\n\t\tresult.push_back(*it);\n\t}\n\treturn result;\n}\n---\nSorting each window independently takes  time. Using a multiset with a persistent iterator to the median allows for  total time, as insertions and deletions are logarithmic and the iterator only shifts by one position per step."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_subarray_sum_excluding_negative(const std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tint max_s = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < (int)nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tint current = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < (int)nums.size(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (nums[j] < 0) break;\n\t\t\tcurrent += nums[j];\n\t\t\tmax_s = std::max(max_s, current);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_s;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_subarray_sum_excluding_negative(const std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tint max_s = 0, current_s = 0;\n\tfor (int x : nums) {\n\t\tif (x < 0) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_s = 0;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tcurrent_s += x;\n\t\t\tmax_s = std::max(max_s, current_s);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_s;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach checks every possible valid subarray. A linear  scan tracks the running sum of positive segments and resets it when a negative number is encountered, providing the same result much more efficiently."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <numeric>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint smallest_sum_subarray_size_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint min_s = 2147483647;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= (int)nums.size() - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint current = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; ++j) current += nums[j];\n\t\tmin_s = std::min(min_s, current);\n\t}\n\treturn min_s;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <numeric>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint smallest_sum_subarray_size_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tif (nums.size() < (size_t)k) return 0;\n\tint current_sum = std::accumulate(nums.begin(), nums.begin() + k, 0);\n\tint min_s = current_sum;\n\tfor (size_t i = k; i < nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[i] - nums[i - k];\n\t\tmin_s = std::min(min_s, current_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn min_s;\n}\n---\nThe  approach repeatedly sums overlapping windows. A fixed-size sliding window updates the sum in  by adding the incoming element and subtracting the outgoing one, reducing the time complexity to ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longest_subarray_sum_exact_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint max_l = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < (int)nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tint sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < (int)nums.size(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tsum += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (sum == k) max_l = std::max(max_l, j - i + 1);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_l;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <unordered_map>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longest_subarray_sum_exact_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tstd::unordered_map<long long, int> prefix_map;\n\tprefix_map[0] = -1;\n\tlong long current_sum = 0;\n\tint max_l = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < (int)nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[i];\n\t\tif (prefix_map.count(current_sum - k)) {\n\t\t\tmax_l = std::max(max_l, i - prefix_map[current_sum - k]);\n\t\t}\n\t\tif (prefix_map.find(current_sum) == prefix_map.end()) {\n\t\t\tprefix_map[current_sum] = i;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_l;\n}\n---\nThe brute-force  approach checks every subarray. Using a hash map to store the first index of each prefix sum allows us to find the longest subarray with sum  in  average time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_consecutive_elements_in_range(const std::vector<int>& nums, int min_v, int max_v) {\n\tint max_c = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < (int)nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tint current = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < (int)nums.size(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (nums[j] >= min_v && nums[j] <= max_v) {\n\t\t\t\tcurrent++;\n\t\t\t\tmax_c = std::max(max_c, current);\n\t\t\t} else break;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_c;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_consecutive_elements_in_range(const std::vector<int>& nums, int min_v, int max_v) {\n\tint max_c = 0, current = 0;\n\tfor (int x : nums) {\n\t\tif (x >= min_v && x <= max_v) {\n\t\t\tcurrent++;\n\t\t\tmax_c = std::max(max_c, current);\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tcurrent = 0;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_c;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach uses nested loops to count segments. A single-pass  solution tracks the current streak and resets it when an out-of-range value is met, ensuring optimal performance."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <numeric>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint min_subarray_sum_size_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint min_s = 2147483647;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= (int)nums.size() - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint current = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; ++j) current += nums[j];\n\t\tmin_s = std::min(min_s, current);\n\t}\n\treturn min_s;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <numeric>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint min_subarray_sum_size_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tif (nums.size() < (size_t)k) return 0;\n\tint window_sum = std::accumulate(nums.begin(), nums.begin() + k, 0);\n\tint min_s = window_sum;\n\tfor (size_t i = k; i < nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\twindow_sum += nums[i] - nums[i - k];\n\t\tmin_s = std::min(min_s, window_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn min_s;\n}\n---\nThe  approach recalculates window sums from scratch. A sliding window reduces the number of additions and subtractions per step to , resulting in an overall  complexity."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_subarray_product(const std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tlong long max_p = -2147483648;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < (int)nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tlong long current = 1;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < (int)nums.size(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tcurrent *= nums[j];\n\t\t\tmax_p = std::max(max_p, current);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (int)max_p;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_subarray_product(const std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tif (nums.empty()) return 0;\n\tlong long max_so_far = nums[0], min_so_far = nums[0], result = nums[0];\n\tfor (size_t i = 1; i < nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tif (nums[i] < 0) std::swap(max_so_far, min_so_far);\n\t\tmax_so_far = std::max((long long)nums[i], max_so_far * nums[i]);\n\t\tmin_so_far = std::min((long long)nums[i], min_so_far * nums[i]);\n\t\tresult = std::max(result, max_so_far);\n\t}\n\treturn (int)result;\n}\n---\nThe  brute-force approach checks all subarray products. A dynamic programming approach tracks both the maximum and minimum product (to handle negative signs) in  time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <unordered_set>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longest_distinct_subarray(const std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tint max_l = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < (int)nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tstd::unordered_set<int> seen;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < (int)nums.size(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (seen.count(nums[j])) break;\n\t\t\tseen.insert(nums[j]);\n\t\t\tmax_l = std::max(max_l, j - i + 1);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_l;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <unordered_map>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longest_distinct_subarray(const std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tstd::unordered_map<int, int> last_seen;\n\tint start = 0, max_l = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < (int)nums.size(); ++end) {\n\t\tif (last_seen.count(nums[end])) {\n\t\t\tstart = std::max(start, last_seen[nums[end]] + 1);\n\t\t}\n\t\tlast_seen[nums[end]] = end;\n\t\tmax_l = std::max(max_l, end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn max_l;\n}\n---\nThe original  code re-scans for every starting index. Using a sliding window with a hash map to track the last index of each element allows identifying the longest distinct range in  average time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <numeric>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_sum_fixed_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint max_s = -2147483648;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= (int)nums.size() - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint current = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; ++j) current += nums[j];\n\t\tmax_s = std::max(max_s, current);\n\t}\n\treturn max_s;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <numeric>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_sum_fixed_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tif (nums.size() < (size_t)k) return 0;\n\tint current_sum = std::accumulate(nums.begin(), nums.begin() + k, 0);\n\tint max_s = current_sum;\n\tfor (size_t i = k; i < nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[i] - nums[i - k];\n\t\tmax_s = std::max(max_s, current_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn max_s;\n}\n---\nThe  approach repeatedly sums overlapping windows. A fixed-size sliding window updates the sum in  by adding the incoming element and subtracting the outgoing one, resulting in  time complexity."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_subarray_sum_fixed(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint max_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint current_sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[j];\n\t\t}\n\t\tif (i == 0 || current_sum > max_sum) max_sum = current_sum;\n\t}\n\treturn max_sum;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_subarray_sum_fixed(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tint window_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) window_sum += nums[i];\n\tint max_sum = window_sum;\n\tfor (int i = k; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\twindow_sum += nums[i] - nums[i - k];\n\t\tmax_sum = std::max(max_sum, window_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn max_sum;\n}\n---\nThe original nested loop approach recomputes the sum for every window, leading to  complexity. The optimized version uses a fixed-size sliding window to update the sum in  time by adding the next element and removing the previous one, achieving  total time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <unordered_set>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint length_of_longest_substring(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length();\n\tint max_len = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tstd::unordered_set<char> chars;\n\t\t\tbool duplicate = false;\n\t\t\tfor (int k = i; k <= j; ++k) {\n\t\t\t\tif (chars.count(s[k])) { duplicate = true; break; }\n\t\t\t\tchars.insert(s[k]);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif (!duplicate) max_len = std::max(max_len, j - i + 1);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_len;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint length_of_longest_substring(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length();\n\tstd::vector<int> last_pos(256, -1);\n\tint max_len = 0, start = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tif (last_pos[(unsigned char)s[end]] >= start) {\n\t\t\tstart = last_pos[(unsigned char)s[end]] + 1;\n\t\t}\n\t\tlast_pos[(unsigned char)s[end]] = end;\n\t\tmax_len = std::max(max_len, end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn max_len;\n}\n---\nThe brute-force  approach is replaced by a variable sliding window using an array as a direct-address table. By tracking the last seen index of each character, we can jump the start pointer in , resulting in an  linear scan."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nlong long count_subarrays_sum_limit(const std::vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n\tlong long count = 0;\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tlong long current_sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (current_sum <= target) count++;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nlong long count_subarrays_sum_limit(const std::vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n\tlong long count = 0, current_sum = 0;\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint start = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[end];\n\t\twhile (current_sum > target && start <= end) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum -= nums[start++];\n\t\t}\n\t\tcount += (end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nThe original  solution redundantly checks every possible subarray. For arrays with non-negative integers, a sliding window maintains a running sum, allowing us to count all valid subarrays ending at a specific index in  total time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nstd::vector<int> max_sliding_window(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tstd::vector<int> result;\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n == 0) return result;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint current_max = INT_MIN;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_max = std::max(current_max, nums[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t\tresult.push_back(current_max);\n\t}\n\treturn result;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <deque>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::vector<int> max_sliding_window(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n == 0) return {};\n\tstd::vector<int> result;\n\tstd::deque<int> dq;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tif (!dq.empty() && dq.front() == i - k) dq.pop_front();\n\t\twhile (!dq.empty() && nums[dq.back()] < nums[i]) dq.pop_back();\n\t\tdq.push_back(i);\n\t\tif (i >= k - 1) result.push_back(nums[dq.front()]);\n\t}\n\treturn result;\n}\n---\nThe nested loop approach has  complexity as it re-scans each window for the maximum. A monotonic deque stores indices of potential maxima in descending order, enabling  access to the window's maximum and reducing total time to ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nint smallest_subarray_sum_at_least(const std::vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint min_len = INT_MAX;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tint current_sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (current_sum >= target) {\n\t\t\t\tmin_len = std::min(min_len, j - i + 1);\n\t\t\t\tbreak;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (min_len == INT_MAX) ? 0 : min_len;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nint smallest_subarray_sum_at_least(const std::vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint min_len = INT_MAX, current_sum = 0, start = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[end];\n\t\twhile (current_sum >= target) {\n\t\t\tmin_len = std::min(min_len, end - start + 1);\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum -= nums[start++];\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (min_len == INT_MAX) ? 0 : min_len;\n}\n---\nThe original  algorithm re-evaluates subarrays from every starting index. By using a variable-size sliding window that contracts from the left once the target is reached, we find the minimum length in  time as each element is processed twice at most."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint min_subarray_sum_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint min_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tsum += nums[j];\n\t\t}\n\t\tif (i == 0 || sum < min_sum) min_sum = sum;\n\t}\n\treturn min_sum;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint min_subarray_sum_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tint window_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) window_sum += nums[i];\n\tint min_sum = window_sum;\n\tfor (int i = k; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\twindow_sum += nums[i] - nums[i - k];\n\t\tmin_sum = std::min(min_sum, window_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn min_sum;\n}\n---\nThe  complexity of the nested loops is suboptimal for large windows. A fixed-size sliding window reduces this to  by maintaining a running sum and updating it in constant time for each shift."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <unordered_map>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longest_k_distinct(std::string s, int k) {\n\tint n = s.length();\n\tint max_len = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tstd::unordered_map<char, int> freq;\n\t\t\tfor (int l = i; l <= j; ++l) freq[s[l]]++;\n\t\t\tif (freq.size() <= (size_t)k) max_len = std::max(max_len, j - i + 1);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_len;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longest_k_distinct(std::string s, int k) {\n\tint n = s.length();\n\tif (k == 0) return 0;\n\tstd::vector<int> freq(256, 0);\n\tint distinct = 0, start = 0, max_len = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tif (freq[(unsigned char)s[end]]++ == 0) distinct++;\n\t\twhile (distinct > k) {\n\t\t\tif (--freq[(unsigned char)s[start++]] == 0) distinct--;\n\t\t}\n\t\tmax_len = std::max(max_len, end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn max_len;\n}\n---\nThe brute-force  approach re-evaluates character frequencies for all substrings. A variable sliding window with a frequency array tracks the number of distinct characters in  per step, reducing the complexity to ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nint count_subarrays_product_less(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tif (k <= 1) return 0;\n\tint count = 0;\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tlong long product = 1;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tproduct *= nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (product < k) count++;\n\t\t\telse break;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nint count_subarrays_product_less(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tif (k <= 1) return 0;\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint count = 0, start = 0;\n\tlong long product = 1;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tproduct *= nums[end];\n\t\twhile (product >= k && start <= end) {\n\t\t\tproduct /= nums[start++];\n\t\t}\n\t\tcount += (end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nThe original  algorithm checks every range from every start point. A sliding window maintaining the product can process the array in  time by shrinking the window when the product threshold is exceeded, counting valid subarrays ending at 'end' efficiently."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <numeric>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::vector<double> average_of_windows(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tstd::vector<double> result;\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tdouble sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tsum += nums[j];\n\t\t}\n\t\tresult.push_back(sum / k);\n\t}\n\treturn result;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nstd::vector<double> average_of_windows(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n < k) return {};\n\tstd::vector<double> result;\n\tdouble window_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) window_sum += nums[i];\n\tresult.push_back(window_sum / k);\n\tfor (int i = k; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\twindow_sum += nums[i] - nums[i - k];\n\t\tresult.push_back(window_sum / k);\n\t}\n\treturn result;\n}\n---\nThe brute-force  approach repeatedly sums overlapping elements. Using a sliding window reduces the update step to  per new element, resulting in  time complexity and avoiding redundant arithmetic."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_consecutive_ones(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint max_len = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tint zeros = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (nums[j] == 0) zeros++;\n\t\t\tif (zeros <= k) max_len = std::max(max_len, j - i + 1);\n\t\t\telse break;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_len;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_consecutive_ones(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint start = 0, zeros = 0, max_len = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tif (nums[end] == 0) zeros++;\n\t\twhile (zeros > k) {\n\t\t\tif (nums[start] == 0) zeros--;\n\t\t\tstart++;\n\t\t}\n\t\tmax_l = std::max(max_len, end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn max_len;\n}\n---\nThe original solution has  complexity due to nested iterations over subarrays. A variable sliding window approach maintains the count of zeros and adjusts the window boundaries in  per element, leading to an overall  performance."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::vector<int> min_sliding_window(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tstd::vector<int> result;\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint current_min = nums[i];\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < i + k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_min = std::min(current_min, nums[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t\tresult.push_back(current_min);\n\t}\n\treturn result;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <deque>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::vector<int> min_sliding_window(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n == 0) return {};\n\tstd::vector<int> result;\n\tstd::deque<int> dq;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tif (!dq.empty() && dq.front() == i - k) dq.pop_front();\n\t\twhile (!dq.empty() && nums[dq.back()] > nums[i]) dq.pop_back();\n\t\tdq.push_back(i);\n\t\tif (i >= k - 1) result.push_back(nums[dq.front()]);\n\t}\n\treturn result;\n}\n---\nRe-calculating the minimum for every window results in  time. By using a monotonic deque to maintain the indices of elements in increasing order of value, we can extract the minimum in , achieving  total time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nint count_subarrays_sum_exactly_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint count = 0;\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tint current_sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (current_sum == k) count++;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <unordered_map>\n\nint count_subarrays_sum_exactly_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tstd::unordered_map<int, int> prefix_sums;\n\tprefix_sums[0] = 1;\n\tint count = 0, current_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int num : nums) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += num;\n\t\tif (prefix_sums.count(current_sum - k)) {\n\t\t\tcount += prefix_sums[current_sum - k];\n\t\t}\n\t\tprefix_sums[current_sum]++;\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nThe original nested loop approach takes  time. By using a hash map to store the frequency of prefix sums, we can find the number of subarrays ending at each index that sum to  in  average time, resulting in  complexity."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_sum_distinct_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint max_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tstd::vector<int> window;\n\t\tint current_sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; ++j) {\n\t\t\twindow.push_back(nums[j]);\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[j];\n\t\t}\n\t\tstd::sort(window.begin(), window.end());\n\t\tbool unique = true;\n\t\tfor (int l = 1; l < k; ++l) {\n\t\t\tif (window[l] == window[l-1]) { unique = false; break; }\n\t\t}\n\t\tif (unique) max_sum = std::max(max_sum, current_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn max_sum;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <unordered_map>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_sum_distinct_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tstd::unordered_map<int, int> freq;\n\tint current_sum = 0, max_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[i];\n\t\tfreq[nums[i]]++;\n\t}\n\tif (freq.size() == (size_t)k) max_sum = current_sum;\n\tfor (int i = k; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[i] - nums[i - k];\n\t\tfreq[nums[i]]++;\n\t\tif (--freq[nums[i - k]] == 0) freq.erase(nums[i - k]);\n\t\tif (freq.size() == (size_t)k) max_sum = std::max(max_sum, current_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn max_sum;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach re-sorts every window to check for uniqueness. A fixed-size sliding window with an `unordered_map` tracks frequencies and window sums in  average time, allowing  uniqueness checks via the map's size."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longest_subarray_sum_at_most_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint max_len = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tint current_sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (current_sum <= k) max_len = std::max(max_len, j - i + 1);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_len;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longest_subarray_sum_at_most_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint start = 0, max_len = 0, current_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[end];\n\t\twhile (current_sum > k && start <= end) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum -= nums[start++];\n\t\t}\n\t\tmax_len = std::max(max_len, end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn max_len;\n}\n---\nThe original  algorithm re-sums from every starting point. A variable sliding window approach maintains the sum as elements are added, contracting from the left only when necessary to achieve an  linear time complexity."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nbool check_permutation(std::string s1, std::string s2) {\n\tint n1 = s1.length(), n2 = s2.length();\n\tif (n1 > n2) return false;\n\tstd::sort(s1.begin(), s1.end());\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n2 - n1; ++i) {\n\t\tstd::string sub = s2.substr(i, n1);\n\t\tstd::sort(sub.begin(), sub.end());\n\t\tif (s1 == sub) return true;\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n\nbool check_permutation(std::string s1, std::string s2) {\n\tint n1 = s1.length(), n2 = s2.length();\n\tif (n1 > n2) return false;\n\tstd::vector<int> s1_freq(26, 0), window_freq(26, 0);\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n1; ++i) {\n\t\ts1_freq[s1[i] - 'a']++;\n\t\twindow_freq[s2[i] - 'a']++;\n\t}\n\tif (s1_freq == window_freq) return true;\n\tfor (int i = n1; i < n2; ++i) {\n\t\twindow_freq[s2[i] - 'a']++;\n\t\twindow_freq[s2[i - n1] - 'a']--;\n\t\tif (s1_freq == window_freq) return true;\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}\n---\nThe  complexity stems from re-sorting substrings. Using a fixed sliding window with character frequency arrays allows  updates and  comparisons per character, reducing the runtime to ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint total_fruits(const std::vector<int>& fruits) {\n\tint n = fruits.size();\n\tint max_fruits = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tstd::unordered_map<int, int> types;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\ttypes[fruits[j]]++;\n\t\t\tif (types.size() <= 2) max_fruits = std::max(max_fruits, j - i + 1);\n\t\t\telse break;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_fruits;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <unordered_map>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint total_fruits(const std::vector<int>& fruits) {\n\tint n = fruits.size();\n\tstd::unordered_map<int, int> window;\n\tint start = 0, max_fruits = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\twindow[fruits[end]]++;\n\t\twhile (window.size() > 2) {\n\t\t\tif (--window[fruits[start]] == 0) window.erase(fruits[start]);\n\t\t\tstart++;\n\t\t}\n\t\tmax_fruits = std::max(max_fruits, end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn max_fruits;\n}\n---\nThe brute-force  approach re-allocates an `unordered_map` for every possible starting index. A variable sliding window with a persistent frequency map tracks distinct values in  per shift, resulting in  time complexity."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_sum_distinct_windows(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint max_s = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tstd::vector<int> sub(nums.begin() + i, nums.begin() + i + k);\n\t\tstd::sort(sub.begin(), sub.end());\n\t\tif (std::unique(sub.begin(), sub.end()) == sub.end()) {\n\t\t\tint sum = 0;\n\t\t\tfor (int x : sub) sum += x;\n\t\t\tmax_s = std::max(max_s, sum);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_s;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <unordered_map>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_sum_distinct_windows(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tstd::unordered_map<int, int> freq;\n\tint current_sum = 0, max_s = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[i];\n\t\tfreq[nums[i]]++;\n\t}\n\tif (freq.size() == (size_t)k) max_s = current_sum;\n\tfor (int i = k; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[i] - nums[i - k];\n\t\tfreq[nums[i]]++;\n\t\tif (--freq[nums[i - k]] == 0) freq.erase(nums[i - k]);\n\t\tif (freq.size() == (size_t)k) max_s = std::max(max_s, current_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn max_s;\n}\n---\nSorting and summing each window leads to  complexity. A sliding window with a frequency map maintains the set of distinct elements and their sum in  average time, providing  checks for window validity."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longest_subarray_sum_limit(const std::vector<int>& nums, int limit) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint res = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tint sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tsum += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (sum <= limit) res = std::max(res, j - i + 1);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longest_subarray_sum_limit(const std::vector<int>& nums, int limit) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint start = 0, res = 0, sum = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tsum += nums[end];\n\t\twhile (sum > limit && start <= end) {\n\t\t\tsum -= nums[start++];\n\t\t}\n\t\tres = std::max(res, end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe original solution iterates through all  subarrays. A variable sliding window approach maintains a running sum and adjusts the window span in a single pass, yielding  time complexity and avoiding redundant summation."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::vector<int> window_maximums(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n < k) return {};\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint m = nums[i];\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < i + k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tm = std::max(m, nums[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t\tres.push_back(m);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <deque>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::vector<int> window_maximums(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tstd::deque<int> dq;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tif (!dq.empty() && dq.front() == i - k) dq.pop_front();\n\t\twhile (!dq.empty() && nums[dq.back()] <= nums[i]) dq.pop_back();\n\t\tdq.push_back(i);\n\t\tif (i >= k - 1) res.push_back(nums[dq.front()]);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe brute-force method takes  time to find window maxima. A monotonic deque ensures that the maximum element is always at the front, reducing the time complexity to  because each index is pushed and popped at most once."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint min_subarray_len(int target, const std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint min_l = n + 1;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tint sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tsum += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (sum >= target) {\n\t\t\t\tmin_l = std::min(min_l, j - i + 1);\n\t\t\t\tbreak;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (min_l > n) ? 0 : min_l;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint min_subarray_len(int target, const std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint start = 0, sum = 0, min_l = n + 1;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tsum += nums[end];\n\t\twhile (sum >= target) {\n\t\t\tmin_l = std::min(min_l, end - start + 1);\n\t\t\tsum -= nums[start++];\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (min_l > n) ? 0 : min_l;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach checks all combinations starting from each index. A sliding window effectively finds the minimal range in  time by incrementally expanding and then aggressively shrinking the start boundary whenever the sum condition is satisfied."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_sum_k_subarray(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint res = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; ++j) s += nums[j];\n\t\tif (i == 0 || s > res) res = s;\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_sum_k_subarray(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tint cur = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) cur += nums[i];\n\tint res = cur;\n\tfor (int i = k; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tcur += nums[i] - nums[i - k];\n\t\tres = std::max(res, cur);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nNested loops cause  performance due to redundant additions. Using a sliding window reduces the update to a constant time  subtraction and addition per step, leading to  time complexity and much better performance for large ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <unordered_set>\n\nint unique_char_substring(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length();\n\tint max_v = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tstd::unordered_set<char> seen;\n\t\t\tbool valid = true;\n\t\t\tfor (int k = i; k <= j; ++k) {\n\t\t\t\tif (seen.count(s[k])) { valid = false; break; }\n\t\t\t\tseen.insert(s[k]);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif (valid) max_v = std::max(max_v, j - i + 1);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_v;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n\nint unique_char_substring(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length();\n\tstd::vector<int> last(256, -1);\n\tint start = 0, max_v = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tif (last[(unsigned char)s[end]] >= start) {\n\t\t\tstart = last[(unsigned char)s[end]] + 1;\n\t\t}\n\t\tlast[(unsigned char)s[end]] = end;\n\t\tif (end - start + 1 > max_v) max_v = end - start + 1;\n\t}\n\treturn max_v;\n}\n---\nThe original  solution checks all substrings using a hash set. The optimized version uses a sliding window with an integer array to track character positions, enabling an  single-pass solution with minimal memory overhead."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nlong long subarray_sum_count(const std::vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n\tlong long count = 0;\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tlong long s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\ts += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (s <= target) count++;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nlong long subarray_sum_count(const std::vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint start = 0;\n\tlong long count = 0, current_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[end];\n\t\twhile (current_sum > target && start <= end) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum -= nums[start++];\n\t\t}\n\t\tcount += (end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nThe  nested loop approach redundantly traverses subarrays. A sliding window with two pointers efficiently counts the number of valid subarrays ending at each position in  time, as each pointer moves monotonically through the array."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::vector<int> sliding_window_max(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint current_max = nums[i];\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < i + k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_max = std::max(current_max, nums[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t\tres.push_back(current_max);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <deque>\n\nstd::vector<int> sliding_window_max(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n == 0) return {};\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tstd::deque<int> dq;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tif (!dq.empty() && dq.front() == i - k) dq.pop_front();\n\t\twhile (!dq.empty() && nums[dq.back()] <= nums[i]) dq.pop_back();\n\t\tdq.push_back(i);\n\t\tif (i >= k - 1) res.push_back(nums[dq.front()]);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe brute-force  approach repeatedly searches for the max in each window. A monotonic deque maintains potential max indices in  total time, allowing  access to the current maximum as the window moves."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint find_shortest_subarray_with_sum(const std::vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint min_len = n + 1;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tint current_sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (current_sum >= target) {\n\t\t\t\tmin_len = std::min(min_len, j - i + 1);\n\t\t\t\tbreak;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (min_len > n) ? 0 : min_len;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint find_shortest_subarray_with_sum(const std::vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint start = 0, sum = 0, min_len = n + 1;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tsum += nums[end];\n\t\twhile (sum >= target) {\n\t\t\tmin_len = std::min(min_len, end - start + 1);\n\t\t\tsum -= nums[start++];\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (min_len > n) ? 0 : min_len;\n}\n---\nThe original  algorithm re-sums subarrays unnecessarily. A variable sliding window approach shrinks the window from the left as soon as the target is met, finding the minimum length in  time while ensuring all elements are visited at most twice."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <numeric>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint min_subarray_sum_size_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint min_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint current_sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[j];\n\t\t}\n\t\tif (i == 0 || current_sum < min_sum) min_sum = current_sum;\n\t}\n\treturn min_sum;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint min_subarray_sum_size_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tint current_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) current_sum += nums[i];\n\tint min_sum = current_sum;\n\tfor (int i = k; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[i] - nums[i - k];\n\t\tmin_sum = std::min(min_sum, current_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn min_sum;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach recalculates the sum of each -size subarray. Using a fixed sliding window reduces this to  by performing one addition and one subtraction per step, which is significantly faster for large  values."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <unordered_map>\n\nint max_substring_distinct(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length();\n\tint result = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tstd::unordered_map<char, bool> used;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (used[s[j]]) break;\n\t\t\tused[s[j]] = true;\n\t\t\tif (j - i + 1 > result) result = j - i + 1;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn result;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_substring_distinct(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length();\n\tstd::vector<int> pos(256, -1);\n\tint start = 0, result = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tstart = std::max(start, pos[(unsigned char)s[end]] + 1);\n\t\tpos[(unsigned char)s[end]] = end;\n\t\tresult = std::max(result, end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn result;\n}\n---\nThe  approach is slow because it checks all substrings and manages a hash map. The sliding window with a position array finds the longest unique character sequence in  time by shifting the start pointer based on previous character indices."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nlong long sum_of_all_subarrays(const std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tlong long total = 0;\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tfor (int k = i; k <= j; ++k) {\n\t\t\t\ttotal += nums[k];\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn total;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nlong long sum_of_all_subarrays(const std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tlong long total = 0;\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\ttotal += (long long)nums[i] * (i + 1) * (n - i);\n\t}\n\treturn total;\n}\n---\nThe original code has  complexity due to triple nesting. By observing that an element at index  appears in  subarrays, we can compute the total sum in a single  pass using frequency math."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nint count_at_most_target(const std::vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n\tint count = 0;\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tint current_sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (current_sum <= target) count++;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nint count_at_most_target(const std::vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint start = 0, current_sum = 0, count = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[end];\n\t\twhile (current_sum > target && start <= end) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum -= nums[start++];\n\t\t}\n\t\tcount += (end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nThe  nested loop redundantly sums the same elements repeatedly. A sliding window reduces the complexity to  by calculating valid subarrays ending at 'end' in constant time after adjusting the 'start' pointer based on the running sum."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::vector<int> window_max_list(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tstd::vector<int> out;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint val = nums[i];\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < i + k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (nums[j] > val) val = nums[j];\n\t\t}\n\t\tout.push_back(val);\n\t}\n\treturn out;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <deque>\n\nstd::vector<int> window_max_list(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tstd::vector<int> out;\n\tstd::deque<int> dq;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tif (!dq.empty() && dq.front() == i - k) dq.pop_front();\n\t\twhile (!dq.empty() && nums[dq.back()] <= nums[i]) dq.pop_back();\n\t\tdq.push_back(i);\n\t\tif (i >= k - 1) out.push_back(nums[dq.front()]);\n\t}\n\treturn out;\n}\n---\nThe naive approach takes  because it re-scans for the max in every window. Using a monotonic deque allows us to maintain elements in a way that the maximum of the current window is always at the front, achieving  time complexity."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nint find_min_window_sum_ge(const std::vector<int>& nums, int s) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint min_len = INT_MAX;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tint current_sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (current_sum >= s) {\n\t\t\t\tmin_len = std::min(min_len, j - i + 1);\n\t\t\t\tbreak;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (min_len == INT_MAX) ? 0 : min_len;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nint find_min_window_sum_ge(const std::vector<int>& nums, int s) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint start = 0, current_sum = 0, min_len = INT_MAX;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[end];\n\t\twhile (current_sum >= s) {\n\t\t\tmin_len = std::min(min_len, end - start + 1);\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum -= nums[start++];\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (min_len == INT_MAX) ? 0 : min_len;\n}\n---\nThe original  code re-evaluates ranges from scratch for every starting index. A variable-size sliding window shrinks from the left once the threshold is reached, finding the global minimum in  as each element is processed twice at most."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_sum_subarray_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint max_v = -1000000000;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; ++j) s += nums[j];\n\t\tif (s > max_v) max_v = s;\n\t}\n\treturn max_v;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_sum_subarray_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tint window_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) window_sum += nums[i];\n\tint max_v = window_sum;\n\tfor (int i = k; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\twindow_sum += nums[i] - nums[i - k];\n\t\tmax_v = std::max(max_v, window_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn max_v;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach has a performance bottleneck due to redundant summing in inner loops. Implementing a fixed sliding window reduces this to  by updating the current window sum in constant time  for each step."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint lengthOfLongestSubstring(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length(), res = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tstd::vector<bool> seen(256, false);\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (seen[(unsigned char)s[j]]) break;\n\t\t\tseen[(unsigned char)s[j]] = true;\n\t\t\tres = std::max(res, j - i + 1);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint lengthOfLongestSubstring(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length();\n\tstd::vector<int> last_idx(256, -1);\n\tint start = 0, res = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tstart = std::max(start, last_idx[(unsigned char)s[end]] + 1);\n\t\tlast_idx[(unsigned char)s[end]] = end;\n\t\tres = std::max(res, end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe  solution redundantly re-checks characters. A sliding window using an index array for tracking character positions achieves  by moving the left pointer only when necessary, effectively processing each character once."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nlong long count_subarrays_under_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tlong long count = 0;\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tlong long s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\ts += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (s < k) count++;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nlong long count_subarrays_under_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint left = 0;\n\tlong long sum = 0, count = 0;\n\tfor (int right = 0; right < n; ++right) {\n\t\tsum += nums[right];\n\t\twhile (sum >= k && left <= right) {\n\t\t\tsum -= nums[left++];\n\t\t}\n\t\tcount += (right - left + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nThe nested  loop is inefficient for long arrays. A sliding window technique allows us to calculate the number of valid subarrays ending at 'right' in  amortized time, leading to a total linear complexity of ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nstd::vector<int> max_in_windows(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint cur_max = INT_MIN;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcur_max = std::max(cur_max, nums[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t\tres.push_back(cur_max);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <deque>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::vector<int> max_in_windows(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n == 0) return {};\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tstd::deque<int> dq;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tif (!dq.empty() && dq.front() == i - k) dq.pop_front();\n\t\twhile (!dq.empty() && nums[dq.back()] <= nums[i]) dq.pop_back();\n\t\tdq.push_back(i);\n\t\tif (i >= k - 1) res.push_back(nums[dq.front()]);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach re-scans the window for the maximum at every step. A monotonic deque preserves the indices of elements in descending order, allowing  access to the window's max and reducing the total complexity to ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint smallest_sub_sum(const std::vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint min_len = n + 1;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tint current_sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (current_sum >= target) {\n\t\t\t\tmin_len = std::min(min_len, j - i + 1);\n\t\t\t\tbreak;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (min_len > n) ? 0 : min_len;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint smallest_sub_sum(const std::vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint start = 0, current_sum = 0, min_len = n + 1;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[end];\n\t\twhile (current_sum >= target) {\n\t\t\tmin_len = std::min(min_len, end - start + 1);\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum -= nums[start++];\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (min_len > n) ? 0 : min_len;\n}\n---\nThe original  solution iterates all subarrays to find the minimum length. Using a variable-sized sliding window shrinks the range once the target is met, ensuring  complexity as each element is pushed and popped exactly once."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_sub_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint res = -2000000000;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; ++j) s += nums[j];\n\t\tres = std::max(res, s);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_sub_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tint cur = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) cur += nums[i];\n\tint res = cur;\n\tfor (int i = k; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tcur += nums[i] - nums[i - k];\n\t\tres = std::max(res, cur);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe  complexity of nested loops is slow for large . Using a fixed sliding window maintains the sum in  by updating only the boundaries, reducing the time complexity to  for any ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <unordered_set>\n\nint longest_no_repeat(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length(), res = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tstd::unordered_set<char> seen;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (seen.count(s[j])) break;\n\t\t\tseen.insert(s[j]);\n\t\t\tif (j - i + 1 > res) res = j - i + 1;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longest_no_repeat(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length();\n\tstd::vector<int> last(256, -1);\n\tint start = 0, res = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tif (last[(unsigned char)s[end]] >= start) {\n\t\t\tstart = last[(unsigned char)s[end]] + 1;\n\t\t}\n\t\tlast[(unsigned char)s[end]] = end;\n\t\tres = std::max(res, end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach checks all substrings with a hash set. A variable sliding window using an index array tracks the last seen positions of characters, providing an  time complexity and much lower memory overhead."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nlong long count_sub_le_target(const std::vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n\tlong long count = 0;\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tlong long current = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcurrent += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (current <= target) count++;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nlong long count_sub_le_target(const std::vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint start = 0;\n\tlong long count = 0, sum = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tsum += nums[end];\n\t\twhile (sum > target && start <= end) {\n\t\t\tsum -= nums[start++];\n\t\t}\n\t\tcount += (end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nThe original nested loops take  time by redundantly checking all subarray sums. A sliding window reduces this to  by adjusting the window bounds dynamically, counting all valid subarrays ending at 'end' in constant time per step."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::vector<int> get_window_max(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tstd::vector<int> result;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint max_val = nums[i];\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < i + k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (nums[j] > max_val) max_val = nums[j];\n\t\t}\n\t\tresult.push_back(max_val);\n\t}\n\treturn result;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <deque>\n\nstd::vector<int> get_window_max(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n == 0) return {};\n\tstd::vector<int> result;\n\tstd::deque<int> dq;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tif (!dq.empty() && dq.front() == i - k) dq.pop_front();\n\t\twhile (!dq.empty() && nums[dq.back()] <= nums[i]) dq.pop_back();\n\t\tdq.push_back(i);\n\t\tif (i >= k - 1) result.push_back(nums[dq.front()]);\n\t}\n\treturn result;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach re-scans the window for the maximum at every step. A monotonic deque preserves the indices of elements in descending order, allowing  access to the window's max and reducing the total complexity to ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint min_subarray_len_ge(const std::vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), res = n + 1;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tint s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\ts += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (s >= target) {\n\t\t\t\tres = std::min(res, j - i + 1);\n\t\t\t\tbreak;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (res > n) ? 0 : res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint min_subarray_len_ge(const std::vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint start = 0, sum = 0, res = n + 1;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tsum += nums[end];\n\t\twhile (sum >= target) {\n\t\t\tres = std::min(res, end - start + 1);\n\t\t\tsum -= nums[start++];\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (res > n) ? 0 : res;\n}\n---\nThe brute-force  approach checks all subarray sums starting from each index. A sliding window effectively finds the minimum length in  time by shrinking the window as soon as the target sum is achieved, processing each element twice at most."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_sum_size_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), max_v = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint current_sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; ++j) current_sum += nums[j];\n\t\tif (i == 0 || current_sum > max_v) max_v = current_sum;\n\t}\n\treturn max_v;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_sum_size_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tint window_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) window_sum += nums[i];\n\tint max_v = window_sum;\n\tfor (int i = k; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\twindow_sum += nums[i] - nums[i - k];\n\t\tmax_v = std::max(max_v, window_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn max_v;\n}\n---\nThe original solution takes  by re-summing windows. Using a fixed sliding window reduces this to  time because updating the sum for a new window only requires two operations regardless of the window size ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <unordered_set>\n\nint longest_unique_str(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length(), max_len = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tstd::unordered_set<char> set;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (set.count(s[j])) break;\n\t\t\tset.insert(s[j]);\n\t\t\tif (j - i + 1 > max_len) max_len = j - i + 1;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_len;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longest_unique_str(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length();\n\tstd::vector<int> m(256, -1);\n\tint start = 0, max_len = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tstart = std::max(start, m[(unsigned char)s[end]] + 1);\n\t\tm[(unsigned char)s[end]] = end;\n\t\tmax_len = std::max(max_len, end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn max_len;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach is slow for large strings. A sliding window with an index-tracking array optimizes the search to  time by jumping the window start pointer to the last known position of the repeating character in constant time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nlong long count_subarrays_sum_le(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tlong long count = 0;\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tlong long current = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcurrent += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (current <= k) count++;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nlong long count_subarrays_sum_le(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint start = 0;\n\tlong long count = 0, sum = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tsum += nums[end];\n\t\twhile (sum > k && start <= end) {\n\t\t\tsum -= nums[start++];\n\t\t}\n\t\tcount += (end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nThe  complexity in the original code arises from checking every possible range. A sliding window reduces this to  because it only moves each boundary forward, counting all valid subarrays ending at a given index efficiently."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::vector<int> window_maxima(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint current_max = nums[i];\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < i + k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_max = std::max(current_max, nums[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t\tres.push_back(current_max);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <deque>\n\nstd::vector<int> window_maxima(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n == 0) return {};\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tstd::deque<int> dq;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tif (!dq.empty() && dq.front() == i - k) dq.pop_front();\n\t\twhile (!dq.empty() && nums[dq.back()] <= nums[i]) dq.pop_back();\n\t\tdq.push_back(i);\n\t\tif (i >= k - 1) res.push_back(nums[dq.front()]);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe original  algorithm re-scans for the maximum in each sliding window. By using a monotonic deque to maintain potentially maximum elements, we can retrieve the maximum in  and process the entire array in  time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nint min_subarray_len_ge_target(const std::vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), min_l = INT_MAX;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tint s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\ts += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (s >= target) {\n\t\t\t\tmin_l = std::min(min_l, j - i + 1);\n\t\t\t\tbreak;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (min_l == INT_MAX) ? 0 : min_l;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nint min_subarray_len_ge_target(const std::vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint start = 0, sum = 0, min_l = INT_MAX;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tsum += nums[end];\n\t\twhile (sum >= target) {\n\t\t\tmin_l = std::min(min_l, end - start + 1);\n\t\t\tsum -= nums[start++];\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (min_l == INT_MAX) ? 0 : min_l;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach checks every subarray starting at each index. A sliding window technique achieves  time complexity by using two pointers to shrink the window once the target sum is met, minimizing the length efficiently."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_sum_fixed_window(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), res = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; ++j) s += nums[j];\n\t\tif (i == 0 || s > res) res = s;\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_sum_fixed_window(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tint window_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) window_sum += nums[i];\n\tint res = window_sum;\n\tfor (int i = k; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\twindow_sum += nums[i] - nums[i - k];\n\t\tres = std::max(res, window_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe nested  loops re-add elements redundantly. By using a sliding window, we maintain the sum in  at each step by subtracting the leaving element and adding the incoming one, resulting in an optimal  solution."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <unordered_set>\n\nint longest_substr_no_rep(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length(), res = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tstd::unordered_set<char> seen;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (seen.count(s[j])) break;\n\t\t\tseen.insert(s[j]);\n\t\t\tif (j - i + 1 > res) res = j - i + 1;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longest_substr_no_rep(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length();\n\tstd::vector<int> last_pos(256, -1);\n\tint start = 0, res = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tif (last_pos[(unsigned char)s[end]] >= start) {\n\t\t\tstart = last_pos[(unsigned char)s[end]] + 1;\n\t\t}\n\t\tlast_pos[(unsigned char)s[end]] = end;\n\t\tres = std::max(res, end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach is slow for large strings due to nested checks. Using a sliding window with an index array to jump the window's start position optimizes the search to  time and minimizes memory access overhead."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nlong long count_subarrays_sum_lte(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tlong long count = 0;\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tlong long current = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcurrent += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (current <= k) count++;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nlong long count_subarrays_sum_lte(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint start = 0;\n\tlong long count = 0, sum = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tsum += nums[end];\n\t\twhile (sum > k && start <= end) {\n\t\t\tsum -= nums[start++];\n\t\t}\n\t\tcount += (end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nThe nested loop  is inefficient for large inputs. A sliding window technique processes the array in  by maintaining a running sum and shifting the start pointer, counting all valid subarrays for each 'end' index in  amortized time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::vector<int> max_sliding_vals(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint m = nums[i];\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < i + k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (nums[j] > m) m = nums[j];\n\t\t}\n\t\tres.push_back(m);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <deque>\n\nstd::vector<int> max_sliding_vals(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n == 0) return {};\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tstd::deque<int> dq;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tif (!dq.empty() && dq.front() == i - k) dq.pop_front();\n\t\twhile (!dq.empty() && nums[dq.back()] <= nums[i]) dq.pop_back();\n\t\tdq.push_back(i);\n\t\tif (i >= k - 1) res.push_back(nums[dq.front()]);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe original code has  complexity due to repeated maximum searches. A monotonic deque keeps track of elements in a way that allows  max retrieval and ensures  total runtime since each element enters and leaves the deque once."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nint min_subarray_sum_size_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint min_sum = INT_MAX;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint current_sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[j];\n\t\t}\n\t\tif (current_sum < min_sum) min_sum = current_sum;\n\t}\n\treturn min_sum;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nint min_subarray_sum_size_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tint current_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) current_sum += nums[i];\n\tint min_sum = current_sum;\n\tfor (int i = k; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[i] - nums[i - k];\n\t\tmin_sum = std::min(min_sum, current_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn min_sum;\n}\n---\nThe original nested loop approach recomputes the sum for every window, leading to  complexity. The optimized fixed-size sliding window updates the sum in  by removing the stale element and adding the new one, achieving  time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longest_k_distinct_chars(std::string s, int k) {\n\tint n = s.length(), max_len = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tstd::vector<int> counts(256, 0);\n\t\t\tint distinct = 0;\n\t\t\tfor (int m = i; m <= j; ++m) {\n\t\t\t\tif (counts[(unsigned char)s[m]]++ == 0) distinct++;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif (distinct <= k) max_len = std::max(max_len, j - i + 1);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_len;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longest_k_distinct_chars(std::string s, int k) {\n\tint n = s.length();\n\tif (k == 0) return 0;\n\tstd::vector<int> counts(256, 0);\n\tint distinct = 0, start = 0, max_len = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tif (counts[(unsigned char)s[end]]++ == 0) distinct++;\n\t\twhile (distinct > k) {\n\t\t\tif (--counts[(unsigned char)s[start++]] == 0) distinct--;\n\t\t}\n\t\tmax_len = std::max(max_len, end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn max_len;\n}\n---\nThe brute-force  approach re-scans every substring for character counts. A variable sliding window with a frequency array tracks distinct characters in  amortized time, resulting in an  linear scan."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nint count_subarrays_sum_le_target(const std::vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n\tint count = 0, n = nums.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tlong long current_sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (current_sum <= target) count++;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nint count_subarrays_sum_le_target(const std::vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint start = 0, count = 0;\n\tlong long current_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[end];\n\t\twhile (current_sum > target && start <= end) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum -= nums[start++];\n\t\t}\n\t\tcount += (end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nThe original  algorithm checks every subarray sum individually. For non-negative inputs, a sliding window can count all valid subarrays ending at 'end' in constant time, reducing the complexity to ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::vector<int> window_mins(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tstd::vector<int> result;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint current_min = nums[i];\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < i + k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_min = std::min(current_min, nums[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t\tresult.push_back(current_min);\n\t}\n\treturn result;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <deque>\n\nstd::vector<int> window_mins(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n == 0) return {};\n\tstd::vector<int> result;\n\tstd::deque<int> dq;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tif (!dq.empty() && dq.front() == i - k) dq.pop_front();\n\t\twhile (!dq.empty() && nums[dq.back()] >= nums[i]) dq.pop_back();\n\t\tdq.push_back(i);\n\t\tif (i >= k - 1) result.push_back(nums[dq.front()]);\n\t}\n\treturn result;\n}\n---\nThe brute-force  approach repeatedly searches for the minimum in each window. A monotonic deque maintains potential minimums in  per element, allowing for a total  linear time complexity."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nint shortest_sub_with_sum_ge(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), res = INT_MAX;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tint s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\ts += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (s >= k) {\n\t\t\t\tres = std::min(res, j - i + 1);\n\t\t\t\tbreak;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (res == INT_MAX) ? 0 : res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nint shortest_sub_with_sum_ge(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint start = 0, sum = 0, res = INT_MAX;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tsum += nums[end];\n\t\twhile (sum >= k) {\n\t\t\tres = std::min(res, end - start + 1);\n\t\t\tsum -= nums[start++];\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (res == INT_MAX) ? 0 : res;\n}\n---\nThe original  code re-evaluates ranges from scratch for every starting index. A variable sliding window approach shrinks from the left as soon as the threshold is met, finding the minimum in  time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <numeric>\n\nstd::vector<double> sliding_avg(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tstd::vector<double> res;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tdouble s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; ++j) s += nums[j];\n\t\tres.push_back(s / k);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nstd::vector<double> sliding_avg(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n < k) return {};\n\tstd::vector<double> res;\n\tdouble current_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) current_sum += nums[i];\n\tres.push_back(current_sum / k);\n\tfor (int i = k; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[i] - nums[i - k];\n\t\tres.push_back(current_sum / k);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe original solution re-sums all  elements for each of the  windows, leading to . The optimized sliding window maintains the sum in  per shift, resulting in  time complexity."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <unordered_set>\n\nint max_unique_substring(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length(), res = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tstd::unordered_set<char> seen;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (seen.count(s[j])) break;\n\t\t\tseen.insert(s[j]);\n\t\t\tres = std::max(res, j - i + 1);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_unique_substring(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length();\n\tstd::vector<int> last_pos(256, -1);\n\tint start = 0, res = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tif (last_pos[(unsigned char)s[end]] >= start) {\n\t\t\tstart = last_pos[(unsigned char)s[end]] + 1;\n\t\t}\n\t\tlast_pos[(unsigned char)s[end]] = end;\n\t\tres = std::max(res, end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe  approach is slow because it checks all possible substrings. The sliding window with a position array finds the longest unique character sequence in  time by shifting the start pointer based on character history."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_subarray_sum_size_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tint max_sum = -1e9;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint current_sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[j];\n\t\t}\n\t\tmax_sum = std::max(max_sum, current_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn max_sum;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_subarray_sum_size_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tint current_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) current_sum += nums[i];\n\tint max_sum = current_sum;\n\tfor (int i = k; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[i] - nums[i - k];\n\t\tmax_sum = std::max(max_sum, current_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn max_sum;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach recalculates the sum of each -size subarray. Using a fixed sliding window reduces this to  by performing one addition and one subtraction per step, which is significantly faster for large ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nlong long count_sub_sum_le(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tlong long count = 0, n = nums.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tlong long current = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcurrent += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (current <= k) count++;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nlong long count_sub_sum_le(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint start = 0;\n\tlong long count = 0, current_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[end];\n\t\twhile (current_sum > k && start <= end) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum -= nums[start++];\n\t\t}\n\t\tcount += (end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nThe original nested loops take  time by checking all subarrays. A sliding window reduces this to  by adjusting the window bounds dynamically, counting valid subarrays in constant time per step."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::vector<int> sliding_window_max(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint cur_max = nums[i];\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < i + k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcur_max = std::max(cur_max, nums[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t\tres.push_back(cur_max);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <deque>\n\nstd::vector<int> sliding_window_max(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n == 0) return {};\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tstd::deque<int> dq;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tif (!dq.empty() && dq.front() == i - k) dq.pop_front();\n\t\twhile (!dq.empty() && nums[dq.back()] <= nums[i]) dq.pop_back();\n\t\tdq.push_back(i);\n\t\tif (i >= k - 1) res.push_back(nums[dq.front()]);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe brute-force  approach repeatedly searches for the max in each window. A monotonic deque ensures the maximum element is always at the front, reducing the complexity to  total time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nint smallest_subarray_sum_ge(const std::vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), res = INT_MAX;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tint s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\ts += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (s >= target) {\n\t\t\t\tres = std::min(res, j - i + 1);\n\t\t\t\tbreak;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (res == INT_MAX) ? 0 : res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nint smallest_subarray_sum_ge(const std::vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint start = 0, sum = 0, res = INT_MAX;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tsum += nums[end];\n\t\twhile (sum >= target) {\n\t\t\tres = std::min(res, end - start + 1);\n\t\t\tsum -= nums[start++];\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (res == INT_MAX) ? 0 : res;\n}\n---\nThe original  algorithm re-sums from every starting point. A variable sliding window approach maintains the sum as elements are added, contracting from the left as needed to achieve  linear time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_sum_k_sub(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), res = -2e9;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint current_sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; ++j) current_sum += nums[j];\n\t\tif (current_sum > res) res = current_sum;\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_sum_k_sub(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tint current_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) current_sum += nums[i];\n\tint res = current_sum;\n\tfor (int i = k; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[i] - nums[i - k];\n\t\tres = std::max(res, current_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe nested  loops re-add elements redundantly. By using a sliding window, we maintain the sum in  at each step by subtracting the leaving element and adding the incoming one, resulting in  time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <unordered_set>\n\nint length_longest_substring_unique(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length(), res = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tstd::unordered_set<char> set;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (set.count(s[j])) break;\n\t\t\tset.insert(s[j]);\n\t\t\tif (j - i + 1 > res) res = j - i + 1;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint length_longest_substring_unique(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length();\n\tstd::vector<int> last_idx(256, -1);\n\tint start = 0, res = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tif (last_idx[(unsigned char)s[end]] >= start) {\n\t\t\tstart = last_idx[(unsigned char)s[end]] + 1;\n\t\t}\n\t\tlast_idx[(unsigned char)s[end]] = end;\n\t\tres = std::max(res, end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach checks all substrings with a hash set. A sliding window with an index array tracks the last seen positions of characters, providing an  time complexity and minimal memory overhead."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nlong long count_subarrays_le_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tlong long count = 0, n = nums.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tlong long current = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcurrent += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (current <= k) count++;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nlong long count_subarrays_le_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint start = 0;\n\tlong long count = 0, sum = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tsum += nums[end];\n\t\twhile (sum > k && start <= end) {\n\t\t\tsum -= nums[start++];\n\t\t}\n\t\tcount += (end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nThe original nested loops take  time by checking all subarrays. A sliding window reduces this to  by adjusting the window bounds dynamically, counting all valid subarrays for each 'end' index in  amortized time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::vector<int> get_window_maxima(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint cur_max = nums[i];\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < i + k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcur_max = std::max(cur_max, nums[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t\tres.push_back(cur_max);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <deque>\n\nstd::vector<int> get_window_maxima(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n == 0) return {};\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tstd::deque<int> dq;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tif (!dq.empty() && dq.front() == i - k) dq.pop_front();\n\t\twhile (!dq.empty() && nums[dq.back()] <= nums[i]) dq.pop_back();\n\t\tdq.push_back(i);\n\t\tif (i >= k - 1) res.push_back(nums[dq.front()]);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach re-scans for the maximum in each sliding window. By using a monotonic deque to maintain potentially maximum elements, we can retrieve the maximum in  and process the entire array in  time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nint min_len_subarray_ge(const std::vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), min_l = INT_MAX;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tint s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\ts += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (s >= target) {\n\t\t\t\tmin_l = std::min(min_l, j - i + 1);\n\t\t\t\tbreak;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (min_l == INT_MAX) ? 0 : min_l;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nint min_len_subarray_ge(const std::vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint start = 0, sum = 0, min_l = INT_MAX;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tsum += nums[end];\n\t\twhile (sum >= target) {\n\t\t\tmin_l = std::min(min_l, end - start + 1);\n\t\t\tsum -= nums[start++];\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (min_l == INT_MAX) ? 0 : min_l;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach checks every subarray starting at each index. A sliding window technique achieves  time complexity by using two pointers to shrink the window once the target sum is met, minimizing the length efficiently."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint find_max_sum_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), res = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; ++j) s += nums[j];\n\t\tif (i == 0 || s > res) res = s;\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint find_max_sum_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tint current_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) current_sum += nums[i];\n\tint res = current_sum;\n\tfor (int i = k; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[i] - nums[i - k];\n\t\tres = std::max(res, current_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe nested  loops re-add elements redundantly. By using a sliding window, we maintain the sum in  at each step by subtracting the leaving element and adding the incoming one, resulting in  time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <unordered_set>\n\nint length_of_longest_no_repeat(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length(), max_len = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tstd::unordered_set<char> seen;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (seen.count(s[j])) break;\n\t\t\tseen.insert(s[j]);\n\t\t\tif (j - i + 1 > max_len) max_len = j - i + 1;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_len;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint length_of_longest_no_repeat(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length();\n\tstd::vector<int> pos(256, -1);\n\tint start = 0, res = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tif (pos[(unsigned char)s[end]] >= start) {\n\t\t\tstart = pos[(unsigned char)s[end]] + 1;\n\t\t}\n\t\tpos[(unsigned char)s[end]] = end;\n\t\tres = std::max(res, end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach is slow for large strings due to nested checks. Using a sliding window with an index array to jump the window's start position optimizes the search to  time and minimizes memory access overhead."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nlong long count_subarrays_lte(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tlong long count = 0, n = nums.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tlong long s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\ts += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (s <= k) count++;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nlong long count_subarrays_lte(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint start = 0;\n\tlong long count = 0, sum = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tsum += nums[end];\n\t\twhile (sum > k && start <= end) {\n\t\t\tsum -= nums[start++];\n\t\t}\n\t\tcount += (end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nThe nested loop  is inefficient for large inputs. A sliding window technique processes the array in  by maintaining a running sum and shifting the start pointer, counting valid subarrays in  amortized time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::vector<int> sliding_max_vals(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint current_max = nums[i];\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < i + k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (nums[j] > current_max) current_max = nums[j];\n\t\t}\n\t\tres.push_back(current_max);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <deque>\n\nstd::vector<int> sliding_max_vals(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n == 0) return {};\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tstd::deque<int> dq;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tif (!dq.empty() && dq.front() == i - k) dq.pop_front();\n\t\twhile (!dq.empty() && nums[dq.back()] <= nums[i]) dq.pop_back();\n\t\tdq.push_back(i);\n\t\tif (i >= k - 1) res.push_back(nums[dq.front()]);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe original code has  complexity due to repeated maximum searches. A monotonic deque keeps track of elements in a way that allows  max retrieval and ensures  total runtime since each element is touched twice."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nint min_subarray_len_sum(const std::vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), res = INT_MAX;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tint sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tsum += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (sum >= target) {\n\t\t\t\tres = std::min(res, j - i + 1);\n\t\t\t\tbreak;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (res == INT_MAX) ? 0 : res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nint min_subarray_len_sum(const std::vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint start = 0, current_sum = 0, res = INT_MAX;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[end];\n\t\twhile (current_sum >= target) {\n\t\t\tres = std::min(res, end - start + 1);\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum -= nums[start++];\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (res == INT_MAX) ? 0 : res;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach checks every subarray starting at each index. A sliding window technique achieves  time complexity by using two pointers to shrink the window once the target sum is met, minimizing the length efficiently."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_sum_k_win(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), res = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; ++j) s += nums[j];\n\t\tif (i == 0 || s > res) res = s;\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_sum_k_win(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tint current_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) current_sum += nums[i];\n\tint res = current_sum;\n\tfor (int i = k; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[i] - nums[i - k];\n\t\tres = std::max(res, current_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe nested  loops re-add elements redundantly. By using a sliding window, we maintain the sum in  at each step by subtracting the leaving element and adding the incoming one, resulting in  time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <unordered_set>\n\nint longest_substr_unique_chars(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length(), res = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tstd::unordered_set<char> set;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (set.count(s[j])) break;\n\t\t\tset.insert(s[j]);\n\t\t\tif (j - i + 1 > res) res = j - i + 1;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longest_substr_unique_chars(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length();\n\tstd::vector<int> last_idx(256, -1);\n\tint start = 0, res = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tif (last_idx[(unsigned char)s[end]] >= start) {\n\t\t\tstart = last_idx[(unsigned char)s[end]] + 1;\n\t\t}\n\t\tlast_idx[(unsigned char)s[end]] = end;\n\t\tres = std::max(res, end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach checks all substrings with a hash set. A sliding window with an index array tracks the last seen positions of characters, providing an  time complexity and minimal memory overhead."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nlong long count_subarrays_sum_target(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tlong long count = 0, n = nums.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tlong long current = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcurrent += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (current <= k) count++;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nlong long count_subarrays_sum_target(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint start = 0;\n\tlong long count = 0, sum = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tsum += nums[end];\n\t\twhile (sum > k && start <= end) {\n\t\t\tsum -= nums[start++];\n\t\t}\n\t\tcount += (end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nThe nested loop  is inefficient for large inputs. A sliding window technique processes the array in  by maintaining a running sum and shifting the start pointer, counting all valid subarrays in  amortized time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::vector<int> get_window_max_values(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint cur_max = nums[i];\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < i + k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcur_max = std::max(cur_max, nums[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t\tres.push_back(cur_max);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <deque>\n\nstd::vector<int> get_window_max_values(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n == 0) return {};\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tstd::deque<int> dq;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tif (!dq.empty() && dq.front() == i - k) dq.pop_front();\n\t\twhile (!dq.empty() && nums[dq.back()] <= nums[i]) dq.pop_back();\n\t\tdq.push_back(i);\n\t\tif (i >= k - 1) res.push_back(nums[dq.front()]);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach re-scans for the maximum in each sliding window. By using a monotonic deque to maintain potentially maximum elements, we can retrieve the maximum in  and process the entire array in  time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nint min_subarray_len_ge_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), res = INT_MAX;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tint s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\ts += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (s >= k) {\n\t\t\t\tres = std::min(res, j - i + 1);\n\t\t\t\tbreak;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (res == INT_MAX) ? 0 : res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nint min_subarray_len_ge_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint start = 0, sum = 0, res = INT_MAX;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tsum += nums[end];\n\t\twhile (sum >= k) {\n\t\t\tres = std::min(res, end - start + 1);\n\t\t\tsum -= nums[start++];\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (res == INT_MAX) ? 0 : res;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach checks every subarray starting at each index. A sliding window technique achieves  time complexity by using two pointers to shrink the window once the target sum is met, minimizing the length efficiently."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_sum_k_subarray(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), res = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; ++j) s += nums[j];\n\t\tif (i == 0 || s > res) res = s;\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_sum_k_subarray(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tint current_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) current_sum += nums[i];\n\tint res = current_sum;\n\tfor (int i = k; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[i] - nums[i - k];\n\t\tres = std::max(res, current_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe nested  loops re-add elements redundantly. By using a sliding window, we maintain the sum in  at each step by subtracting the leaving element and adding the incoming one, resulting in  time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <unordered_set>\n\nint length_longest_substring_no_rep(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length(), res = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tstd::unordered_set<char> set;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (set.count(s[j])) break;\n\t\t\tset.insert(s[j]);\n\t\t\tif (j - i + 1 > res) res = j - i + 1;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint length_longest_substring_no_rep(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length();\n\tstd::vector<int> last_idx(256, -1);\n\tint start = 0, res = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tif (last_idx[(unsigned char)s[end]] >= start) {\n\t\t\tstart = last_idx[(unsigned char)s[end]] + 1;\n\t\t}\n\t\tlast_idx[(unsigned char)s[end]] = end;\n\t\tres = std::max(res, end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach checks all substrings with a hash set. A sliding window with an index array tracks the last seen positions of characters, providing an  time complexity and minimal memory overhead."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nlong long count_subarrays_sum_lte_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tlong long count = 0, n = nums.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tlong long s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\ts += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (s <= k) count++;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nlong long count_subarrays_sum_lte_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint start = 0;\n\tlong long count = 0, sum = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tsum += nums[end];\n\t\twhile (sum > k && start <= end) {\n\t\t\tsum -= nums[start++];\n\t\t}\n\t\tcount += (end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nThe nested loop  is inefficient for large inputs. A sliding window technique processes the array in  by maintaining a running sum and shifting the start pointer, counting all valid subarrays in  amortized time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::vector<int> get_window_max_val(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint cur_max = nums[i];\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < i + k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcur_max = std::max(cur_max, nums[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t\tres.push_back(cur_max);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <deque>\n\nstd::vector<int> get_window_max_val(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n == 0) return {};\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tstd::deque<int> dq;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tif (!dq.empty() && dq.front() == i - k) dq.pop_front();\n\t\twhile (!dq.empty() && nums[dq.back()] <= nums[i]) dq.pop_back();\n\t\tdq.push_back(i);\n\t\tif (i >= k - 1) res.push_back(nums[dq.front()]);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach re-scans for the maximum in each sliding window. By using a monotonic deque to maintain potentially maximum elements, we can retrieve the maximum in  and process the entire array in  time."}
