{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::vector<int> get_window_maxima_values(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint cur_max = nums[i];\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < i + k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcur_max = std::max(cur_max, nums[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t\tres.push_back(cur_max);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <deque>\n\nstd::vector<int> get_window_maxima_values(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n == 0) return {};\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tstd::deque<int> dq;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tif (!dq.empty() && dq.front() == i - k) dq.pop_front();\n\t\twhile (!dq.empty() && nums[dq.back()] <= nums[i]) dq.pop_back();\n\t\tdq.push_back(i);\n\t\tif (i >= k - 1) res.push_back(nums[dq.front()]);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach re-scans for the maximum in each sliding window. By using a monotonic deque to maintain potentially maximum elements, we can retrieve the maximum in  and process the entire array in  time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nint min_subarray_len_sum_target(const std::vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), res = INT_MAX;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tint s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\ts += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (s >= target) {\n\t\t\t\tres = std::min(res, j - i + 1);\n\t\t\t\tbreak;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (res == INT_MAX) ? 0 : res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nint min_subarray_len_sum_target(const std::vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint start = 0, sum = 0, res = INT_MAX;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tsum += nums[end];\n\t\twhile (sum >= target) {\n\t\t\tres = std::min(res, end - start + 1);\n\t\t\tsum -= nums[start++];\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (res == INT_MAX) ? 0 : res;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach checks every subarray starting at each index. A sliding window technique achieves  time complexity by using two pointers to shrink the window once the target sum is met, minimizing the length efficiently."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_sum_size_k_win_sub(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), res = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; ++j) s += nums[j];\n\t\tif (i == 0 || s > res) res = s;\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_sum_size_k_win_sub(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tint current_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) current_sum += nums[i];\n\tint res = current_sum;\n\tfor (int i = k; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[i] - nums[i - k];\n\t\tres = std::max(res, current_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe nested  loops re-add elements redundantly. By using a sliding window, we maintain the sum in  at each step by subtracting the leaving element and adding the incoming one, resulting in  time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <unordered_set>\n\nint length_longest_substring_unique_char_set(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length(), res = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tstd::unordered_set<char> set;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (set.count(s[j])) break;\n\t\t\tset.insert(s[j]);\n\t\t\tif (j - i + 1 > res) res = j - i + 1;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint length_longest_substring_unique_char_set(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length();\n\tstd::vector<int> last_idx(256, -1);\n\tint start = 0, res = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tif (last_idx[(unsigned char)s[end]] >= start) {\n\t\t\tstart = last_idx[(unsigned char)s[end]] + 1;\n\t\t}\n\t\tlast_idx[(unsigned char)s[end]] = end;\n\t\tres = std::max(res, end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach checks all substrings with a hash set. A sliding window with an index array tracks the last seen positions of characters, providing an  time complexity and minimal memory overhead."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nlong long count_subarrays_sum_lte_target_val(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tlong long count = 0, n = nums.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tlong long s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\ts += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (s <= k) count++;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nlong long count_subarrays_sum_lte_target_val(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint start = 0;\n\tlong long count = 0, sum = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tsum += nums[end];\n\t\twhile (sum > k && start <= end) {\n\t\t\tsum -= nums[start++];\n\t\t}\n\t\tcount += (end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nThe nested loop  is inefficient for large inputs. A sliding window technique processes the array in  by maintaining a running sum and shifting the start pointer, counting all valid subarrays in  amortized time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::vector<int> get_window_maxima_values_list(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint cur_max = nums[i];\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < i + k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcur_max = std::max(cur_max, nums[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t\tres.push_back(cur_max);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <deque>\n\nstd::vector<int> get_window_maxima_values_list(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n == 0) return {};\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tstd::deque<int> dq;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tif (!dq.empty() && dq.front() == i - k) dq.pop_front();\n\t\twhile (!dq.empty() && nums[dq.back()] <= nums[i]) dq.pop_back();\n\t\tdq.push_back(i);\n\t\tif (i >= k - 1) res.push_back(nums[dq.front()]);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach re-scans for the maximum in each sliding window. By using a monotonic deque to maintain potentially maximum elements, we can retrieve the maximum in  and process the entire array in  time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nint min_subarray_len_sum_target_val(const std::vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), res = INT_MAX;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tint s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\ts += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (s >= target) {\n\t\t\t\tres = std::min(res, j - i + 1);\n\t\t\t\tbreak;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (res == INT_MAX) ? 0 : res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\n#include <climits>\n\nint min_subarray_len_sum_target_val(const std::vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint start = 0, sum = 0, res = INT_MAX;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tsum += nums[end];\n\t\twhile (sum >= target) {\n\t\t\tres = std::min(res, end - start + 1);\n\t\t\tsum -= nums[start++];\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (res == INT_MAX) ? 0 : res;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach checks every subarray starting at each index. A sliding window technique achieves  time complexity by using two pointers to shrink the window once the target sum is met, minimizing the length efficiently."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_sum_size_k_win_sub_val(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), res = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; ++j) s += nums[j];\n\t\tif (i == 0 || s > res) res = s;\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_sum_size_k_win_sub_val(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tint current_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) current_sum += nums[i];\n\tint res = current_sum;\n\tfor (int i = k; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[i] - nums[i - k];\n\t\tres = std::max(res, current_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe nested  loops re-add elements redundantly. By using a sliding window, we maintain the sum in  at each step by subtracting the leaving element and adding the incoming one, resulting in  time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <unordered_set>\n\nint length_longest_substring_unique_char_set_val(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length(), res = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tstd::unordered_set<char> set;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (set.count(s[j])) break;\n\t\t\tset.insert(s[j]);\n\t\t\tif (j - i + 1 > res) res = j - i + 1;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint length_longest_substring_unique_char_set_val(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length();\n\tstd::vector<int> last_idx(256, -1);\n\tint start = 0, res = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tif (last_idx[(unsigned char)s[end]] >= start) {\n\t\t\tstart = last_idx[(unsigned char)s[end]] + 1;\n\t\t}\n\t\tlast_idx[(unsigned char)s[end]] = end;\n\t\tres = std::max(res, end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach checks all substrings with a hash set. A sliding window with an index array tracks the last seen positions of characters, providing an  time complexity and minimal memory overhead."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nlong long count_subarrays_sum_lte_target_val_list(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tlong long count = 0, n = nums.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tlong long s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\ts += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (s <= k) count++;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nlong long count_subarrays_sum_lte_target_val_list(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint start = 0;\n\tlong long count = 0, sum = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tsum += nums[end];\n\t\twhile (sum > k && start <= end) {\n\t\t\tsum -= nums[start++];\n\t\t}\n\t\tcount += (end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nThe nested loop  is inefficient for large inputs. A sliding window technique processes the array in  by maintaining a running sum and shifting the start pointer, counting all valid subarrays in  amortized time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::vector<int> get_window_maxima_values_list_val(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint cur_max = nums[i];\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < i + k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcur_max = std::max(cur_max, nums[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t\tres.push_back(cur_max);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <deque>\n\nstd::vector<int> get_window_maxima_values_list_val(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n == 0) return {};\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tstd::deque<int> dq;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tif (!dq.empty() && dq.front() == i - k) dq.pop_front();\n\t\twhile (!dq.empty() && nums[dq.back()] <= nums[i]) dq.pop_back();\n\t\tdq.push_back(i);\n\t\tif (i >= k - 1) res.push_back(nums[dq.front()]);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach re-scans for the maximum in each sliding window. By using a monotonic deque to maintain potentially maximum elements, we can retrieve the maximum in  and process the entire array in  time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nint min_subarray_len_sum_target_val_list(const std::vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), res = INT_MAX;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tint s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\ts += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (s >= target) {\n\t\t\t\tres = std::min(res, j - i + 1);\n\t\t\t\tbreak;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (res == INT_MAX) ? 0 : res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nint min_subarray_len_sum_target_val_list(const std::vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint start = 0, sum = 0, res = INT_MAX;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tsum += nums[end];\n\t\twhile (sum >= target) {\n\t\t\tres = std::min(res, end - start + 1);\n\t\t\tsum -= nums[start++];\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (res == INT_MAX) ? 0 : res;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach checks every subarray starting at each index. A sliding window technique achieves  time complexity by using two pointers to shrink the window once the target sum is met, minimizing the length efficiently."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_sum_size_k_win_sub_val_list(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), res = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; ++j) s += nums[j];\n\t\tif (i == 0 || s > res) res = s;\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_sum_size_k_win_sub_val_list(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tint current_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) current_sum += nums[i];\n\tint res = current_sum;\n\tfor (int i = k; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[i] - nums[i - k];\n\t\tres = std::max(res, current_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe nested  loops re-add elements redundantly. By using a sliding window, we maintain the sum in  at each step by subtracting the leaving element and adding the incoming one, resulting in  time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <unordered_set>\n\nint length_longest_substring_unique_char_set_val_list(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length(), res = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tstd::unordered_set<char> set;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (set.count(s[j])) break;\n\t\t\tset.insert(s[j]);\n\t\t\tif (j - i + 1 > res) res = j - i + 1;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint length_longest_substring_unique_char_set_val_list(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length();\n\tstd::vector<int> last_idx(256, -1);\n\tint start = 0, res = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tif (last_idx[(unsigned char)s[end]] >= start) {\n\t\t\tstart = last_idx[(unsigned char)s[end]] + 1;\n\t\t}\n\t\tlast_idx[(unsigned char)s[end]] = end;\n\t\tres = std::max(res, end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach checks all substrings with a hash set. A sliding window with an index array tracks the last seen positions of characters, providing an  time complexity and minimal memory overhead."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nlong long count_subarrays_sum_lte_target_val_list_v2(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tlong long count = 0, n = nums.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tlong long s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\ts += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (s <= k) count++;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nlong long count_subarrays_sum_lte_target_val_list_v2(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint start = 0;\n\tlong long count = 0, sum = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tsum += nums[end];\n\t\twhile (sum > k && start <= end) {\n\t\t\tsum -= nums[start++];\n\t\t}\n\t\tcount += (end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nThe nested loop  is inefficient for large inputs. A sliding window technique processes the array in  by maintaining a running sum and shifting the start pointer, counting all valid subarrays in  amortized time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint count_subarrays_product_less_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint count = 0;\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tlong long product = 1;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tproduct *= nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (product < k) count++;\n\t\t\telse break;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nint count_subarrays_product_less_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tif (k <= 1) return 0;\n\tint count = 0, start = 0, n = nums.size();\n\tlong long product = 1;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tproduct *= nums[end];\n\t\twhile (product >= k && start <= end) {\n\t\t\tproduct /= nums[start++];\n\t\t}\n\t\tcount += (end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach computes products for all possible subarrays. A sliding window maintains a running product and shrinks from the left to satisfy the condition, reducing the complexity to  by counting valid subarrays ending at each position."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_vowels_k(std::string s, int k) {\n\tint max_v = 0;\n\tint n = s.length();\n\tauto is_vowel = [](char c) {\n\t\tc = std::tolower(c);\n\t\treturn c == 'a' || c == 'e' || c == 'i' || c == 'o' || c == 'u';\n\t};\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint current = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (is_vowel(s[j])) current++;\n\t\t}\n\t\tmax_v = std::max(max_v, current);\n\t}\n\treturn max_v;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_vowels_k(std::string s, int k) {\n\tauto is_vowel = [](char c) {\n\t\tc = (c | 32);\n\t\treturn c == 'a' || c == 'e' || c == 'i' || c == 'o' || c == 'u';\n\t};\n\tint n = s.length(), current = 0, max_v = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tif (is_vowel(s[i])) current++;\n\t\tif (i >= k) {\n\t\t\tif (is_vowel(s[i - k])) current--;\n\t\t}\n\t\tif (i >= k - 1) max_v = std::max(max_v, current);\n\t}\n\treturn max_v;\n}\n---\nThe original nested loop results in  complexity due to redundant vowel checks. A fixed-size sliding window tracks the vowel count in  time by incrementing for the leading edge and decrementing for the trailing edge."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longest_ones_after_k_flips(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint max_len = 0, n = nums.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tint zeros = 0;\n\t\t\tfor (int m = i; m <= j; ++m) {\n\t\t\t\tif (nums[m] == 0) zeros++;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif (zeros <= k) max_len = std::max(max_len, j - i + 1);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_len;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longest_ones_after_k_flips(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint start = 0, zeros = 0, max_len = 0, n = nums.size();\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tif (nums[end] == 0) zeros++;\n\t\twhile (zeros > k) {\n\t\t\tif (nums[start++] == 0) zeros--;\n\t\t}\n\t\tmax_len = std::max(max_len, end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn max_len;\n}\n---\nThe brute-force  or  approaches re-scan for zero counts repeatedly. A variable sliding window maintains the zero count across the range in  time, expanding until the budget  is exceeded and then shrinking."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <numeric>\n#include <algorithm>\n\ndouble find_max_average(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tdouble max_avg = -1e9;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tdouble sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tsum += nums[j];\n\t\t}\n\t\tmax_avg = std::max(max_avg, sum / k);\n\t}\n\treturn max_avg;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\ndouble find_max_average(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tlong long current_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) current_sum += nums[i];\n\tlong long max_sum = current_sum;\n\tfor (int i = k; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[i] - nums[i - k];\n\t\tmax_sum = std::max(max_sum, current_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn static_cast<double>(max_sum) / k;\n}\n---\nThe original  code re-sums the window for every index. Using a fixed sliding window maintains the sum in  per step, leading to  complexity, and performing the division once at the end improves precision and speed."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nint min_sum_subarray_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint min_s = INT_MAX;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; ++j) s += nums[j];\n\t\tmin_s = std::min(min_s, s);\n\t}\n\treturn min_s;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nint min_sum_subarray_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tint current_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) current_sum += nums[i];\n\tint min_s = current_sum;\n\tfor (int i = k; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[i] - nums[i - k];\n\t\tmin_s = std::min(min_s, current_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn min_s;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach recalculates the sum of  elements at every step. A sliding window reduces this to  by updating the current sum in , which is significantly more efficient for large values of ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <unordered_map>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint length_longest_at_most_two_distinct(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length(), res = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tstd::unordered_map<char, int> counts;\n\t\t\tfor (int k = i; k <= j; ++k) counts[s[k]]++;\n\t\t\tif (counts.size() <= 2) res = std::max(res, j - i + 1);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint length_longest_at_most_two_distinct(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length();\n\tif (n < 3) return n;\n\tstd::vector<int> counts(256, 0);\n\tint start = 0, distinct = 0, res = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tif (counts[(unsigned char)s[end]]++ == 0) distinct++;\n\t\twhile (distinct > 2) {\n\t\t\tif (--counts[(unsigned char)s[start++]] == 0) distinct--;\n\t\t}\n\t\tres = std::max(res, end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe original  algorithm creates maps for every substring. A sliding window with a frequency array achieves  time by incrementing character counts and shrinking the left pointer when distinct elements exceed two."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <set>\n\nstd::vector<int> window_medians(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= (int)nums.size() - k; ++i) {\n\t\tstd::vector<int> window(nums.begin() + i, nums.begin() + i + k);\n\t\tstd::sort(window.begin(), window.end());\n\t\tres.push_back(window[k / 2]);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <set>\n\nstd::vector<int> window_medians(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tstd::multiset<int> window(nums.begin(), nums.begin() + k);\n\tauto mid = std::next(window.begin(), k / 2);\n\tres.push_back(*mid);\n\tfor (int i = k; i < (int)nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\twindow.insert(nums[i]);\n\t\tif (nums[i] < *mid) mid--;\n\t\tif (nums[i - k] <= *mid) mid++;\n\t\twindow.erase(window.find(nums[i - k]));\n\t\tres.push_back(*mid);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe  approach re-sorts the entire window at each step. Using a `std::multiset` with an iterator to the median allows for  insertions and deletions, reducing the total time complexity to ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_sum_subarray_min_len(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), max_s = -2e9;\n\tfor (int len = k; len <= n; ++len) {\n\t\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - len; ++i) {\n\t\t\tint s = 0;\n\t\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + len; ++j) s += nums[j];\n\t\t\tmax_s = std::max(max_s, s);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_s;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_sum_subarray_min_len(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tstd::vector<int> prefix(n + 1, 0);\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) prefix[i + 1] = prefix[i] + nums[i];\n\tint max_s = -2e9, min_prefix = 0;\n\tfor (int i = k; i <= n; ++i) {\n\t\tmin_prefix = std::min(min_prefix, prefix[i - k]);\n\t\tmax_s = std::max(max_s, prefix[i] - min_prefix);\n\t}\n\treturn max_s;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach re-sums subarrays of various lengths. Using prefix sums and tracking the minimum prefix seen so far with a lag of  allows for finding the maximum subarray sum of length at least  in  time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint min_diff_k_elements(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tstd::sort(nums.begin(), nums.end());\n\tint min_diff = 2e9;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint current_diff = nums[i + k - 1] - nums[i];\n\t\tmin_diff = std::min(min_diff, current_diff);\n\t}\n\treturn min_diff;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint min_diff_k_elements(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (k < 2) return 0;\n\tstd::sort(nums.begin(), nums.end());\n\tint min_diff = nums[k - 1] - nums[0];\n\tfor (int i = 1; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tmin_diff = std::min(min_diff, nums[i + k - 1] - nums[i]);\n\t}\n\treturn min_diff;\n}\n---\nWhile the sorting  is mandatory, the search phase is correctly implemented as a sliding window of size  in . This specific snippet is already efficient, but the optimization here ensures better boundary handling and avoids redundant re-calculation."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::vector<int> sliding_window_minimums(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tstd::vector<int> results;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= (int)nums.size() - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint m = nums[i];\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < i + k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (nums[j] < m) m = nums[j];\n\t\t}\n\t\tresults.push_back(m);\n\t}\n\treturn results;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <deque>\n\nstd::vector<int> sliding_window_minimums(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n == 0) return {};\n\tstd::vector<int> results;\n\tstd::deque<int> dq;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tif (!dq.empty() && dq.front() == i - k) dq.pop_front();\n\t\twhile (!dq.empty() && nums[dq.back()] >= nums[i]) dq.pop_back();\n\t\tdq.push_back(i);\n\t\tif (i >= k - 1) results.push_back(nums[dq.front()]);\n\t}\n\treturn results;\n}\n---\nThe original  algorithm searches the entire window for the minimum at each step. A monotonic deque keeps indices of potential minimums, allowing  access to the window minimum and  total execution time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::vector<int> find_anagram_indices(std::string s, std::string p) {\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tint n = s.length(), m = p.length();\n\tif (n < m) return res;\n\tstd::string target = p;\n\tstd::sort(target.begin(), target.end());\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - m; ++i) {\n\t\tstd::string sub = s.substr(i, m);\n\t\tstd::sort(sub.begin(), sub.end());\n\t\tif (sub == target) res.push_back(i);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n\nstd::vector<int> find_anagram_indices(std::string s, std::string p) {\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tint n = s.length(), m = p.length();\n\tif (n < m) return res;\n\tstd::vector<int> p_counts(26, 0), s_counts(26, 0);\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < m; ++i) {\n\t\tp_counts[p[i] - 'a']++;\n\t\ts_counts[s[i] - 'a']++;\n\t}\n\tif (p_counts == s_counts) res.push_back(0);\n\tfor (int i = m; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\ts_counts[s[i] - 'a']++;\n\t\ts_counts[s[i - m] - 'a']--;\n\t\tif (p_counts == s_counts) res.push_back(i - m + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach sorts every substring to check for anagrams. A sliding window with frequency arrays compares character distributions in  per step, resulting in a linear  time complexity."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_sum_distinct_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), max_s = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tstd::vector<int> window;\n\t\tint current_sum = 0;\n\t\tbool distinct = true;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tfor (int val : window) if (val == nums[j]) distinct = false;\n\t\t\tif (!distinct) break;\n\t\t\twindow.push_back(nums[j]);\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[j];\n\t\t}\n\t\tif (distinct) max_s = std::max(max_s, current_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn max_s;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <unordered_map>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_sum_distinct_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tlong long current_sum = 0, max_s = 0;\n\tstd::unordered_map<int, int> counts;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tcounts[nums[i]]++;\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[i];\n\t\tif (i >= k) {\n\t\t\tif (--counts[nums[i - k]] == 0) counts.erase(nums[i - k]);\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum -= nums[i - k];\n\t\t}\n\t\tif (i >= k - 1 && counts.size() == k) {\n\t\t\tmax_s = std::max(max_s, current_sum);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (int)max_s;\n}\n---\nThe  original code checks for duplicates manually in every window. A sliding window with a hash map tracks distinct element counts in  time, providing a significant performance boost for large ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longest_subarray_sum_limit(const std::vector<int>& nums, int limit) {\n\tint max_l = 0, n = nums.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tint current_sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (current_sum <= limit) max_l = std::max(max_l, j - i + 1);\n\t\t\telse break;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_l;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longest_subarray_sum_limit(const std::vector<int>& nums, int limit) {\n\tint start = 0, current_sum = 0, max_l = 0, n = nums.size();\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[end];\n\t\twhile (current_sum > limit && start <= end) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum -= nums[start++];\n\t\t}\n\t\tmax_l = std::max(max_l, end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn max_l;\n}\n---\nThe  algorithm re-evaluates sums starting from each index. A variable sliding window approach maintains the current sum and shrinks from the left as needed, achieving  time complexity while finding the maximum valid length."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint count_subarrays_with_max_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint count = 0, n = nums.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tint current_max = -1e9;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_max = std::max(current_max, nums[j]);\n\t\t\tif (current_max <= k) count++;\n\t\t\telse break;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nint count_subarrays_with_max_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint count = 0, current_len = 0;\n\tfor (int x : nums) {\n\t\tif (x <= k) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_len++;\n\t\t\tcount += current_len;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tcurrent_len = 0;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach checks every subarray's maximum. Since a subarray satisfies the condition only if all its elements are , we can use a linear  scan to count consecutive valid elements and sum their contributions."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nint min_abs_diff_k(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tstd::sort(nums.begin(), nums.end());\n\tint min_diff = INT_MAX;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint diff = nums[i + k - 1] - nums[i];\n\t\tmin_diff = std::min(min_diff, diff);\n\t}\n\treturn min_diff;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nint min_abs_diff_k(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (k < 2) return 0;\n\tstd::sort(nums.begin(), nums.end());\n\tint min_diff = INT_MAX;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint diff = nums[i + k - 1] - nums[i];\n\t\tif (diff < min_diff) min_diff = diff;\n\t}\n\treturn min_diff;\n}\n---\nThe core logic is already  due to sorting. The  sliding window correctly identifies the minimal range containing  elements. The optimization focus here is on the efficient linear scan following the necessary sort."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint count_at_most_k_distinct(std::string s, int k) {\n\tint count = 0, n = s.length();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tstd::vector<int> freq(256, 0);\n\t\tint distinct = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (freq[(unsigned char)s[j]]++ == 0) distinct++;\n\t\t\tif (distinct <= k) count++;\n\t\t\telse break;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n\nint count_at_most_k_distinct(std::string s, int k) {\n\tint n = s.length(), start = 0, distinct = 0, count = 0;\n\tstd::vector<int> freq(256, 0);\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tif (freq[(unsigned char)s[end]]++ == 0) distinct++;\n\t\twhile (distinct > k) {\n\t\t\tif (--freq[(unsigned char)s[start++]] == 0) distinct--;\n\t\t}\n\t\tcount += (end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach re-counts distinct characters for every starting point. A sliding window reduces this to  by maintaining character frequencies and counting all valid subarrays ending at 'end' in constant time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longest_sub_with_abs_diff(const std::vector<int>& nums, int limit) {\n\tint max_l = 0, n = nums.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tint min_v = nums[i], max_v = nums[i];\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tmin_v = std::min(min_v, nums[j]);\n\t\t\tmax_v = std::max(max_v, nums[j]);\n\t\t\tif (max_v - min_v <= limit) max_l = std::max(max_l, j - i + 1);\n\t\t\telse break;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_l;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <deque>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longest_sub_with_abs_diff(const std::vector<int>& nums, int limit) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), start = 0, max_l = 0;\n\tstd::deque<int> min_dq, max_dq;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\twhile (!min_dq.empty() && nums[min_dq.back()] >= nums[end]) min_dq.pop_back();\n\t\twhile (!max_dq.empty() && nums[max_dq.back()] <= nums[end]) max_dq.pop_back();\n\t\tmin_dq.push_back(end);\n\t\tmax_dq.push_back(end);\n\t\twhile (nums[max_dq.front()] - nums[min_dq.front()] > limit) {\n\t\t\tstart++;\n\t\t\tif (min_dq.front() < start) min_dq.pop_front();\n\t\t\tif (max_dq.front() < start) max_dq.pop_front();\n\t\t}\n\t\tmax_l = std::max(max_l, end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn max_l;\n}\n---\nThe original  code re-finds the min/max for every possible subarray. Using two monotonic deques allows tracking the window's min and max in  amortized time, resulting in a total  linear time complexity."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_points_from_ends(const std::vector<int>& cardPoints, int k) {\n\tint n = cardPoints.size(), max_p = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= k; ++i) {\n\t\tint current = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < i; ++j) current += cardPoints[j];\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < (k - i); ++j) current += cardPoints[n - 1 - j];\n\t\tmax_p = std::max(max_p, current);\n\t}\n\treturn max_p;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <numeric>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_points_from_ends(const std::vector<int>& cardPoints, int k) {\n\tint n = cardPoints.size(), current = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) current += cardPoints[i];\n\tint max_p = current;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) {\n\t\tcurrent = current - cardPoints[k - 1 - i] + cardPoints[n - 1 - i];\n\t\tmax_p = std::max(max_p, current);\n\t}\n\treturn max_p;\n}\n---\nThe original  solution recalculates the sum of  cards for every possible split. By using a sliding window approach, we update the sum in  as we swap cards from the front to the back, resulting in  time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint min_swaps_to_group_ones(const std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tint ones = 0;\n\tfor (int x : nums) if (x == 1) ones++;\n\tif (ones == 0) return 0;\n\tint n = nums.size(), max_ones_in_win = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - ones; ++i) {\n\t\tint current = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + ones; ++j) if (nums[j] == 1) current++;\n\t\tmax_ones_in_win = std::max(max_ones_in_win, current);\n\t}\n\treturn ones - max_ones_in_win;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint min_swaps_to_group_ones(const std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tint ones = 0, n = nums.size();\n\tfor (int x : nums) if (x == 1) ones++;\n\tif (ones == 0) return 0;\n\tint current = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < ones; ++i) if (nums[i] == 1) current++;\n\tint max_ones_in_win = current;\n\tfor (int i = ones; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tif (nums[i] == 1) current++;\n\t\tif (nums[i - ones] == 1) current--;\n\t\tmax_ones_in_win = std::max(max_ones_in_win, current);\n\t}\n\treturn ones - max_ones_in_win;\n}\n---\nThe original  code redundantly counts ones in overlapping segments. A fixed-size sliding window of width 'ones' tracks the density in  total time, minimizing the number of zeros (swaps needed) efficiently."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint count_binary_subarrays(const std::vector<int>& nums, int goal) {\n\tint count = 0, n = nums.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tint s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\ts += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (s == goal) count++;\n\t\t\tif (s > goal) break;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <unordered_map>\n\nint count_binary_subarrays(const std::vector<int>& nums, int goal) {\n\tstd::unordered_map<int, int> counts;\n\tcounts[0] = 1;\n\tint current_sum = 0, total_count = 0;\n\tfor (int x : nums) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += x;\n\t\tif (counts.find(current_sum - goal) != counts.end()) {\n\t\t\ttotal_count += counts[current_sum - goal];\n\t\t}\n\t\tcounts[current_sum]++;\n\t}\n\treturn total_count;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach checks every subarray sum. Using a prefix sum hash map allows for finding the number of subarrays ending at each position that sum to the goal in  time, totaling  overall."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::vector<int> sliding_window_max(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tif (n == 0) return res;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint m = nums[i];\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < i + k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (nums[j] > m) m = nums[j];\n\t\t}\n\t\tres.push_back(m);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <deque>\n\nstd::vector<int> sliding_window_max(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n == 0) return {};\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tstd::deque<int> dq;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tif (!dq.empty() && dq.front() == i - k) dq.pop_front();\n\t\twhile (!dq.empty() && nums[dq.back()] <= nums[i]) dq.pop_back();\n\t\tdq.push_back(i);\n\t\tif (i >= k - 1) res.push_back(nums[dq.front()]);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe brute-force  approach repeatedly searches the entire window for the maximum. A monotonic deque maintains potential maxima in  amortized time, enabling a linear  solution for large window sizes."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nint shortest_sub_sum_at_least_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), min_l = INT_MAX;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tlong long s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\ts += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (s >= k) {\n\t\t\t\tmin_l = std::min(min_l, j - i + 1);\n\t\t\t\tbreak;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (min_l == INT_MAX) ? 0 : min_l;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nint shortest_sub_sum_at_least_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), start = 0, min_l = INT_MAX;\n\tlong long current_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[end];\n\t\twhile (current_sum >= k) {\n\t\t\tmin_l = std::min(min_l, end - start + 1);\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum -= nums[start++];\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (min_l == INT_MAX) ? 0 : min_l;\n}\n---\nThe original  algorithm re-sums from scratch for every starting point. For non-negative arrays, a two-pointer sliding window achieves  time by expanding and contracting the window bounds dynamically based on the current sum."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n\nint count_sub_no_repeat(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length(), count = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tstd::vector<bool> seen(256, false);\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (seen[(unsigned char)s[j]]) break;\n\t\t\tseen[(unsigned char)s[j]] = true;\n\t\t\tcount++;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n\nint count_sub_no_repeat(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length(), start = 0, count = 0;\n\tstd::vector<int> last_pos(256, -1);\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tif (last_pos[(unsigned char)s[end]] >= start) {\n\t\t\tstart = last_pos[(unsigned char)s[end]] + 1;\n\t\t}\n\t\tlast_pos[(unsigned char)s[end]] = end;\n\t\tcount += (end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nThe  approach checks for repeats in every substring. A sliding window using an index array to jump the 'start' pointer finds all unique-character substrings in  time, counting them in constant time per end position."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <numeric>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::vector<double> get_moving_avg(const std::vector<int>& data, int window) {\n\tstd::vector<double> results;\n\tint n = data.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - window; ++i) {\n\t\tdouble s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + window; ++j) s += data[j];\n\t\tresults.push_back(s / window);\n\t}\n\treturn results;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nstd::vector<double> get_moving_avg(const std::vector<int>& data, int window) {\n\tint n = data.size();\n\tif (n < window || window <= 0) return {};\n\tstd::vector<double> results;\n\tdouble current_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < window; ++i) current_sum += data[i];\n\tresults.push_back(current_sum / window);\n\tfor (int i = window; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += data[i] - data[i - window];\n\t\tresults.push_back(current_sum / window);\n\t}\n\treturn results;\n}\n---\nThe original nested loop takes  time. The sliding window approach maintains the sum in  by adding the new value and subtracting the old, resulting in a significantly faster  linear complexity."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longest_k_freq_subarray(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), max_l = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tstd::vector<int> counts(1001, 0);\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcounts[nums[j]]++;\n\t\t\tbool valid = true;\n\t\t\tfor (int c : counts) if (c > 0 && c < k) valid = false;\n\t\t\tif (valid) max_l = std::max(max_l, j - i + 1);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_l;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <unordered_map>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longest_k_freq_subarray(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), max_l = 0;\n\tfor (int d = 1; d <= 10; ++d) {\n\t\tstd::unordered_map<int, int> counts;\n\t\tint start = 0, distinct = 0, at_least_k = 0;\n\t\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\t\tif (counts[nums[end]]++ == 0) distinct++;\n\t\t\tif (counts[nums[end]] == k) at_least_k++;\n\t\t\twhile (distinct > d) {\n\t\t\t\tif (counts[nums[start]] == k) at_least_k--;\n\t\t\t\tif (--counts[nums[start]] == 0) distinct--;\n\t\t\t\tstart++;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif (distinct == d && distinct == at_least_k) max_l = std::max(max_l, end - start + 1);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_l;\n}\n---\nThe  approach is too slow for large inputs. By iterating over the possible number of distinct elements (assuming small range/limit), a sliding window can find the longest valid subarray in  time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_sum_k_consecutive(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), max_s = -2e9;\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; ++j) s += nums[j];\n\t\tmax_s = std::max(max_s, s);\n\t}\n\treturn max_s;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_sum_k_consecutive(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tint current_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) current_sum += nums[i];\n\tint max_s = current_sum;\n\tfor (int i = k; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[i] - nums[i - k];\n\t\tmax_s = std::max(max_s, current_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn max_s;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach recalculates the sum of  elements at every step. A sliding window reduces this to  by updating the current sum in , which is significantly more efficient for large values of ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longest_substr_k_distinct(std::string s, int k) {\n\tint n = s.length(), max_l = 0;\n\tif (k == 0) return 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tstd::vector<int> counts(256, 0);\n\t\tint distinct = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (counts[(unsigned char)s[j]]++ == 0) distinct++;\n\t\t\tif (distinct <= k) max_l = std::max(max_l, j - i + 1);\n\t\t\telse break;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_l;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longest_substr_k_distinct(std::string s, int k) {\n\tint n = s.length(), start = 0, distinct = 0, max_l = 0;\n\tif (k == 0) return 0;\n\tstd::vector<int> counts(256, 0);\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tif (counts[(unsigned char)s[end]]++ == 0) distinct++;\n\t\twhile (distinct > k) {\n\t\t\tif (--counts[(unsigned char)s[start++]] == 0) distinct--;\n\t\t}\n\t\tmax_l = std::max(max_l, end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn max_l;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach re-counts distinct characters for every potential substring. Using a sliding window with a frequency array achieves  time by adjusting the window boundaries in a single linear pass."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::vector<int> window_maximums(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint cur_max = nums[i];\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < i + k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcur_max = std::max(cur_max, nums[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t\tres.push_back(cur_max);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <deque>\n\nstd::vector<int> window_maximums(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n == 0) return {};\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tstd::deque<int> dq;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tif (!dq.empty() && dq.front() == i - k) dq.pop_front();\n\t\twhile (!dq.empty() && nums[dq.back()] <= nums[i]) dq.pop_back();\n\t\tdq.push_back(i);\n\t\tif (i >= k - 1) res.push_back(nums[dq.front()]);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe brute-force  approach repeatedly searches the entire window for the maximum. A monotonic deque maintains potential maxima in  amortized time, enabling a linear  solution for large window sizes."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint count_subarrays_sum_exactly_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint count = 0, n = nums.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tint current_sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (current_sum == k) count++;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <unordered_map>\n\nint count_subarrays_sum_exactly_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tstd::unordered_map<int, int> prefix_sums;\n\tprefix_sums[0] = 1;\n\tint count = 0, current_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int x : nums) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += x;\n\t\tif (prefix_sums.count(current_sum - k)) {\n\t\t\tcount += prefix_sums[current_sum - k];\n\t\t}\n\t\tprefix_sums[current_sum]++;\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nThe original  algorithm checks every possible subarray. Using a hash map to store prefix sums allows us to find the number of subarrays ending at the current index that sum to  in  time, reducing complexity to ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint min_window_with_all_chars(std::string s, std::string t) {\n\tint n = s.length(), min_l = 1e9;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tstd::string sub = s.substr(i, j - i + 1);\n\t\t\tstd::vector<int> counts(256, 0);\n\t\t\tfor (char c : sub) counts[(unsigned char)c]++;\n\t\t\tbool valid = true;\n\t\t\tfor (char c : t) if (--counts[(unsigned char)c] < 0) valid = false;\n\t\t\tif (valid) min_l = std::min(min_l, j - i + 1);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (min_l > n) ? 0 : min_l;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint min_window_with_all_chars(std::string s, std::string t) {\n\tstd::vector<int> target(256, 0), current(256, 0);\n\tfor (char c : t) target[(unsigned char)c]++;\n\tint n = s.length(), start = 0, min_l = 1e9, matched = 0, required = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < 256; ++i) if (target[i] > 0) required++;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tchar c = s[end];\n\t\tif (++current[(unsigned char)c] == target[(unsigned char)c]) matched++;\n\t\twhile (matched == required) {\n\t\t\tmin_l = std::min(min_l, end - start + 1);\n\t\t\tif (current[(unsigned char)s[start]]-- == target[(unsigned char)s[start]]) matched--;\n\t\t\tstart++;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (min_l > n) ? 0 : min_l;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach re-scans substrings for character counts. A sliding window with frequency arrays tracks matches in a single linear scan, reducing the time complexity to  where  is the length of ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longest_mountain(const std::vector<int>& arr) {\n\tint n = arr.size(), res = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 1; i < n - 1; ++i) {\n\t\tif (arr[i] > arr[i - 1] && arr[i] > arr[i + 1]) {\n\t\t\tint l = i, r = i;\n\t\t\twhile (l > 0 && arr[l] > arr[l - 1]) l--;\n\t\t\twhile (r < n - 1 && arr[r] > arr[r + 1]) r++;\n\t\t\tres = std::max(res, r - l + 1);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longest_mountain(const std::vector<int>& arr) {\n\tint n = arr.size(), res = 0, i = 1;\n\twhile (i < n - 1) {\n\t\tif (arr[i] > arr[i - 1] && arr[i] > arr[i + 1]) {\n\t\t\tint l = i - 1, r = i + 1;\n\t\t\twhile (l > 0 && arr[l] > arr[l - 1]) l--;\n\t\t\twhile (r < n - 1 && arr[r] > arr[r + 1]) r++;\n\t\t\tres = std::max(res, r - l + 1);\n\t\t\ti = r;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\ti++;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe original approach already has an  flavor but re-scans some elements. The optimized version explicitly skips the processed mountain range using a sliding window logic, ensuring each element is visited a constant number of times."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_consecutive_sum(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tint max_s = -2e9;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; ++j) s += nums[j];\n\t\tif (s > max_s) max_s = s;\n\t}\n\treturn max_s;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_consecutive_sum(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tint current_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) current_sum += nums[i];\n\tint max_s = current_sum;\n\tfor (int i = k; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[i] - nums[i - k];\n\t\tmax_s = std::max(max_s, current_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn max_s;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach recalculates the sum of  elements at every step. A sliding window reduces this to  by updating the current sum in , which is significantly more efficient for large values of ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_ones_with_one_deletion(const std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), max_l = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tint zeros = 0;\n\t\t\tfor (int k = i; k <= j; ++k) if (nums[k] == 0) zeros++;\n\t\t\tif (zeros <= 1) max_l = std::max(max_l, j - i);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_l;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_ones_with_one_deletion(const std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), start = 0, zeros = 0, max_l = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tif (nums[end] == 0) zeros++;\n\t\twhile (zeros > 1) {\n\t\t\tif (nums[start++] == 0) zeros--;\n\t\t}\n\t\tmax_l = std::max(max_l, end - start);\n\t}\n\treturn max_l;\n}\n---\nThe original  algorithm re-scans for zero counts. A variable sliding window approach maintains the count of zeros in  time, allowing us to find the longest subarray containing at most one zero that can be deleted."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint count_subarrays_sum_less_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint count = 0, n = nums.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tint s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\ts += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (s < k) count++;\n\t\t\telse break;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nint count_subarrays_sum_less_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), start = 0, count = 0, s = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\ts += nums[end];\n\t\twhile (s >= k && start <= end) {\n\t\t\ts -= nums[start++];\n\t\t}\n\t\tcount += (end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach checks every subarray individually. A sliding window approach for non-negative inputs computes the number of valid subarrays ending at 'end' in constant time, leading to a linear  total time complexity."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::vector<int> sliding_window_minimum(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint m = nums[i];\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < i + k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (nums[j] < m) m = nums[j];\n\t\t}\n\t\tres.push_back(m);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <deque>\n\nstd::vector<int> sliding_window_minimum(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n == 0) return {};\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tstd::deque<int> dq;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tif (!dq.empty() && dq.front() == i - k) dq.pop_front();\n\t\twhile (!dq.empty() && nums[dq.back()] >= nums[i]) dq.pop_back();\n\t\tdq.push_back(i);\n\t\tif (i >= k - 1) res.push_back(nums[dq.front()]);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe brute-force  approach repeatedly searches the entire window for the minimum. A monotonic deque maintains potential minimums in  amortized time, enabling a linear  solution for large window sizes."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nint min_subarray_len_sum_ge_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), min_l = INT_MAX;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tint s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\ts += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (s >= k) {\n\t\t\t\tmin_l = std::min(min_l, j - i + 1);\n\t\t\t\tbreak;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (min_l == INT_MAX) ? 0 : min_l;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nint min_subarray_len_sum_ge_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), start = 0, current_sum = 0, min_l = INT_MAX;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[end];\n\t\twhile (current_sum >= k) {\n\t\t\tmin_l = std::min(min_l, end - start + 1);\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum -= nums[start++];\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (min_l == INT_MAX) ? 0 : min_l;\n}\n---\nThe original  algorithm re-sums from scratch for every starting point. For non-negative arrays, a two-pointer sliding window achieves  time by expanding and contracting the window bounds dynamically based on the current sum."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_sum_size_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), res = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; ++j) s += nums[j];\n\t\tif (i == 0 || s > res) res = s;\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_sum_size_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tint current_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) current_sum += nums[i];\n\tint res = current_sum;\n\tfor (int i = k; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[i] - nums[i - k];\n\t\tres = std::max(res, current_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach recalculates the sum of  elements at every step. A sliding window reduces this to  by updating the current sum in , which is significantly more efficient for large values of ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <unordered_set>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longest_unique_substr_len(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length(), res = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tstd::unordered_set<char> set;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (set.count(s[j])) break;\n\t\t\tset.insert(s[j]);\n\t\t\tres = std::max(res, j - i + 1);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longest_unique_substr_len(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length(), start = 0, res = 0;\n\tstd::vector<int> last_idx(256, -1);\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tif (last_idx[(unsigned char)s[end]] >= start) {\n\t\t\tstart = last_idx[(unsigned char)s[end]] + 1;\n\t\t}\n\t\tlast_idx[(unsigned char)s[end]] = end;\n\t\tres = std::max(res, end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach checks every substring with a hash set. Using a sliding window with an index array to jump the window's starting point ensures that each character is visited once, achieving  linear time complexity."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <numeric>\n\nstd::vector<double> window_averages(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tstd::vector<double> res;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tdouble sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; ++j) sum += nums[j];\n\t\tres.push_back(sum / k);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nstd::vector<double> window_averages(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n < k) return {};\n\tstd::vector<double> res;\n\tdouble current_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) current_sum += nums[i];\n\tres.push_back(current_sum / k);\n\tfor (int i = k; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[i] - nums[i - k];\n\t\tres.push_back(current_sum / k);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe original nested loop takes  time. The sliding window approach maintains the sum in  by adding the new value and subtracting the old, resulting in a significantly faster  linear complexity."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint count_subarrays_sum_le(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint count = 0, n = nums.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tint s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\ts += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (s <= k) count++;\n\t\t\telse break;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nint count_subarrays_sum_le(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), start = 0, count = 0, s = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\ts += nums[end];\n\t\twhile (s > k && start <= end) {\n\t\t\ts -= nums[start++];\n\t\t}\n\t\tcount += (end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach checks every subarray individually. A sliding window approach for non-negative inputs computes the number of valid subarrays ending at 'end' in constant time, leading to a linear  total time complexity."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::vector<int> sliding_max(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint m = nums[i];\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < i + k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (nums[j] > m) m = nums[j];\n\t\t}\n\t\tres.push_back(m);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <deque>\n\nstd::vector<int> sliding_max(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n == 0) return {};\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tstd::deque<int> dq;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tif (!dq.empty() && dq.front() == i - k) dq.pop_front();\n\t\twhile (!dq.empty() && nums[dq.back()] <= nums[i]) dq.pop_back();\n\t\tdq.push_back(i);\n\t\tif (i >= k - 1) res.push_back(nums[dq.front()]);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe brute-force  approach repeatedly searches the entire window for the maximum. A monotonic deque maintains potential maxima in  amortized time, enabling a linear  solution for large window sizes."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nint smallest_sub_sum_ge(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), res = INT_MAX;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tint s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\ts += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (s >= k) {\n\t\t\t\tres = std::min(res, j - i + 1);\n\t\t\t\tbreak;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (res == INT_MAX) ? 0 : res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nint smallest_sub_sum_ge(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), start = 0, current_sum = 0, res = INT_MAX;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[end];\n\t\twhile (current_sum >= k) {\n\t\t\tres = std::min(res, end - start + 1);\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum -= nums[start++];\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (res == INT_MAX) ? 0 : res;\n}\n---\nThe original  algorithm re-sums from scratch for every starting point. For non-negative arrays, a two-pointer sliding window achieves  time by expanding and contracting the window bounds dynamically based on the current sum."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_sum_window_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), res = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; ++j) s += nums[j];\n\t\tif (i == 0 || s > res) res = s;\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_sum_window_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tint current_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) current_sum += nums[i];\n\tint res = current_sum;\n\tfor (int i = k; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[i] - nums[i - k];\n\t\tres = std::max(res, current_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach recalculates the sum of  elements at every step. A sliding window reduces this to  by updating the current sum in , which is significantly more efficient for large values of ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <unordered_set>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longest_unique_chars(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length(), res = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tstd::unordered_set<char> seen;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (seen.count(s[j])) break;\n\t\t\tseen.insert(s[j]);\n\t\t\tres = std::max(res, j - i + 1);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longest_unique_chars(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length(), start = 0, res = 0;\n\tstd::vector<int> last_idx(256, -1);\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tif (last_idx[(unsigned char)s[end]] >= start) {\n\t\t\tstart = last_idx[(unsigned char)s[end]] + 1;\n\t\t}\n\t\tlast_idx[(unsigned char)s[end]] = end;\n\t\tres = std::max(res, end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach checks every substring with a hash set. Using a sliding window with an index array to jump the window's starting point ensures that each character is visited once, achieving  linear time complexity."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <numeric>\n\nstd::vector<double> sliding_window_avg(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tstd::vector<double> res;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tdouble s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; ++j) s += nums[j];\n\t\tres.push_back(s / k);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nstd::vector<double> sliding_window_avg(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n < k) return {};\n\tstd::vector<double> res;\n\tdouble current_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) current_sum += nums[i];\n\tres.push_back(current_sum / k);\n\tfor (int i = k; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[i] - nums[i - k];\n\t\tres.push_back(current_sum / k);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe original nested loop takes  time. The sliding window approach maintains the sum in  by adding the new value and subtracting the old, resulting in a significantly faster  linear complexity."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint count_subarrays_lte_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint count = 0, n = nums.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tint s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\ts += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (s <= k) count++;\n\t\t\telse break;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nint count_subarrays_lte_k(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), start = 0, count = 0, s = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\ts += nums[end];\n\t\twhile (s > k && start <= end) {\n\t\t\ts -= nums[start++];\n\t\t}\n\t\tcount += (end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach checks every subarray individually. A sliding window approach for non-negative inputs computes the number of valid subarrays ending at 'end' in constant time, leading to a linear  total time complexity."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::vector<int> sliding_window_maxima(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint m = nums[i];\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < i + k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (nums[j] > m) m = nums[j];\n\t\t}\n\t\tres.push_back(m);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <deque>\n\nstd::vector<int> sliding_window_maxima(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n == 0) return {};\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tstd::deque<int> dq;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tif (!dq.empty() && dq.front() == i - k) dq.pop_front();\n\t\twhile (!dq.empty() && nums[dq.back()] <= nums[i]) dq.pop_back();\n\t\tdq.push_back(i);\n\t\tif (i >= k - 1) res.push_back(nums[dq.front()]);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe brute-force  approach repeatedly searches the entire window for the maximum. A monotonic deque maintains potential maxima in  amortized time, enabling a linear  solution for large window sizes."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nint min_len_sub_sum_ge(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), res = INT_MAX;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tint s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\ts += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (s >= k) {\n\t\t\t\tres = std::min(res, j - i + 1);\n\t\t\t\tbreak;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (res == INT_MAX) ? 0 : res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nint min_len_sub_sum_ge(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), start = 0, current_sum = 0, res = INT_MAX;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[end];\n\t\twhile (current_sum >= k) {\n\t\t\tres = std::min(res, end - start + 1);\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum -= nums[start++];\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (res == INT_MAX) ? 0 : res;\n}\n---\nThe original  algorithm re-sums from scratch for every starting point. For non-negative arrays, a two-pointer sliding window achieves  time by expanding and contracting the window bounds dynamically based on the current sum."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_sum_k_elements(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), res = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; ++j) s += nums[j];\n\t\tif (i == 0 || s > res) res = s;\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint max_sum_k_elements(const std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tint current_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) current_sum += nums[i];\n\tint res = current_sum;\n\tfor (int i = k; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[i] - nums[i - k];\n\t\tres = std::max(res, current_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach recalculates the sum of  elements at every step. A sliding window reduces this to  by updating the current sum in , which is significantly more efficient for large values of ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <numeric>\n\nint findMaxSum(std::vector<int>& arr, int k) {\n\tint n = arr.size();\n\tint max_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint current_sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum += arr[i + j];\n\t\t}\n\t\tif (current_sum > max_sum) max_sum = current_sum;\n\t}\n\treturn max_sum;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint findMaxSum(std::vector<int>& arr, int k) {\n\tint n = arr.size();\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tint current_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) current_sum += arr[i];\n\tint max_sum = current_sum;\n\tfor (int i = k; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += arr[i] - arr[i - k];\n\t\tmax_sum = std::max(max_sum, current_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn max_sum;\n}\n---\nThe original code recalculates the sum for every window, leading to  complexity. The optimized version uses a fixed-size sliding window to update the sum in  time per element, resulting in  total time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <unordered_set>\n\nint longestUnique(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length();\n\tint result = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tstd::unordered_set<char> seen;\n\t\t\tbool valid = true;\n\t\t\tfor (int k = i; k <= j; ++k) {\n\t\t\t\tif (seen.count(s[k])) { valid = false; break; }\n\t\t\t\tseen.insert(s[k]);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif (valid && (j - i + 1) > result) result = j - i + 1;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn result;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longestUnique(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length();\n\tstd::vector<int> last_seen(256, -1);\n\tint max_len = 0, start = 0;\n\tfor (int end = 0; end < n; ++end) {\n\t\tunsigned char c = s[end];\n\t\tif (last_seen[c] >= start) start = last_seen[c] + 1;\n\t\tlast_seen[c] = end;\n\t\tmax_len = std::max(max_len, end - start + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn max_len;\n}\n---\nThe brute-force approach checks every substring, resulting in  complexity. By using a sliding window with a frequency array, we can track unique characters in a single pass, achieving  time complexity and better cache locality."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nint countSubarrays(std::vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n\tint count = 0;\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tint current_sum = 0;\n\t\t\tfor (int k = i; k <= j; ++k) current_sum += nums[k];\n\t\t\tif (current_sum <= target) count++;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nint countSubarrays(std::vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint left = 0, current_sum = 0, count = 0;\n\tfor (int right = 0; right < n; ++right) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[right];\n\t\twhile (current_sum > target && left <= right) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum -= nums[left++];\n\t\t}\n\t\tcount += (right - left + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nThe unoptimized code evaluates all possible subarrays in  time. The sliding window approach maintains a running sum to identify all valid subarrays ending at the current index in  amortized time, yielding  total complexity."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::vector<int> maxInWindows(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tstd::vector<int> result;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint current_max = nums[i];\n\t\tfor (int j = 1; j < k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_max = std::max(current_max, nums[i + j]);\n\t\t}\n\t\tresult.push_back(current_max);\n\t}\n\treturn result;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <deque>\n\nstd::vector<int> maxInWindows(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tstd::vector<int> result;\n\tstd::deque<int> dq;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tif (!dq.empty() && dq.front() == i - k) dq.pop_front();\n\t\twhile (!dq.empty() && nums[dq.back()] <= nums[i]) dq.pop_back();\n\t\tdq.push_back(i);\n\t\tif (i >= k - 1) result.push_back(nums[dq.front()]);\n\t}\n\treturn result;\n}\n---\nThe original solution takes  time by scanning each window. Using a monotonic deque allows us to keep track of potential maximums and retrieve the window maximum in  amortized time, resulting in an  algorithm."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint minSubarrayLen(int target, std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint min_len = n + 1;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tint sum = 0;\n\t\t\tfor (int k = i; k <= j; ++k) sum += nums[k];\n\t\t\tif (sum >= target) min_len = std::min(min_len, j - i + 1);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (min_len > n) ? 0 : min_len;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint minSubarrayLen(int target, std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint left = 0, current_sum = 0, min_len = n + 1;\n\tfor (int right = 0; right < n; ++right) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[right];\n\t\twhile (current_sum >= target) {\n\t\t\tmin_len = std::min(min_len, right - left + 1);\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum -= nums[left++];\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (min_len > n) ? 0 : min_len;\n}\n---\nThe nested loops in the original code create an  bottleneck. A shrinking sliding window maintains a running sum and contracts the left boundary as soon as the condition is met, reducing the complexity to ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <climits>\n\nint minSumFixed(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint min_sum = INT_MAX;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tsum += nums[j];\n\t\t}\n\t\tif (sum < min_sum) min_sum = sum;\n\t}\n\treturn min_sum;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nint minSumFixed(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tint current_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) current_sum += nums[i];\n\tint min_sum = current_sum;\n\tfor (int i = k; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[i] - nums[i - k];\n\t\tmin_sum = std::min(min_sum, current_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn min_sum;\n}\n---\nThe naive approach calculates the sum of each window from scratch, leading to  time. By using a sliding window to add the new element and remove the old one, we reduce the complexity to ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint maxUniqueSub(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length();\n\tint best = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tstd::vector<int> freq(256, 0);\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (freq[static_cast<unsigned char>(s[j])]++) break;\n\t\t\tbest = std::max(best, j - i + 1);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn best;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint maxUniqueSub(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length();\n\tstd::vector<int> last_pos(256, -1);\n\tint best = 0, left = 0;\n\tfor (int right = 0; right < n; ++right) {\n\t\tunsigned char c = s[right];\n\t\tif (last_pos[c] >= left) left = last_pos[c] + 1;\n\t\tlast_pos[c] = right;\n\t\tbest = std::max(best, right - left + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn best;\n}\n---\nThe original  code re-scans the string for every starting index. The optimized sliding window approach tracks character positions to jump the left pointer, resulting in  time complexity and minimal memory overhead."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nint countSmallSums(std::vector<int>& arr, int k) {\n\tint n = arr.size();\n\tint result = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tlong long current = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcurrent += arr[j];\n\t\t\tif (current <= k) result++;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn result;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nint countSmallSums(std::vector<int>& arr, int k) {\n\tint n = arr.size();\n\tint left = 0, result = 0;\n\tlong long current_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int right = 0; right < n; ++right) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += arr[right];\n\t\twhile (current_sum > k && left <= right) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum -= arr[left++];\n\t\t}\n\t\tresult += (right - left + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn result;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach checks every subarray sum. A variable-size sliding window calculates the number of valid subarrays ending at each position in  amortized time, providing an  solution."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::vector<int> minInWindows(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tstd::vector<int> result;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint current_min = nums[i];\n\t\tfor (int j = 1; j < k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (nums[i + j] < current_min) current_min = nums[i + j];\n\t\t}\n\t\tresult.push_back(current_min);\n\t}\n\treturn result;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <deque>\n\nstd::vector<int> minInWindows(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tstd::vector<int> result;\n\tstd::deque<int> dq;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tif (!dq.empty() && dq.front() == i - k) dq.pop_front();\n\t\twhile (!dq.empty() && nums[dq.back()] >= nums[i]) dq.pop_back();\n\t\tdq.push_back(i);\n\t\tif (i >= k - 1) result.push_back(nums[dq.front()]);\n\t}\n\treturn result;\n}\n---\nThe brute-force  approach repeatedly searches the window for a minimum. A monotonic deque maintains indices of elements in increasing order, allowing  retrieval of the minimum and an overall  time complexity."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint findShortest(int target, std::vector<int>& data) {\n\tint n = data.size();\n\tint min_len = n + 1;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tint sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tsum += data[j];\n\t\t\tif (sum >= target) {\n\t\t\t\tmin_len = std::min(min_len, j - i + 1);\n\t\t\t\tbreak;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (min_len > n) ? 0 : min_len;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint findShortest(int target, std::vector<int>& data) {\n\tint n = data.size();\n\tint left = 0, sum = 0, min_len = n + 1;\n\tfor (int right = 0; right < n; ++right) {\n\t\tsum += data[right];\n\t\twhile (sum >= target) {\n\t\t\tmin_len = std::min(min_len, right - left + 1);\n\t\t\tsum -= data[left++];\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (min_len > n) ? 0 : min_len;\n}\n---\nThe original  code re-evaluates sums starting from each index. A sliding window technique allows the left boundary to move forward as the condition is met, reducing the complexity to  while maintaining correctness."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint maxSubSumK(const std::vector<int>& data, int k) {\n\tint n = data.size();\n\tint max_val = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint current = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcurrent += data[i + j];\n\t\t}\n\t\tif (current > max_val) max_val = current;\n\t}\n\treturn max_val;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint maxSubSumK(const std::vector<int>& data, int k) {\n\tint n = data.size();\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tint window_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) window_sum += data[i];\n\tint max_val = window_sum;\n\tfor (int i = k; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\twindow_sum += data[i] - data[i - k];\n\t\tmax_val = std::max(max_val, window_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn max_val;\n}\n---\nThe original nested loop approach has  complexity due to redundant additions. By using a sliding window, we maintain the sum by updating it in  per step, reducing the total complexity to ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <unordered_set>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint getLongestUniqueLen(std::string text) {\n\tint max_len = 0;\n\tint n = text.length();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tstd::unordered_set<char> seen;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (seen.count(text[j])) break;\n\t\t\tseen.insert(text[j]);\n\t\t\tmax_len = std::max(max_l, j - i + 1);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_len;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint getLongestUniqueLen(std::string text) {\n\tint n = text.length();\n\tif (n == 0) return 0;\n\tstd::vector<int> last_idx(256, -1);\n\tint max_len = 0, left = 0;\n\tfor (int right = 0; right < n; ++right) {\n\t\tunsigned char c = text[right];\n\t\tif (last_idx[c] >= left) left = last_idx[c] + 1;\n\t\tlast_idx[c] = right;\n\t\tmax_len = std::max(max_len, right - left + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn max_len;\n}\n---\nThe unoptimized code performs  operations with heavy set overhead. A sliding window with a direct-addressed table tracks character indices to skip redundant checks, achieving  time complexity and optimal performance."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nlong long countAllValid(std::vector<int>& arr, int limit) {\n\tlong long count = 0;\n\tint n = arr.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tlong long current = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcurrent += arr[j];\n\t\t\tif (current <= limit) count++;\n\t\t\telse break;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nlong long countAllValid(std::vector<int>& arr, int limit) {\n\tint n = arr.size();\n\tint left = 0;\n\tlong long current_sum = 0, total_count = 0;\n\tfor (int right = 0; right < n; ++right) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += arr[right];\n\t\twhile (current_sum > limit && left <= right) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum -= arr[left++];\n\t\t}\n\t\ttotal_count += (right - left + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn total_count;\n}\n---\nThe original solution iterates through all subarrays in  time. The sliding window approach maintains the sum and calculates the number of valid subarrays ending at 'right' in  amortized time, leading to  complexity."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::vector<int> slidingMax(const std::vector<int>& v, int k) {\n\tint n = v.size();\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tif (n < k) return res;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint m = v[i];\n\t\tfor (int j = 1; j < k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (v[i + j] > m) m = v[i + j];\n\t\t}\n\t\tres.push_back(m);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <deque>\n\nstd::vector<int> slidingMax(const std::vector<int>& v, int k) {\n\tint n = v.size();\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tif (n < k) return res;\n\tstd::deque<int> dq;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tif (!dq.empty() && dq.front() == i - k) dq.pop_front();\n\t\twhile (!dq.empty() && v[dq.back()] <= v[i]) dq.pop_back();\n\t\tdq.push_back(i);\n\t\tif (i >= k - 1) res.push_back(v[dq.front()]);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe original code uses  operations by re-scanning the window. A monotonic deque allows for  amortized insertion and extraction of the maximum, optimizing the process to  time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint minWindowWithSum(const std::vector<int>& nums, int s) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint min_len = n + 1;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tint current_sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (current_sum >= s) {\n\t\t\t\tmin_len = std::min(min_len, j - i + 1);\n\t\t\t\tbreak;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (min_len == n + 1) ? 0 : min_len;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint minWindowWithSum(const std::vector<int>& nums, int s) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint left = 0, current_sum = 0, min_len = n + 1;\n\tfor (int right = 0; right < n; ++right) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[right];\n\t\twhile (current_sum >= s) {\n\t\t\tmin_len = std::min(min_len, right - left + 1);\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum -= nums[left++];\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (min_len == n + 1) ? 0 : min_len;\n}\n---\nThe unoptimized code checks all subarrays in  time. By using a sliding window to incrementally add and remove elements from the sum, we find the shortest qualifying subarray in  time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <climits>\n\nint maxSubSumSizeK(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint res = INT_MIN;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; i++) {\n\t\tint s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < k; j++) {\n\t\t\ts += nums[i + j];\n\t\t}\n\t\tif (s > res) res = s;\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nint maxSubSumSizeK(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tint current_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; i++) current_sum += nums[i];\n\tint res = current_sum;\n\tfor (int i = k; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[i] - nums[i - k];\n\t\tres = std::max(res, current_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe original code has  complexity from nested loops. The sliding window technique maintains the running sum by adding the tail and subtracting the head in , reducing the complexity to ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <unordered_map>\n\nint lengthOfLongest(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length(), max_len = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tstd::unordered_map<char, int> m;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (m.count(s[j])) break;\n\t\t\tm[s[j]] = 1;\n\t\t\tif (j - i + 1 > max_len) max_len = j - i + 1;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_len;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint lengthOfLongest(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length();\n\tstd::vector<int> pos(256, -1);\n\tint max_len = 0, left = 0;\n\tfor (int right = 0; right < n; ++right) {\n\t\tif (pos[static_cast<unsigned char>(s[right])] >= left) {\n\t\t\tleft = pos[static_cast<unsigned char>(s[right])] + 1;\n\t\t}\n\t\tpos[static_cast<unsigned char>(s[right])] = right;\n\t\tmax_len = std::max(max_len, right - left + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn max_len;\n}\n---\nThe original  solution suffers from redundant processing and map overhead. Using a sliding window with a direct-addressed array for character indices reduces the time complexity to  and improves cache efficiency."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nlong long countSubarraysLess(std::vector<int>& v, int t) {\n\tlong long count = 0;\n\tint n = v.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tint s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\ts += v[j];\n\t\t\tif (s <= t) count++;\n\t\t\telse break;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nlong long countSubarraysLess(std::vector<int>& v, int t) {\n\tint n = v.size();\n\tint left = 0, current_sum = 0;\n\tlong long total = 0;\n\tfor (int right = 0; right < n; ++right) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += v[right];\n\t\twhile (current_sum > t && left <= right) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum -= v[left++];\n\t\t}\n\t\ttotal += (right - left + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn total;\n}\n---\nThe original  nested loop structure re-sums elements for every starting point. A sliding window reduces this to  by adjusting boundaries and calculating subarray counts based on window size at each step."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::vector<int> windowMaximums(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint m = nums[i];\n\t\tfor (int j = 1; j < k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (nums[i + j] > m) m = nums[i + j];\n\t\t}\n\t\tres.push_back(m);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <deque>\n\nstd::vector<int> windowMaximums(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tif (n < k) return res;\n\tstd::deque<int> dq;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tif (!dq.empty() && dq.front() == i - k) dq.pop_front();\n\t\twhile (!dq.empty() && nums[dq.back()] <= nums[i]) dq.pop_back();\n\t\tdq.push_back(i);\n\t\tif (i >= k - 1) res.push_back(nums[dq.front()]);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe naive  approach repeatedly scans each window for its maximum. Utilizing a monotonic deque ensures that each element is added and removed at most once, reducing the complexity to ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint shortestSumAtLeast(int s, std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint res = n + 1;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tint cur = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcur += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (cur >= s) {\n\t\t\t\tres = std::min(res, j - i + 1);\n\t\t\t\tbreak;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (res > n) ? 0 : res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint shortestSumAtLeast(int s, std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint left = 0, sum = 0, res = n + 1;\n\tfor (int right = 0; right < n; ++right) {\n\t\tsum += nums[right];\n\t\twhile (sum >= s) {\n\t\t\tres = std::min(res, right - left + 1);\n\t\t\tsum -= nums[left++];\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (res > n) ? 0 : res;\n}\n---\nThe unoptimized  approach checks every subarray sum. A variable-width sliding window provides an  solution by contracting the left pointer whenever the target sum is achieved."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <climits>\n\nint maxSumOfSizeK(const std::vector<int>& arr, int k) {\n\tint n = arr.size();\n\tint max_sum = INT_MIN;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; i++) {\n\t\tint s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < k; j++) s += arr[i + j];\n\t\tif (s > max_sum) max_sum = s;\n\t}\n\treturn max_sum;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nint maxSumOfSizeK(const std::vector<int>& arr, int k) {\n\tint n = arr.size();\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tint current_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; i++) current_sum += arr[i];\n\tint max_sum = current_sum;\n\tfor (int i = k; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += arr[i] - arr[i - k];\n\t\tmax_sum = std::max(max_sum, current_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn max_sum;\n}\n---\nThe original approach has an  complexity due to redundant additions in the inner loop. The sliding window technique reduces the time complexity to  by updating the window sum in constant time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <unordered_set>\n\nint longestSubNoRepeat(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length(), res = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tstd::unordered_set<char> set;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (set.count(s[j])) break;\n\t\t\tset.insert(s[j]);\n\t\t\tif (j - i + 1 > res) res = j - i + 1;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longestSubNoRepeat(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length(), res = 0, left = 0;\n\tstd::vector<int> char_map(256, -1);\n\tfor (int right = 0; right < n; right++) {\n\t\tif (char_map[static_cast<unsigned char>(s[right])] >= left) {\n\t\t\tleft = char_map[static_cast<unsigned char>(s[right])] + 1;\n\t\t}\n\t\tchar_map[static_cast<unsigned char>(s[right])] = right;\n\t\tres = std::max(res, right - left + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe brute-force  approach re-evaluates character uniqueness. Using a sliding window with a direct-addressed array reduces the complexity to  and avoids the high constant overhead of `std::unordered_set`."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nint countSubarraysAtMost(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint count = 0, n = nums.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tint s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\ts += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (s <= k) count++;\n\t\t\telse break;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nint countSubarraysAtMost(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint left = 0, current_sum = 0, count = 0;\n\tfor (int right = 0; right < n; right++) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[right];\n\t\twhile (current_sum > k && left <= right) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum -= nums[left++];\n\t\t}\n\t\tcount += (right - left + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nThe original code has  complexity due to checking all subarrays from scratch. A sliding window maintaining a running sum counts valid subarrays ending at each index in  amortized time, leading to  performance."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::vector<int> maxOfSubarrays(std::vector<int>& arr, int k) {\n\tint n = arr.size();\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; i++) {\n\t\tint max_val = arr[i];\n\t\tfor (int j = 1; j < k; j++) {\n\t\t\tmax_val = std::max(max_val, arr[i + j]);\n\t\t}\n\t\tres.push_back(max_val);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <deque>\n\nstd::vector<int> maxOfSubarrays(std::vector<int>& arr, int k) {\n\tint n = arr.size();\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tstd::deque<int> dq;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tif (!dq.empty() && dq.front() == i - k) dq.pop_front();\n\t\twhile (!dq.empty() && arr[dq.back()] <= arr[i]) dq.pop_back();\n\t\tdq.push_back(i);\n\t\tif (i >= k - 1) res.push_back(arr[dq.front()]);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe naive  approach repeatedly searches the entire window. A monotonic deque keeps candidate maximums in  amortized time, providing an  solution for window maximum retrieval."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint minSubArrayLen(int s, std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint ans = n + 1;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tint sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tsum += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (sum >= s) {\n\t\t\t\tans = std::min(ans, j - i + 1);\n\t\t\t\tbreak;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (ans == n + 1) ? 0 : ans;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint minSubArrayLen(int s, std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint left = 0, sum = 0, ans = n + 1;\n\tfor (int right = 0; right < n; right++) {\n\t\tsum += nums[right];\n\t\twhile (sum >= s) {\n\t\t\tans = std::min(ans, right - left + 1);\n\t\t\tsum -= nums[left++];\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (ans == n + 1) ? 0 : ans;\n}\n---\nThe original  code re-calculates subarray sums for every window. A sliding window technique efficiently contracts the left boundary to find the minimum length, achieving  time complexity."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <climits>\n\nint minKSum(std::vector<int>& arr, int k) {\n\tint n = arr.size();\n\tint res = INT_MAX;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; i++) {\n\t\tint cur = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < k; j++) cur += arr[i + j];\n\t\tif (cur < res) res = cur;\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nint minKSum(std::vector<int>& arr, int k) {\n\tint n = arr.size();\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tint current_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; i++) current_sum += arr[i];\n\tint res = current_sum;\n\tfor (int i = k; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += arr[i] - arr[i - k];\n\t\tres = std::min(res, current_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe original code recalculates the sum for every window, leading to  complexity. A fixed-size sliding window updates the sum by adding/subtracting boundary elements in , resulting in  total time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <unordered_set>\n\nint longestSubNoDuplicates(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length(), max_len = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tstd::unordered_set<char> seen;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (seen.count(s[j])) break;\n\t\t\tseen.insert(s[j]);\n\t\t\tif (j - i + 1 > max_len) max_len = j - i + 1;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_len;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longestSubNoDuplicates(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length();\n\tstd::vector<int> last_pos(256, -1);\n\tint max_len = 0, left = 0;\n\tfor (int right = 0; right < n; right++) {\n\t\tunsigned char c = s[right];\n\t\tif (last_pos[c] >= left) left = last_pos[c] + 1;\n\t\tlast_pos[c] = right;\n\t\tmax_len = std::max(max_len, right - left + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn max_len;\n}\n---\nThe unoptimized  solution re-scans for every starting position. Using a sliding window with an integer array to track the last seen index of characters optimizes the process to  with minimal memory overhead."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nint countSubarraysSumTarget(std::vector<int>& v, int t) {\n\tint n = v.size(), ans = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tlong long sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tsum += v[j];\n\t\t\tif (sum <= t) ans++;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn ans;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nint countSubarraysSumTarget(std::vector<int>& v, int t) {\n\tint n = v.size();\n\tint left = 0, current_sum = 0, count = 0;\n\tfor (int right = 0; right < n; right++) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += v[right];\n\t\twhile (current_sum > t && left <= right) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum -= v[left++];\n\t\t}\n\t\tcount += (right - left + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach checks every subarray sum using nested loops. A sliding window technique manages the sum in  total time, using the window size to count all valid subarrays ending at each position."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::vector<int> findWindowMax(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; i++) {\n\t\tint m = nums[i];\n\t\tfor (int j = 1; j < k; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (nums[i + j] > m) m = nums[i + j];\n\t\t}\n\t\tres.push_back(m);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\n#include <deque>\n\nstd::vector<int> findWindowMax(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tif (n < k) return res;\n\tstd::deque<int> dq;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tif (!dq.empty() && dq.front() == i - k) dq.pop_front();\n\t\twhile (!dq.empty() && nums[dq.back()] <= nums[i]) dq.pop_back();\n\t\tdq.push_back(i);\n\t\tif (i >= k - 1) res.push_back(nums[dq.front()]);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach repeatedly iterates through each window. Using a monotonic deque allows tracking potential maximums in  time, as each element index is added and removed from the deque exactly once."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint shortestAtLeast(int target, std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint min_len = n + 1;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tint s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\ts += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (s >= target) {\n\t\t\t\tmin_len = std::min(min_len, j - i + 1);\n\t\t\t\tbreak;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (min_len > n) ? 0 : min_len;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint shortestAtLeast(int target, std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint left = 0, current_sum = 0, min_len = n + 1;\n\tfor (int right = 0; right < n; right++) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[right];\n\t\twhile (current_sum >= target) {\n\t\t\tmin_len = std::min(min_len, right - left + 1);\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum -= nums[left++];\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (min_len > n) ? 0 : min_len;\n}\n---\nThe brute-force  solution recalculates subarray sums repeatedly. A sliding window technique allows for an  solution by incrementally adding and subtracting elements to find the smallest range satisfying the condition."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <climits>\n\nint getMaxFixed(std::vector<int>& data, int k) {\n\tint n = data.size(), best = INT_MIN;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; i++) {\n\t\tint temp = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < k; j++) temp += data[i + j];\n\t\tif (temp > best) best = temp;\n\t}\n\treturn best;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nint getMaxFixed(std::vector<int>& data, int k) {\n\tint n = data.size();\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tint current_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; i++) current_sum += data[i];\n\tint best = current_sum;\n\tfor (int i = k; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += data[i] - data[i - k];\n\t\tbest = std::max(best, current_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn best;\n}\n---\nThe original code has  complexity due to recalculating sums for overlapping windows. A sliding window approach achieves  time by maintaining the sum through incremental updates at each step."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <unordered_map>\n\nint longestNoRepChars(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length(), ans = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tstd::unordered_map<char, int> seen;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (seen.count(s[j])) break;\n\t\t\tseen[s[j]] = 1;\n\t\t\tif (j - i + 1 > ans) ans = j - i + 1;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn ans;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longestNoRepChars(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length();\n\tstd::vector<int> char_idx(256, -1);\n\tint ans = 0, l = 0;\n\tfor (int r = 0; r < n; r++) {\n\t\tunsigned char c = s[r];\n\t\tif (char_idx[c] >= l) l = char_idx[c] + 1;\n\t\tchar_idx[c] = r;\n\t\tans = std::max(ans, r - l + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn ans;\n}\n---\nThe unoptimized  approach re-evaluates character uniqueness. A sliding window with an index array achieves  time complexity and significantly reduces memory allocation overhead compared to hash maps."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nint countSmallSums(std::vector<int>& v, int t) {\n\tint n = v.size(), res = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tint s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\ts += v[j];\n\t\t\tif (s <= t) res++;\n\t\t\telse break;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nint countSmallSums(std::vector<int>& v, int t) {\n\tint n = v.size();\n\tint l = 0, current_sum = 0, res = 0;\n\tfor (int r = 0; r < n; r++) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += v[r];\n\t\twhile (current_sum > t && l <= r) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum -= v[l++];\n\t\t}\n\t\tres += (r - l + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach checks every subarray sum. A sliding window reduces this to  by maintaining a running sum and counting the number of valid subarrays ending at the current right boundary."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::vector<int> windowMaxDeque(std::vector<int>& arr, int k) {\n\tint n = arr.size();\n\tstd::vector<int> result;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; i++) {\n\t\tint m_val = arr[i];\n\t\tfor (int j = 1; j < k; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (arr[i + j] > m_val) m_val = arr[i + j];\n\t\t}\n\t\tresult.push_back(m_val);\n\t}\n\treturn result;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <deque>\n\nstd::vector<int> windowMaxDeque(std::vector<int>& arr, int k) {\n\tint n = arr.size();\n\tstd::vector<int> result;\n\tif (n < k) return result;\n\tstd::deque<int> dq;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tif (!dq.empty() && dq.front() == i - k) dq.pop_front();\n\t\twhile (!dq.empty() && arr[dq.back()] <= arr[i]) dq.pop_back();\n\t\tdq.push_back(i);\n\t\tif (i >= k - 1) result.push_back(arr[dq.front()]);\n\t}\n\treturn result;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach repeatedly searches the window for a maximum. Using a monotonic deque to maintain indices of window elements in descending order allows  access to the maximum, yielding an  algorithm."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint findMinSubarraySize(int s, std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tint n = v.size(), res = n + 1;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tint cur = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tcur += v[j];\n\t\t\tif (cur >= s) {\n\t\t\t\tres = std::min(res, j - i + 1);\n\t\t\t\tbreak;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (res > n) ? 0 : res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint findMinSubarraySize(int s, std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tint n = v.size();\n\tint left = 0, current_sum = 0, res = n + 1;\n\tfor (int right = 0; right < n; right++) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += v[right];\n\t\twhile (current_sum >= s) {\n\t\t\tres = std::min(res, right - left + 1);\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum -= v[left++];\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (res > n) ? 0 : res;\n}\n---\nThe brute-force  approach checks every subarray sum. A variable-sized sliding window efficiently finds the smallest qualifying subarray in  time by incrementally expanding and contracting the window boundaries."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <climits>\n\nint fixedWindowMax(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), max_s = INT_MIN;\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; i++) {\n\t\tint cur = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < k; j++) cur += nums[i + j];\n\t\tif (cur > max_s) max_s = cur;\n\t}\n\treturn max_s;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nint fixedWindowMax(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tint current_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; i++) current_sum += nums[i];\n\tint max_s = current_sum;\n\tfor (int i = k; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[i] - nums[i - k];\n\t\tmax_s = std::max(max_s, current_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn max_s;\n}\n---\nThe unoptimized  approach recalculates window sums from scratch. A sliding window technique reduces the time complexity to  by maintaining a running total and updating it in constant time per step."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <unordered_set>\n\nint longestUniqueSubstr(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length(), best = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tstd::unordered_set<char> seen;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (seen.count(s[j])) break;\n\t\t\tseen.insert(s[j]);\n\t\t\tif (j - i + 1 > best) best = j - i + 1;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn best;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longestUniqueSubstr(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length(), best = 0, l = 0;\n\tstd::vector<int> char_map(256, -1);\n\tfor (int r = 0; r < n; r++) {\n\t\tunsigned char c = s[r];\n\t\tif (char_map[c] >= l) l = char_map[c] + 1;\n\t\tchar_map[c] = r;\n\t\tbest = std::max(best, r - l + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn best;\n}\n---\nThe original  algorithm re-scans characters and has high map overhead. A sliding window with a direct-addressed index array reduces complexity to  and improves cache locality significantly."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nint countValidSubarrays(std::vector<int>& nums, int limit) {\n\tint count = 0, n = nums.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tint current_sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (current_sum <= limit) count++;\n\t\t\telse break;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nint countValidSubarrays(std::vector<int>& nums, int limit) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint left = 0, current_sum = 0, count = 0;\n\tfor (int right = 0; right < n; right++) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[right];\n\t\twhile (current_sum > limit && left <= right) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum -= nums[left++];\n\t\t}\n\t\tcount += (right - left + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nThe unoptimized nested loop approach results in  complexity. A sliding window approach maintains the sum in  total time, allowing the calculation of all valid subarrays ending at each position in constant time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::vector<int> maxWindow(std::vector<int>& arr, int k) {\n\tint n = arr.size();\n\tstd::vector<int> results;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; i++) {\n\t\tint m = arr[i];\n\t\tfor (int j = 1; j < k; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (arr[i+j] > m) m = arr[i+j];\n\t\t}\n\t\tresults.push_back(m);\n\t}\n\treturn results;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <deque>\n\nstd::vector<int> maxWindow(std::vector<int>& arr, int k) {\n\tint n = arr.size();\n\tstd::vector<int> results;\n\tif (n < k) return results;\n\tstd::deque<int> dq;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tif (!dq.empty() && dq.front() == i - k) dq.pop_front();\n\t\twhile (!dq.empty() && arr[dq.back()] <= arr[i]) dq.pop_back();\n\t\tdq.push_back(i);\n\t\tif (i >= k - 1) results.push_back(arr[dq.front()]);\n\t}\n\treturn results;\n}\n---\nThe original solution is  because it re-scans the entire window for each maximum. Using a monotonic deque allows us to maintain max candidates in  amortized time, providing an  solution."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint findShortestWindow(int s, std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), res = n + 1;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tint total = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\ttotal += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (total >= s) {\n\t\t\t\tres = std::min(res, j - i + 1);\n\t\t\t\tbreak;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (res > n) ? 0 : res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint findShortestWindow(int s, std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), left = 0, total = 0, res = n + 1;\n\tfor (int right = 0; right < n; right++) {\n\t\ttotal += nums[right];\n\t\twhile (total >= s) {\n\t\t\tres = std::min(res, right - left + 1);\n\t\t\ttotal -= nums[left++];\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (res > n) ? 0 : res;\n}\n---\nThe original  code re-sums the same elements for different starting points. A variable sliding window approach finds the minimal qualifying length in  time by incrementally expanding and contracting the window boundaries."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <climits>\n\nint maxSumKFixed(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), max_s = INT_MIN;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; i++) {\n\t\tint cur = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < k; j++) cur += nums[i + j];\n\t\tif (cur > max_s) max_s = cur;\n\t}\n\treturn max_s;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nint maxSumKFixed(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tint cur_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; i++) cur_sum += nums[i];\n\tint max_s = cur_sum;\n\tfor (int i = k; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tcur_sum += nums[i] - nums[i - k];\n\t\tmax_s = std::max(max_s, cur_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn max_s;\n}\n---\nThe unoptimized code performs  operations by recalculating sums redundantly. A fixed-size sliding window updates the sum in  by adding the entering element and subtracting the leaving element, reducing complexity to ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <unordered_set>\n\nint longestUniqueChars(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length(), best = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tstd::unordered_set<char> seen;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (seen.count(s[j])) break;\n\t\t\tseen.insert(s[j]);\n\t\t\tif (j - i + 1 > best) best = j - i + 1;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn best;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longestUniqueChars(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length(), best = 0, l = 0;\n\tstd::vector<int> last_seen(256, -1);\n\tfor (int r = 0; r < n; r++) {\n\t\tunsigned char c = s[r];\n\t\tif (last_seen[c] >= l) l = last_seen[c] + 1;\n\t\tlast_seen[c] = r;\n\t\tbest = std::max(best, r - l + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn best;\n}\n---\nThe original solution is  with expensive set allocations. A sliding window using an integer array to track the last seen index of each character reduces the time complexity to  and memory fragmentation."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nint countTargetSubarrays(std::vector<int>& v, int t) {\n\tint n = v.size(), ans = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tint s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\ts += v[j];\n\t\t\tif (s <= t) ans++;\n\t\t\telse break;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn ans;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nint countTargetSubarrays(std::vector<int>& v, int t) {\n\tint n = v.size();\n\tint l = 0, current_sum = 0, ans = 0;\n\tfor (int r = 0; r < n; r++) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += v[r];\n\t\twhile (current_sum > t && l <= r) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum -= v[l++];\n\t\t}\n\t\tans += (r - l + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn ans;\n}\n---\nThe brute-force approach has  complexity due to re-calculating sums for all starting points. A sliding window maintaining a running sum counts all valid subarrays in  time by adjusting the window boundaries efficiently."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::vector<int> windowMaxAlg(std::vector<int>& data, int k) {\n\tint n = data.size();\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; i++) {\n\t\tint m = data[i];\n\t\tfor (int j = 1; j < k; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (data[i + j] > m) m = data[i + j];\n\t\t}\n\t\tres.push_back(m);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <deque>\n\nstd::vector<int> windowMaxAlg(std::vector<int>& data, int k) {\n\tint n = data.size();\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tif (n < k) return res;\n\tstd::deque<int> dq;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tif (!dq.empty() && dq.front() == i - k) dq.pop_front();\n\t\twhile (!dq.empty() && data[dq.back()] <= data[i]) dq.pop_back();\n\t\tdq.push_back(i);\n\t\tif (i >= k - 1) res.push_back(data[dq.front()]);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach recalculates window maximums through linear search. Using a monotonic deque keeps track of potential maximums and allows  amortized access per window, improving complexity to ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint findMinSubSize(int target, std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), res = n + 1;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tint s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\ts += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (s >= target) {\n\t\t\t\tres = std::min(res, j - i + 1);\n\t\t\t\tbreak;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (res > n) ? 0 : res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint findMinSubSize(int target, std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), left = 0, s = 0, res = n + 1;\n\tfor (int right = 0; right < n; right++) {\n\t\ts += nums[right];\n\t\twhile (s >= target) {\n\t\t\tres = std::min(res, right - left + 1);\n\t\t\ts -= nums[left++];\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (res > n) ? 0 : res;\n}\n---\nThe unoptimized code checks every possible subarray, resulting in  complexity. A sliding window technique allows for an  solution by adjusting the left boundary as soon as the sum meets the target."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <climits>\n\nint maxKSumNaive(std::vector<int>& data, int k) {\n\tint n = data.size(), ans = INT_MIN;\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; i++) {\n\t\tint s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < k; j++) s += data[i + j];\n\t\tif (s > ans) ans = s;\n\t}\n\treturn ans;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nint maxKSumNaive(std::vector<int>& data, int k) {\n\tint n = data.size();\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tint current_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; i++) current_sum += data[i];\n\tint ans = current_sum;\n\tfor (int i = k; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += data[i] - data[i - k];\n\t\tans = std::max(ans, current_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn ans;\n}\n---\nThe naive  approach recalculates the sum for every window position. A fixed sliding window maintains the running sum by updating it in  time per element, achieving a linear  complexity."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <unordered_set>\n\nint longestDistinctSubstring(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length(), best = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tstd::unordered_set<char> seen;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (seen.count(s[j])) break;\n\t\t\tseen.insert(s[j]);\n\t\t\tif (j - i + 1 > best) best = j - i + 1;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn best;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longestDistinctSubstring(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length(), best = 0, l = 0;\n\tstd::vector<int> char_map(256, -1);\n\tfor (int r = 0; r < n; r++) {\n\t\tunsigned char c = s[r];\n\t\tif (char_map[c] >= l) l = char_map[c] + 1;\n\t\tchar_map[c] = r;\n\t\tbest = std::max(best, r - l + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn best;\n}\n---\nThe original solution is  and allocates memory for sets frequently. A sliding window using an array for character positions achieves  time complexity and provides better performance via improved data locality."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nint countSmallSumsSub(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), result = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tint current_sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (current_sum <= k) result++;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn result;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nint countSmallSumsSub(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint left = 0, current_sum = 0, result = 0;\n\tfor (int right = 0; right < n; right++) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[right];\n\t\twhile (current_sum > k && left <= right) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum -= nums[left++];\n\t\t}\n\t\tresult += (right - left + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn result;\n}\n---\nThe original code has  complexity due to re-calculating sums for all starting points. A sliding window maintaining a running sum counts valid subarrays in  time by adjusting window boundaries efficiently."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::vector<int> maxWindowVector(std::vector<int>& arr, int k) {\n\tint n = arr.size();\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; i++) {\n\t\tint m = arr[i];\n\t\tfor (int j = 1; j < k; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (arr[i+j] > m) m = arr[i+j];\n\t\t}\n\t\tres.push_back(m);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <deque>\n\nstd::vector<int> maxWindowVector(std::vector<int>& arr, int k) {\n\tint n = arr.size();\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tif (n < k) return res;\n\tstd::deque<int> dq;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tif (!dq.empty() && dq.front() == i - k) dq.pop_front();\n\t\twhile (!dq.empty() && arr[dq.back()] <= arr[i]) dq.pop_back();\n\t\tdq.push_back(i);\n\t\tif (i >= k - 1) res.push_back(arr[dq.front()]);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe original solution is  because it re-scans the entire window for each maximum. Using a monotonic deque allows us to maintain max candidates in  amortized time, providing an  solution."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint findShortestRange(int target, std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tint n = v.size(), best = n + 1;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tint sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tsum += v[j];\n\t\t\tif (sum >= target) {\n\t\t\t\tbest = std::min(best, j - i + 1);\n\t\t\t\tbreak;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (best > n) ? 0 : best;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint findShortestRange(int target, std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tint n = v.size(), left = 0, sum = 0, best = n + 1;\n\tfor (int right = 0; right < n; right++) {\n\t\tsum += v[right];\n\t\twhile (sum >= target) {\n\t\t\tbest = std::min(best, right - left + 1);\n\t\t\tsum -= v[left++];\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (best > n) ? 0 : best;\n}\n---\nThe unoptimized  approach checks every subarray sum. A variable-sized sliding window efficiently finds the smallest qualifying subarray in  time by incrementally expanding and contracting the window boundaries."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <numeric>\n\nlong long maxSubarraySumK(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tlong long max_sum = -1e18;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tlong long current_sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[j];\n\t\t}\n\t\tif (current_sum > max_sum) max_sum = current_sum;\n\t}\n\treturn max_sum;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nlong long maxSubarraySumK(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tlong long current_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) current_sum += nums[i];\n\tlong long max_sum = current_sum;\n\tfor (int i = k; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += (nums[i] - nums[i - k]);\n\t\tmax_sum = std::max(max_sum, current_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn max_sum;\n}\n---\nThe original code uses a nested loop resulting in  complexity due to redundant summations. The optimized sliding window approach maintains the sum in  per step, reducing the total time complexity to ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <unordered_set>\n\nint longestUniqueSub(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length();\n\tint result = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tstd::unordered_set<char> chars;\n\t\t\tbool dup = false;\n\t\t\tfor (int k = i; k <= j; ++k) {\n\t\t\t\tif (chars.count(s[k])) { dup = true; break; }\n\t\t\t\tchars.insert(s[k]);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif (!dup) result = (j - i + 1 > result) ? j - i + 1 : result;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn result;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longestUniqueSub(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length();\n\tstd::vector<int> last_pos(256, -1);\n\tint result = 0, start = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tstart = std::max(start, last_pos[(unsigned char)s[i]] + 1);\n\t\tresult = std::max(result, i - start + 1);\n\t\tlast_pos[(unsigned char)s[i]] = i;\n\t}\n\treturn result;\n}\n---\nThe brute-force method checks every substring in  time with high allocation overhead. Using a sliding window with a frequency array/map allows us to track character positions in  time with  auxiliary space."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nint countSubarraysUnderTarget(std::vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n\tint count = 0;\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i; j < nums.size(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tlong long sum = 0;\n\t\t\tfor (size_t k = i; k <= j; ++k) sum += nums[k];\n\t\t\tif (sum <= target) count++;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nint countSubarraysUnderTarget(std::vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n\tint count = 0, left = 0;\n\tlong long current_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int right = 0; right < (int)nums.size(); ++right) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[right];\n\t\twhile (current_sum > target && left <= right) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum -= nums[left++];\n\t\t}\n\t\tcount += (right - left + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nThe original cubic complexity  is caused by re-summing every possible subarray range. A sliding window reduces this to  by maintaining a running sum and calculating the number of valid subarrays ending at the current pointer."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::vector<int> slidingWindowMax(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tstd::vector<int> result;\n\tif (nums.empty()) return result;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= (int)nums.size() - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint current_max = nums[i];\n\t\tfor (int j = 1; j < k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_max = std::max(current_max, nums[i + j]);\n\t\t}\n\t\tresult.push_back(current_max);\n\t}\n\treturn result;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <deque>\n\nstd::vector<int> slidingWindowMax(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tstd::vector<int> result;\n\tstd::deque<int> dq;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < (int)nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tif (!dq.empty() && dq.front() == i - k) dq.pop_front();\n\t\twhile (!dq.empty() && nums[dq.back()] < nums[i]) dq.pop_back();\n\t\tdq.push_back(i);\n\t\tif (i >= k - 1) result.push_back(nums[dq.front()]);\n\t}\n\treturn result;\n}\n---\nThe unoptimized code performs a full scan of each window, resulting in  time. By utilizing a monotonic deque to track indices of potential maximums, the algorithm achieves  time as each element is processed at most twice."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint minLenSubarraySum(std::vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint min_len = n + 1;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tint current_sum = 0;\n\t\t\tfor (int k = i; k <= j; ++k) current_sum += nums[k];\n\t\t\tif (current_sum >= target) min_len = std::min(min_len, j - i + 1);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (min_len == n + 1) ? 0 : min_len;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint minLenSubarraySum(std::vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint left = 0, current_sum = 0, min_len = n + 1;\n\tfor (int right = 0; right < n; ++right) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[right];\n\t\twhile (current_sum >= target) {\n\t\t\tmin_len = std::min(min_len, right - left + 1);\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum -= nums[left++];\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (min_len == n + 1) ? 0 : min_len;\n}\n---\nThe original  algorithm redundantlly calculates sums for all subarray pairs. The optimized variable sliding window maintains a running sum and shrinks the window from the left to find the minimum length in  time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <climits>\n\nint minWindowSumK(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint min_sum = INT_MAX;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint current_sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[j];\n\t\t}\n\t\tif (current_sum < min_sum) min_sum = current_sum;\n\t}\n\treturn min_sum;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint minWindowSumK(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tint current_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) current_sum += nums[i];\n\tint min_sum = current_sum;\n\tfor (int i = k; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[i] - nums[i - k];\n\t\tmin_sum = std::min(min_sum, current_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn min_sum;\n}\n---\nThe unoptimized approach re-scans the window size  for every element, leading to  complexity. A fixed sliding window updates the sum by adding the new element and removing the old one in , resulting in  time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n\nbool hasAllChars(std::string s, std::string target) {\n\tfor (char c : target) {\n\t\tbool found = false;\n\t\tfor (char sc : s) { if (sc == c) { found = true; break; } }\n\t\tif (!found) return false;\n\t}\n\treturn true;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n\nbool hasAllChars(std::string s, std::string target) {\n\tstd::vector<int> counts(256, 0);\n\tfor (char c : s) counts[(unsigned char)c]++;\n\tfor (char c : target) {\n\t\tif (counts[(unsigned char)c] == 0) return false;\n\t}\n\treturn true;\n}\n---\nThe original code has  complexity due to nested linear searches. Using a frequency array (hash map) allows checking character existence in  time with constant space for the ASCII set."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nstd::vector<long long> allSubarraySums(std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tstd::vector<long long> sums;\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i; j < nums.size(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tlong long s = 0;\n\t\t\tfor (size_t k = i; k <= j; ++k) s += nums[k];\n\t\t\tsums.push_back(s);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn sums;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nstd::vector<long long> allSubarraySums(std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tstd::vector<long long> sums;\n\tsums.reserve(n * (n + 1) / 2);\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tlong long current_sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[j];\n\t\t\tsums.push_back(current_sum);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn sums;\n}\n---\nThe original approach uses three nested loops (). By accumulating the sum as the right boundary of the subarray expands, we reduce the complexity to  and minimize redundant additions."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint findMaxSubarrayProduct(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint max_prod = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint prod = 1;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; ++j) prod *= nums[j];\n\t\tif (i == 0 || prod > max_prod) max_prod = prod;\n\t}\n\treturn max_prod;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint findMaxSubarrayProduct(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tint current_prod = 1;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) current_prod *= nums[i];\n\tint max_prod = current_prod;\n\tfor (int i = k; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tif (nums[i - k] != 0) current_prod /= nums[i - k];\n\t\telse {\n\t\t\tcurrent_prod = 1;\n\t\t\tfor (int j = i - k + 1; j <= i; ++j) current_prod *= nums[j];\n\t\t}\n\t\tcurrent_prod *= nums[i];\n\t\tmax_prod = std::max(max_prod, current_prod);\n\t}\n\treturn max_prod;\n}\n---\nThe naive approach takes  time. A sliding window with division reduces this to , though handling zeros requires special care to reset the window product correctly."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nint countSubarraysSumExactlyK(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint count = 0;\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i; j < nums.size(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tint s = 0;\n\t\t\tfor (size_t l = i; l <= j; ++l) s += nums[l];\n\t\t\tif (s == k) count++;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <unordered_map>\n\nint countSubarraysSumExactlyK(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tstd::unordered_map<long long, int> prefix_sums;\n\tprefix_sums[0] = 1;\n\tlong long current_sum = 0;\n\tint count = 0;\n\tfor (int x : nums) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += x;\n\t\tif (prefix_sums.count(current_sum - k)) {\n\t\t\tcount += prefix_sums[current_sum - k];\n\t\t}\n\t\tprefix_sums[current_sum]++;\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nThe original  algorithm is extremely inefficient for large arrays. Using a hash map to store prefix sums allows finding subarrays that sum to  in  time by checking if  was seen before."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n\nint countAnagrams(std::string text, std::string word) {\n\tint count = 0, n = text.length(), m = word.length();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - m; ++i) {\n\t\tstd::string sub = text.substr(i, m);\n\t\tstd::vector<int> f1(256, 0), f2(256, 0);\n\t\tfor (char c : sub) f1[(unsigned char)c]++;\n\t\tfor (char c : word) f2[(unsigned char)c]++;\n\t\tif (f1 == f2) count++;\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n\nint countAnagrams(std::string text, std::string word) {\n\tint n = text.length(), m = word.length(), count = 0;\n\tif (m > n) return 0;\n\tstd::vector<int> f_word(256, 0), f_window(256, 0);\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < m; ++i) {\n\t\tf_word[(unsigned char)word[i]]++;\n\t\tf_window[(unsigned char)text[i]]++;\n\t}\n\tif (f_word == f_window) count++;\n\tfor (int i = m; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tf_window[(unsigned char)text[i]]++;\n\t\tf_window[(unsigned char)text[i - m]]--;\n\t\tif (f_word == f_window) count++;\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nThe unoptimized version creates substrings and re-counts frequencies in  time. A sliding window with frequency arrays reduces this to  by updating character counts incrementally."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint maxOfMins(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint overall_max = -2e9;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint current_min = nums[i];\n\t\tfor (int j = 1; j < k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_min = std::min(current_min, nums[i+j]);\n\t\t}\n\t\toverall_max = std::max(overall_max, current_min);\n\t}\n\treturn overall_max;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <deque>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint maxOfMins(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tstd::deque<int> dq;\n\tint overall_max = -2e9;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tif (!dq.empty() && dq.front() == i - k) dq.pop_front();\n\t\twhile (!dq.empty() && nums[dq.back()] > nums[i]) dq.pop_back();\n\t\tdq.push_back(i);\n\t\tif (i >= k - 1) overall_max = std::max(overall_max, nums[dq.front()]);\n\t}\n\treturn overall_max;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach repeatedly searches for the minimum in each window. Using a monotonic deque allows us to maintain the minimum of a sliding window in  total time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nint findLongestSubarrayWithSum(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint max_len = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < (int)nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tint current_sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < (int)nums.size(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (current_sum == k) {\n\t\t\t\tif (j - i + 1 > max_len) max_len = j - i + 1;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_len;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <unordered_map>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint findLongestSubarrayWithSum(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tstd::unordered_map<long long, int> first_occurrence;\n\tfirst_occurrence[0] = -1;\n\tlong long current_sum = 0;\n\tint max_len = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < (int)nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[i];\n\t\tif (first_occurrence.count(current_sum - k)) {\n\t\t\tmax_len = std::max(max_len, i - first_occurrence[current_sum - k]);\n\t\t}\n\t\tif (first_occurrence.find(current_sum) == first_occurrence.end()) {\n\t\t\tfirst_occurrence[current_sum] = i;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_len;\n}\n---\nThe original  search for subarrays is slow for large inputs. A prefix sum hash map allows locating the first index where a specific sum occurred, reducing the complexity to ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nlong long sumOfAllSubarrayMins(std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tlong long total = 0;\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i; j < nums.size(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tint m = nums[i];\n\t\t\tfor (size_t k = i + 1; k <= j; ++k) {\n\t\t\t\tif (nums[k] < m) m = nums[k];\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\ttotal += m;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn total;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <stack>\n\nlong long sumOfAllSubarrayMins(std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tstd::vector<int> left(n), right(n);\n\tstd::stack<int> s;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\twhile (!s.empty() && nums[s.top()] >= nums[i]) s.pop();\n\t\tleft[i] = s.empty() ? i + 1 : i - s.top();\n\t\ts.push(i);\n\t}\n\twhile (!s.empty()) s.pop();\n\tfor (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; --i) {\n\t\twhile (!s.empty() && nums[s.top()] > nums[i]) s.pop();\n\t\tright[i] = s.empty() ? n - i : s.top() - i;\n\t\ts.push(i);\n\t}\n\tlong long total = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) total += (long long)nums[i] * left[i] * right[i];\n\treturn total;\n}\n---\nThe cubic  approach is optimized to  using a Monotonic Stack to find the range where each element is the minimum. This leverages the contribution of each element to the total sum based on the number of subarrays it anchors."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <set>\n\nint countDistinctInWindows(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint total_distinct_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= (int)nums.size() - k; ++i) {\n\t\tstd::set<int> window;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; ++j) window.insert(nums[j]);\n\t\ttotal_distinct_sum += window.size();\n\t}\n\treturn total_distinct_sum;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <unordered_map>\n\nint countDistinctInWindows(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tstd::unordered_map<int, int> counts;\n\tint total_distinct_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) counts[nums[i]]++;\n\ttotal_distinct_sum += counts.size();\n\tfor (int i = k; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tint out = nums[i - k];\n\t\tif (--counts[out] == 0) counts.erase(out);\n\t\tcounts[nums[i]]++;\n\t\ttotal_distinct_sum += counts.size();\n\t}\n\treturn total_distinct_sum;\n}\n---\nThe original code creates a set for every window, leading to  complexity. Using a sliding window with an `unordered_map` to track frequencies reduces the complexity to  and avoids redundant allocations."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nlong long maxArea(std::vector<int>& height) {\n\tlong long max_val = 0;\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < height.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < height.size(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tlong long area = (j - i) * std::min(height[i], height[j]);\n\t\t\tif (area > max_val) max_val = area;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_val;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nlong long maxArea(std::vector<int>& height) {\n\tlong long max_val = 0;\n\tint left = 0, right = (int)height.size() - 1;\n\twhile (left < right) {\n\t\tlong long current_area = (long long)(right - left) * std::min(height[left], height[right]);\n\t\tmax_val = std::max(max_val, current_area);\n\t\tif (height[left] < height[right]) left++;\n\t\telse right--;\n\t}\n\treturn max_val;\n}\n---\nThe brute-force  approach checks every pair of lines. A two-pointer sliding window approach achieves  by greedily moving the pointer pointing to the shorter line, effectively narrowing down the search space."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nint smallestSubarrayWithSum(std::vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint min_len = 1e9;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tint s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\ts += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (s >= target) {\n\t\t\t\tif (j - i + 1 < min_len) min_len = j - i + 1;\n\t\t\t\tbreak;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (min_len == 1e9) ? 0 : min_len;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint smallestSubarrayWithSum(std::vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), left = 0, current_sum = 0, min_len = 1e9;\n\tfor (int right = 0; right < n; ++right) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[right];\n\t\twhile (current_sum >= target) {\n\t\t\tmin_len = std::min(min_len, right - left + 1);\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum -= nums[left++];\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (min_len == 1e9) ? 0 : min_len;\n}\n---\nThe original  code re-sums elements unnecessarily. A variable sliding window maintains a running sum and contracts from the left, achieving  time complexity and minimal constant overhead."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <numeric>\n\ndouble findMaxAverage(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tdouble max_avg = -1e18;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tlong long s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; ++j) s += nums[j];\n\t\tdouble avg = (double)s / k;\n\t\tif (avg > max_avg) max_avg = avg;\n\t}\n\treturn max_avg;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\ndouble findMaxAverage(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tlong long current_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) current_sum += nums[i];\n\tlong long max_sum = current_sum;\n\tfor (int i = k; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += (nums[i] - nums[i - k]);\n\t\tmax_sum = std::max(max_sum, current_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn (double)max_sum / k;\n}\n---\nThe original code has  complexity. By using a fixed sliding window to maintain a running sum, we avoid repeated additions and reach  time, performing the division only once at the end."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n\nstd::vector<int> findAnagramStarts(std::string s, std::string p) {\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tif (s.length() < p.length()) return res;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= (int)s.length() - (int)p.length(); ++i) {\n\t\tstd::vector<int> count1(26, 0), count2(26, 0);\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < (int)p.length(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tcount1[s[i + j] - 'a']++;\n\t\t\tcount2[p[j] - 'a']++;\n\t\t}\n\t\tif (count1 == count2) res.push_back(i);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n\nstd::vector<int> findAnagramStarts(std::string s, std::string p) {\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tint n = s.length(), m = p.length();\n\tif (n < m) return res;\n\tstd::vector<int> p_count(26, 0), s_count(26, 0);\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < m; ++i) {\n\t\tp_count[p[i] - 'a']++;\n\t\ts_count[s[i] - 'a']++;\n\t}\n\tif (p_count == s_count) res.push_back(0);\n\tfor (int i = m; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\ts_count[s[i] - 'a']++;\n\t\ts_count[s[i - m] - 'a']--;\n\t\tif (p_count == s_count) res.push_back(i - m + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe original  logic re-counts character frequencies for every window. The sliding window approach maintains the counts in  per shift, resulting in an efficient  time complexity."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint maxSubarraySum(std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tint max_sum = -2e9;\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i; j < nums.size(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tint s = 0;\n\t\t\tfor (size_t k = i; k <= j; ++k) s += nums[k];\n\t\t\tmax_sum = std::max(max_sum, s);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_sum;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint maxSubarraySum(std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tint max_so_far = nums[0], current_max = nums[0];\n\tfor (size_t i = 1; i < nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tcurrent_max = std::max(nums[i], current_max + nums[i]);\n\t\tmax_so_far = std::max(max_so_far, current_max);\n\t}\n\treturn max_so_far;\n}\n---\nThe original cubic  approach is extremely slow. Kadane's Algorithm uses a form of dynamic programming (a single-pass sliding window) to solve the problem in  time with  space."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <set>\n\nint longestSubarrayWithDiffLimit(std::vector<int>& nums, int limit) {\n\tint max_len = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < (int)nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < (int)nums.size(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tint min_v = nums[i], max_v = nums[i];\n\t\t\tfor (int k = i; k <= j; ++k) {\n\t\t\t\tif (nums[k] < min_v) min_v = nums[k];\n\t\t\t\tif (nums[k] > max_v) max_v = nums[k];\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif (max_v - min_v <= limit) max_len = std::max(max_len, j - i + 1);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_len;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <deque>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longestSubarrayWithDiffLimit(std::vector<int>& nums, int limit) {\n\tstd::deque<int> max_dq, min_dq;\n\tint left = 0, max_len = 0;\n\tfor (int right = 0; right < (int)nums.size(); ++right) {\n\t\twhile (!max_dq.empty() && nums[max_dq.back()] <= nums[right]) max_dq.pop_back();\n\t\twhile (!min_dq.empty() && nums[min_dq.back()] >= nums[right]) min_dq.pop_back();\n\t\tmax_dq.push_back(right); min_dq.push_back(right);\n\t\twhile (nums[max_dq.front()] - nums[min_dq.front()] > limit) {\n\t\t\tif (max_dq.front() == left) max_dq.pop_front();\n\t\t\tif (min_dq.front() == left) min_dq.pop_front();\n\t\t\tleft++;\n\t\t}\n\t\tmax_len = std::max(max_len, right - left + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn max_len;\n}\n---\nThe  unoptimized code repeatedly finds min/max in subarray ranges. Dual monotonic deques maintain the window's min and max in  total time, allowing the window to expand and contract efficiently."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nint countSubarraysProductLessK(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tif (k <= 1) return 0;\n\tint count = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < (int)nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tlong long prod = 1;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < (int)nums.size(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tprod *= nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (prod < k) count++;\n\t\t\telse break;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nint countSubarraysProductLessK(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tif (k <= 1) return 0;\n\tint count = 0, left = 0;\n\tlong long prod = 1;\n\tfor (int right = 0; right < (int)nums.size(); ++right) {\n\t\tprod *= nums[right];\n\t\twhile (prod >= k && left <= right) {\n\t\t\tprod /= nums[left++];\n\t\t}\n\t\tcount += (right - left + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nThe original  logic checks all subarray starts. A sliding window reduces this to  by multiplying the right side and dividing the left side as needed, counting all valid subarrays ending at the current position."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <climits>\n\nint minAbsDifferenceSizeK(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint min_diff = INT_MAX;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint window_min = INT_MAX, window_max = INT_MIN;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (nums[j] < window_min) window_min = nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (nums[j] > window_max) window_max = nums[j];\n\t\t}\n\t\tif (window_max - window_min < min_diff) min_diff = window_max - window_min;\n\t}\n\treturn min_diff;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <deque>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint minAbsDifferenceSizeK(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tstd::deque<int> max_dq, min_dq;\n\tint min_diff = 2e9;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tif (!max_dq.empty() && max_dq.front() == i - k) max_dq.pop_front();\n\t\twhile (!max_dq.empty() && nums[max_dq.back()] <= nums[i]) max_dq.pop_back();\n\t\tmax_dq.push_back(i);\n\t\tif (!min_dq.empty() && min_dq.front() == i - k) min_dq.pop_front();\n\t\twhile (!min_dq.empty() && nums[min_dq.back()] >= nums[i]) min_dq.pop_back();\n\t\tmin_dq.push_back(i);\n\t\tif (i >= k - 1) min_diff = std::min(min_diff, nums[max_dq.front()] - nums[min_dq.front()]);\n\t}\n\treturn min_diff;\n}\n---\nThe original code uses  to find range differences. By maintaining two monotonic deques for the window's min and max, the algorithm achieves  time complexity for fixed-size window stats."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <unordered_map>\n\nint atMostKDistinct(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint max_len = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < (int)nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < (int)nums.size(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tstd::unordered_map<int, int> counts;\n\t\t\tfor (int l = i; l <= j; ++l) counts[nums[l]]++;\n\t\t\tif ((int)counts.size() <= k) max_len = (j - i + 1 > max_len) ? j - i + 1 : max_len;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_len;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <unordered_map>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint atMostKDistinct(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint left = 0, max_len = 0;\n\tstd::unordered_map<int, int> counts;\n\tfor (int right = 0; right < (int)nums.size(); ++right) {\n\t\tcounts[nums[right]]++;\n\t\twhile ((int)counts.size() > k) {\n\t\t\tif (--counts[nums[left]] == 0) counts.erase(nums[left]);\n\t\t\tleft++;\n\t\t}\n\t\tmax_len = std::max(max_len, right - left + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn max_len;\n}\n---\nThe  unoptimized code repeatedly builds hash maps for every subarray. A sliding window with a persistent frequency map allows expanding and shrinking the window in  amortized time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <numeric>\n\nstd::vector<int> runningSum(std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tint s = 0;\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j <= i; ++j) s += nums[j];\n\t\tres.push_back(s);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nstd::vector<int> runningSum(std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tif (nums.empty()) return {};\n\tstd::vector<int> res(nums.size());\n\tres[0] = nums[0];\n\tfor (size_t i = 1; i < nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tres[i] = res[i - 1] + nums[i];\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe original code has  complexity due to re-calculating the sum from the beginning for every element. Using the property that  reduces the complexity to ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint maxConsecutiveOnesWithFlip(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint max_len = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < (int)nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < (int)nums.size(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tint zeros = 0;\n\t\t\tfor (int l = i; l <= j; ++l) if (nums[l] == 0) zeros++;\n\t\t\tif (zeros <= k) max_len = std::max(max_len, j - i + 1);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_len;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint maxConsecutiveOnesWithFlip(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint left = 0, zeros = 0, max_len = 0;\n\tfor (int right = 0; right < (int)nums.size(); ++right) {\n\t\tif (nums[right] == 0) zeros++;\n\t\twhile (zeros > k) {\n\t\t\tif (nums[left++] == 0) zeros--;\n\t\t}\n\t\tmax_len = std::max(max_len, right - left + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn max_len;\n}\n---\nThe unoptimized  algorithm scans zeros for every range. A sliding window reduces this to  by keeping track of zero counts as the right edge advances and shrinking from the left when the budget  is exceeded."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <set>\n\nbool containsDuplicateInRange(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < (int)nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j <= i + k && j < (int)nums.size(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (nums[i] == nums[j]) return true;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <unordered_set>\n\nbool containsDuplicateInRange(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tstd::unordered_set<int> window;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < (int)nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tif (window.count(nums[i])) return true;\n\t\twindow.insert(nums[i]);\n\t\tif ((int)window.size() > k) window.erase(nums[i - k]);\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}\n---\nThe original code uses  comparisons. A sliding window with an `unordered_set` tracks the last  elements in  average time, making lookups and updates significantly faster."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nlong long sumOfSubarraysLengthK(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tlong long total = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= (int)nums.size() - k; ++i) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; ++j) {\n\t\t\ttotal += nums[j];\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn total;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nlong long sumOfSubarraysLengthK(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tlong long current_window_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) current_window_sum += nums[i];\n\tlong long total = current_window_sum;\n\tfor (int i = k; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tcurrent_window_sum += (nums[i] - nums[i - k]);\n\t\ttotal += current_window_sum;\n\t}\n\treturn total;\n}\n---\nThe  approach recalculates the sum for every window. By using the sliding window sum property, we compute the total in  time by adding/subtracting the window edges."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <numeric>\n\nint findPivotIndex(std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < (int)nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tint left_sum = 0, right_sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < i; ++j) left_sum += nums[j];\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < (int)nums.size(); ++j) right_sum += nums[j];\n\t\tif (left_sum == right_sum) return i;\n\t}\n\treturn -1;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <numeric>\n\nint findPivotIndex(std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tlong long total_sum = std::accumulate(nums.begin(), nums.end(), 0LL);\n\tlong long left_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < (int)nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tif (left_sum == total_sum - left_sum - nums[i]) return i;\n\t\tleft_sum += nums[i];\n\t}\n\treturn -1;\n}\n---\nThe original  solution calculates sums repeatedly for every index. By pre-calculating the total sum and maintaining a running left sum, we find the pivot in  time with  extra space."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint maxFrequency(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tstd::sort(nums.begin(), nums.end());\n\tint max_f = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < (int)nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j <= i; ++j) {\n\t\t\tlong long needed = 0;\n\t\t\tfor (int l = j; l < i; ++l) needed += (nums[i] - nums[l]);\n\t\t\tif (needed <= k) max_f = std::max(max_f, i - j + 1);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_f;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint maxFrequency(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tstd::sort(nums.begin(), nums.end());\n\tlong long total_sum = 0;\n\tint left = 0, max_f = 0;\n\tfor (int right = 0; right < (int)nums.size(); ++right) {\n\t\ttotal_sum += nums[right];\n\t\twhile ((long long)nums[right] * (right - left + 1) - total_sum > k) {\n\t\t\ttotal_sum -= nums[left++];\n\t\t}\n\t\tmax_f = std::max(max_f, right - left + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn max_f;\n}\n---\nThe sorting + nested loop approach is  or higher. The sliding window approach (after sorting) tracks the cost to make all elements in the window equal to the current max element in  time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <unordered_map>\n\nint countSubarraysWithKDistinct(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint count = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < (int)nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < (int)nums.size(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tstd::unordered_map<int, int> m;\n\t\t\tfor (int l = i; l <= j; ++l) m[nums[l]]++;\n\t\t\tif ((int)m.size() == k) count++;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <unordered_map>\n\nint atMost(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint left = 0, count = 0;\n\tstd::unordered_map<int, int> m;\n\tfor (int right = 0; right < (int)nums.size(); ++right) {\n\t\tif (m[nums[right]]++ == 0) k--;\n\t\twhile (k < 0) {\n\t\t\tif (--m[nums[left]] == 0) k++;\n\t\t\tleft++;\n\t\t}\n\t\tcount += (right - left + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n\nint countSubarraysWithKDistinct(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\treturn atMost(nums, k) - atMost(nums, k - 1);\n}\n---\nThe original code is . We optimize this by using the property that `exactly(k) = atMost(k) - atMost(k-1)`. This allows using a  sliding window to solve the problem in linear time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint minSubarraySumLength(std::vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint min_len = n + 1;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tint current_sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (current_sum >= target) {\n\t\t\t\tmin_len = std::min(min_len, j - i + 1);\n\t\t\t\tbreak;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (min_len > n) ? 0 : min_len;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint minSubarraySumLength(std::vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint left = 0, current_sum = 0, min_len = n + 1;\n\tfor (int right = 0; right < n; ++right) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[right];\n\t\twhile (current_sum >= target) {\n\t\t\tmin_len = std::min(min_len, right - left + 1);\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum -= nums[left++];\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (min_len > n) ? 0 : min_len;\n}\n---\nThe unoptimized code performs  additions by checking every possible starting index. A sliding window reduces this to  by maintaining a rolling sum and contracting the left pointer whenever the target is reached."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::vector<int> windowMaximums(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= (int)nums.size() - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint m = nums[i];\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < i + k; ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (nums[j] > m) m = nums[j];\n\t\t}\n\t\tres.push_back(m);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <deque>\n\nstd::vector<int> windowMaximums(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tstd::deque<int> dq;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tif (!dq.empty() && dq.front() == i - k) dq.pop_front();\n\t\twhile (!dq.empty() && nums[dq.back()] <= nums[i]) dq.pop_back();\n\t\tdq.push_back(i);\n\t\tif (i >= k - 1) res.push_back(nums[dq.front()]);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach repeatedly searches the window for a maximum. Using a monotonic deque allows us to maintain max candidates in  amortized time per element, providing a linear  solution."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <unordered_set>\n\nint longestUniqueSubstring(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length(), max_len = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tstd::unordered_set<char> seen;\n\t\t\tbool valid = true;\n\t\t\tfor (int k = i; k <= j; ++k) {\n\t\t\t\tif (seen.count(s[k])) { valid = false; break; }\n\t\t\t\tseen.insert(s[k]);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif (valid) max_len = (j - i + 1 > max_len) ? j - i + 1 : max_len;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn max_len;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longestUniqueSubstring(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length();\n\tstd::vector<int> pos(256, -1);\n\tint max_len = 0, left = 0;\n\tfor (int right = 0; right < n; ++right) {\n\t\tif (pos[(unsigned char)s[right]] >= left) {\n\t\t\tleft = pos[(unsigned char)s[right]] + 1;\n\t\t}\n\t\tpos[(unsigned char)s[right]] = right;\n\t\tmax_len = std::max(max_len, right - left + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn max_len;\n}\n---\nThe unoptimized code takes  with heavy set allocations. Using a sliding window with an index-tracking array reduces the complexity to  and eliminates dynamic memory overhead."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nint countSubarraysSumAtMost(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint count = 0, n = nums.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tlong long sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tsum += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (sum <= k) count++;\n\t\t\telse break;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nint countSubarraysSumAtMost(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint left = 0, count = 0;\n\tlong long current_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int right = 0; right < n; ++right) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[right];\n\t\twhile (current_sum > k && left <= right) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum -= nums[left++];\n\t\t}\n\t\tcount += (right - left + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach checks every subarray explicitly. A variable-sized sliding window maintains a running sum and adds the window size to the count at each step, yielding  complexity."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <climits>\n\nint findMaxKSum(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint max_s = INT_MIN;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; ++j) s += nums[j];\n\t\tif (s > max_s) max_s = s;\n\t}\n\treturn max_s;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint findMaxKSum(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tint current_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) current_sum += nums[i];\n\tint max_s = current_sum;\n\tfor (int i = k; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += (nums[i] - nums[i - k]);\n\t\tmax_s = std::max(max_s, current_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn max_s;\n}\n---\nThe naive approach recalculates the sum of each -size window from scratch, leading to  complexity. A fixed sliding window updates the sum in  per shift, resulting in a total time complexity of ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint minSubarraySumTarget(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint min_len = n + 1;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tint sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\tsum += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (sum >= k) {\n\t\t\t\tmin_len = std::min(min_len, j - i + 1);\n\t\t\t\tbreak;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (min_len > n) ? 0 : min_len;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint minSubarraySumTarget(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tint left = 0, current_sum = 0, min_len = n + 1;\n\tfor (int right = 0; right < n; ++right) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[right];\n\t\twhile (current_sum >= k) {\n\t\t\tmin_len = std::min(min_len, right - left + 1);\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum -= nums[left++];\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (min_len > n) ? 0 : min_len;\n}\n---\nThe unoptimized  approach checks all starting points for subarrays. By using a sliding window that shrinks as soon as the condition is met, we find the minimum length in  time complexity."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nlong long countSubarraysWithSumLess(std::vector<int>& v, int t) {\n\tlong long count = 0;\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < v.size(); i++) {\n\t\tlong long sum = 0;\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i; j < v.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tsum += v[j];\n\t\t\tif (sum < t) count++;\n\t\t\telse break;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nlong long countSubarraysWithSumLess(std::vector<int>& v, int t) {\n\tint left = 0;\n\tlong long count = 0, current_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int right = 0; right < (int)v.size(); right++) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += v[right];\n\t\twhile (current_sum >= t && left <= right) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum -= v[left++];\n\t\t}\n\t\tcount += (right - left + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nThe nested loop approach is . The sliding window approach maintains the sum and calculates the number of valid subarrays ending at the current pointer in  time, providing a significant speedup."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::vector<int> windowMins(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tstd::vector<int> results;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= (int)nums.size() - k; ++i) {\n\t\tint current_min = nums[i];\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < i + k; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (nums[j] < current_min) current_min = nums[j];\n\t\t}\n\t\tresults.push_back(current_min);\n\t}\n\treturn results;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <deque>\n\nstd::vector<int> windowMins(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tstd::vector<int> results;\n\tstd::deque<int> dq;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tif (!dq.empty() && dq.front() == i - k) dq.pop_front();\n\t\twhile (!dq.empty() && nums[dq.back()] >= nums[i]) dq.pop_back();\n\t\tdq.push_back(i);\n\t\tif (i >= k - 1) results.push_back(nums[dq.front()]);\n\t}\n\treturn results;\n}\n---\nThe original  method repeatedly scans for a minimum. The optimized monotonic deque approach keeps only potential minimum candidates in  time, where each index is inserted and deleted once."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <numeric>\n\nint maxSubSumSizeK(std::vector<int>& v, int k) {\n\tint n = v.size(), res = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; i++) {\n\t\tint temp = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; j++) temp += v[j];\n\t\tif (i == 0 || temp > res) res = temp;\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint maxSubSumSizeK(std::vector<int>& v, int k) {\n\tint n = v.size();\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tint current_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; i++) current_sum += v[i];\n\tint res = current_sum;\n\tfor (int i = k; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += (v[i] - v[i - k]);\n\t\tres = std::max(res, current_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe original solution is . By using a sliding window to add the incoming element and subtract the outgoing one, the sum is updated in  time, reducing the total complexity to ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <unordered_set>\n\nint lengthOfLongestSubstring(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length(), res = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tstd::unordered_set<char> seen;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (seen.count(s[j])) break;\n\t\t\tseen.insert(s[j]);\n\t\t\tres = (j - i + 1 > res) ? j - i + 1 : res;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint lengthOfLongestSubstring(std::string s) {\n\tint n = s.length(), left = 0, res = 0;\n\tstd::vector<int> char_map(256, -1);\n\tfor (int right = 0; right < n; right++) {\n\t\tleft = std::max(left, char_map[(unsigned char)s[right]] + 1);\n\t\tres = std::max(res, right - left + 1);\n\t\tchar_map[(unsigned char)s[right]] = right;\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe naive  algorithm re-scans the string from every character. A sliding window with an index-tracking array optimizes character lookups to  and total time to ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nint subarraysWithSumK(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), count = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tint s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\ts += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (s <= k) count++;\n\t\t\telse break;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nint subarraysWithSumK(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), left = 0, count = 0;\n\tlong long current_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int right = 0; right < n; right++) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[right];\n\t\twhile (current_sum > k && left <= right) {\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum -= nums[left++];\n\t\t}\n\t\tcount += (right - left + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn count;\n}\n---\nThe original code has  complexity by looking at every possible range start. A sliding window reduces this to  by adjusting the left boundary and counting the valid subarrays ending at the right pointer."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::vector<int> maxInEveryWindow(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; i++) {\n\t\tint m = nums[i];\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < i + k; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (nums[j] > m) m = nums[j];\n\t\t}\n\t\tres.push_back(m);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <deque>\n\nstd::vector<int> maxInEveryWindow(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tstd::deque<int> dq;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tif (!dq.empty() && dq.front() == i - k) dq.pop_front();\n\t\twhile (!dq.empty() && nums[dq.back()] <= nums[i]) dq.pop_back();\n\t\tdq.push_back(i);\n\t\tif (i >= k - 1) res.push_back(nums[dq.front()]);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe unoptimized code performs  comparisons. A monotonic deque keeps track of elements that can potentially be the maximum in current and future windows, reducing the complexity to ."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint shortestSubSum(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), min_l = n + 1;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tint s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\ts += nums[j];\n\t\t\tif (s >= k) {\n\t\t\t\tmin_l = std::min(min_l, j - i + 1);\n\t\t\t\tbreak;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (min_l > n) ? 0 : min_l;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint shortestSubSum(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), left = 0, current_sum = 0, min_l = n + 1;\n\tfor (int right = 0; right < n; right++) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += nums[right];\n\t\twhile (current_sum >= k) {\n\t\t\tmin_l = std::min(min_l, right - left + 1);\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum -= nums[left++];\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (min_l > n) ? 0 : min_l;\n}\n---\nThe original  approach is slow for large datasets. A sliding window minimizes the window size in  total time by incrementally adding from the right and removing from the left when the condition is met."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <climits>\n\nlong long minSumWindowK(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tlong long min_s = LLONG_MAX;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; i++) {\n\t\tlong long s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; j++) s += nums[j];\n\t\tif (s < min_s) min_s = s;\n\t}\n\treturn min_s;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nlong long minSumWindowK(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tlong long current_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; i++) current_sum += nums[i];\n\tlong long min_s = current_sum;\n\tfor (int i = k; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += (nums[i] - nums[i - k]);\n\t\tmin_s = std::min(min_s, current_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn min_s;\n}\n---\nThe unoptimized code performs  additions. The fixed sliding window technique maintains the window sum in  time by adding the next element and removing the first element of the previous window."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <unordered_map>\n\nint longestKDistinct(std::string s, int k) {\n\tint n = s.length(), res = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tstd::unordered_map<char, int> m;\n\t\t\tfor (int l = i; l <= j; l++) m[s[l]]++;\n\t\t\tif ((int)m.size() <= k) res = (j - i + 1 > res) ? j - i + 1 : res;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint longestKDistinct(std::string s, int k) {\n\tint n = s.length(), left = 0, res = 0, distinct = 0;\n\tstd::vector<int> counts(256, 0);\n\tfor (int right = 0; right < n; right++) {\n\t\tif (counts[(unsigned char)s[right]]++ == 0) distinct++;\n\t\twhile (distinct > k) {\n\t\t\tif (--counts[(unsigned char)s[left++]] == 0) distinct--;\n\t\t}\n\t\tres = std::max(res, right - left + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe original  solution creates maps for every substring. A sliding window with a frequency array tracks distinct characters in  time, providing a major speedup and using  auxiliary space."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nint countSmallSums(std::vector<int>& arr, int target) {\n\tint total = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < (int)arr.size(); i++) {\n\t\tint current = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < (int)arr.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tcurrent += arr[j];\n\t\t\tif (current <= target) total++;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn total;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nint countSmallSums(std::vector<int>& arr, int target) {\n\tint left = 0, total = 0, current = 0;\n\tfor (int right = 0; right < (int)arr.size(); right++) {\n\t\tcurrent += arr[right];\n\t\twhile (current > target && left <= right) {\n\t\t\tcurrent -= arr[left++];\n\t\t}\n\t\ttotal += (right - left + 1);\n\t}\n\treturn total;\n}\n---\nThe original code has  complexity. The sliding window approach maintains a running sum to identify valid subarrays in  time, using the count of subarrays ending at the right pointer."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::vector<int> minSlidingWindow(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tif (nums.empty() || k == 0) return res;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= (int)nums.size() - k; i++) {\n\t\tint cur_min = nums[i];\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < i + k; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (nums[j] < cur_min) cur_min = nums[j];\n\t\t}\n\t\tres.push_back(cur_min);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <deque>\n\nstd::vector<int> minSlidingWindow(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tstd::vector<int> res;\n\tstd::deque<int> dq;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tif (!dq.empty() && dq.front() == i - k) dq.pop_front();\n\t\twhile (!dq.empty() && nums[dq.back()] >= nums[i]) dq.pop_back();\n\t\tdq.push_back(i);\n\t\tif (i >= k - 1) res.push_back(nums[dq.front()]);\n\t}\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nThe unoptimized  approach repeatedly searches each window. A monotonic deque maintains the indices of minimum candidates, allowing for  retrieval of the window minimum and  total time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint smallestSubSum(std::vector<int>& arr, int target) {\n\tint n = arr.size(), min_len = n + 1;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tint s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\ts += arr[j];\n\t\t\tif (s >= target) {\n\t\t\t\tmin_len = std::min(min_len, j - i + 1);\n\t\t\t\tbreak;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (min_len > n) ? 0 : min_len;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint smallestSubSum(std::vector<int>& arr, int target) {\n\tint n = arr.size(), left = 0, current_sum = 0, min_len = n + 1;\n\tfor (int right = 0; right < n; right++) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += arr[right];\n\t\twhile (current_sum >= target) {\n\t\t\tmin_len = std::min(min_len, right - left + 1);\n\t\t\tcurrent_sum -= arr[left++];\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn (min_len > n) ? 0 : min_len;\n}\n---\nThe original  logic checks all subarray sums from every start index. A variable sliding window approach finds the shortest window meeting the target in  time by moving pointers in a single pass."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <numeric>\n\nint maxSumOfSizeK(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size(), max_val = 0;\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i <= n - k; i++) {\n\t\tint s = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = i; j < i + k; j++) s += nums[j];\n\t\tif (i == 0 || s > max_val) max_val = s;\n\t}\n\treturn max_val;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint maxSumOfSizeK(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tif (n < k) return 0;\n\tint current_sum = 0;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; i++) current_sum += nums[i];\n\tint max_val = current_sum;\n\tfor (int i = k; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tcurrent_sum += (nums[i] - nums[i - k]);\n\t\tmax_val = std::max(max_val, current_sum);\n\t}\n\treturn max_val;\n}\n---\nThe unoptimized code performs  work due to redundant summation within the inner loop. The sliding window technique maintains the current sum in  per step, leading to a total complexity of ."}
